/em> Uses Bytecode\",\"matchLevel\":\"full\",\"fullyHighlighted\":false,\"matchedWords\":[\"sqlite\"]},\"url\":{\"value\":\"https://<em>sqlite</em>.org/draft/whybytecode.html\",\"matchLevel\":\"full\",\"fullyHighlighted\":false,\"matchedWords\":[\"sqlite\"]},\"author\":{\"value\":\"todsacerdoti\",\"matchLevel\":\"none\",\"matchedWords\":[]}},\"_rankingInfo\":{\"nbTypos\":0,\"firstMatchedWord\":0,\"proximityDistance\":0,\"userScore\":86,\"geoDistance\":0,\"geoPrecision\":1,\"nbExactWords\":0,\"words\":1,\"filters\":1}},{\"title\":\"SQLite 3 Fiddle\",\"url\":\"https://sqlite.org/fiddle/\",\"author\":\"sgbeal\",\"points\":765,\"num_comments\":136,\"story_id\":31518618,\"created_at_i\":1653574719,\"created_at\":\"2022-05-26T14:18:39Z\",\"updated_at\":\"2025-11-05T11:17:16Z\",\"_tags\":[\"story\",\"author_sgbeal\",\"story_31518618\"],\"children\":[31518619,31518830,31518837,31518840,31518979,31519011,31519046,31519065,31519097,31519153,31519637,31519937,31520414,31520444,31520489,31520495,31520738,31521069,31521129,31521356,31522135,31523697,31524142,31524299,31528699,31529690,31533735],\"objectID\":\"31518618\",\"_highlightResult\":{\"title\":{\"value\":\"<em>SQLite</em> 3 Fiddle\",\"matchLevel\":\"full\",\"fullyHighlighted\":false,\"matchedWords\":[\"sqlite\"]},\"url\":{\"value\":\"https://<em>sqlite</em>.org/fiddle/\",\"matchLevel\":\"full\",\"fullyHighlighted\":false,\"matchedWords\":[\"sqlite\"]},\"author\":{\"value\":\"sgbeal\",\"matchLevel\":\"none\",\"matchedWords\":[]}},\"_rankingInfo\":{\"nbTypos\":0,\"firstMatchedWord\":0,\"proximityDistance\":0,\"userScore\":85,\"geoDistance\":0,\"geoPrecision\":1,\"nbExactWords\":0,\"words\":1,\"filters\":1}},{\"title\":\"One-liner for running queries against CSV files with SQLite\",\"url\":\"https://til.simonwillison.net/sqlite/one-line-csv-operations\",\"author\":\"jdblair\",\"points\":747,\"num_comments\":127,\"story_id\":31824030,\"created_at_i\":1655819976,\"created_at\":\"2022-06-21T13:59:36Z\",\"updated_at\":\"2025-05-31T10:59:03Z\",\"_tags\":[\"story\",\"author_jdblair\",\"story_31824030\"],\"children\":[31824192,31824197,31824265,31824385,31824506,31824590,31824609,31824667,31824731,31825071,31825093,31825195,31825199,31825215,31825261,31825337,31825374,31825464,31825558,31825577,31825632,31825905,31826052,31826397,31826642,31827293,31827714,31827820,31828094,31832424,31835669],\"objectID\":\"31824030\",\"_highlightResult\":{\"title\":{\"value\":\"One-liner for running queries against CSV files with <em>SQLite</em>\",\"matchLevel\":\"full\",\"fullyHighlighted\":false,\"matchedWords\":[\"sqlite\"]},\"url\":{\"value\":\"https://til.simonwillison.net/<em>sqlite</em>/one-line-csv-operations\",\"matchLevel\":\"full\",\"fullyHighlighted\":false,\"matchedWords\":[\"sqlite\"]},\"author\":{\"value\":\"jdblair\",\"matchLevel\":\"none\",\"matchedWords\":[]}},\"_rankingInfo\":{\"nbTypos\":0,\"firstMatchedWord\":0,\"proximityDistance\":0,\"userScore\":84,\"geoDistance\":0,\"geoPrecision\":1,\"nbExactWords\":0,\"words\":1,\"filters\":1}},{\"title\":\"Show HN: 22 GB of Hacker News in SQLite\",\"url\":\"https://hackerbook.dosaygo.com\",\"author\":\"keepamovin\",\"points\":730,\"story_text\":\"Community, All the HN belong to you. This is an archive of hacker news that fits in your browser. When I made HN Made of Primes I realized I could probably do this offline sqlite&#x2F;wasm thing with the whole GBs of archive. The whole dataset. So I tried it, and this is it. Have Hacker News on your device.<p>Go to this repo (<a href=\\\"https:&#x2F;&#x2F;github.com&#x2F;DOSAYGO-STUDIO&#x2F;HackerBook\\\" rel=\\\"nofollow\\\">https:&#x2F;&#x2F;github.com&#x2F;DOSAYGO-STUDIO&#x2F;HackerBook</a>): you can download it. Big Query -&gt; ETL -&gt; npx serve docs - that&#x27;s it. 20 years of HN arguments and beauty, can be yours forever. So they&#x27;ll never die. Ever. It&#x27;s the unkillable static archive of HN and it&#x27;s your hands. That&#x27;s my Year End gift to you all. Thank you for a wonderful year, have happy and wonderful 2026. make something of it.\",\"num_comments\":221,\"story_id\":46435308,\"created_at_i\":1767114119,\"created_at\":\"2025-12-30T17:01:59Z\",\"updated_at\":\"2026-10-19T16:02:00Z\",\"_tags\":[\"story\",\"author_keepamovin\",\"story_46435308\",\"show_hn\"],\"children\":[46435316,46435771,46436422,46436488,46436543,46436571,46436803,46436853,46436910,46436937,46436985,46437009,46437026,46437110,46438109,46438133,46438345,46438509,46438637,46438681,46438884,46438955,46439002,46439263,46439282,46439356,46439422,46440119,46440374,46440398,46440946,46442123,46442280,46442902,46443012,46443346,46443496,46443965,46446367,46487080],\"objectID\":\"46435308\",\"_highlightResult\":{\"title\":{\"value\":\"Show HN: 22 GB of Hacker News in <em>SQLite</em>\",\"matchLevel\":\"full\",\"fullyHighlighted\":false,\"matchedWords\":[\"sqlite\"]},\"url\":{\"value\":\"https://hackerbook.dosaygo.com\",\"matchLevel\":\"none\",\"matchedWords\":[]},\"author\":{\"value\":\"keepamovin\",\"matchLevel\":\"none\",\"matchedWords\":[]},\"story_text\":{\"value\":\"Community, All the HN belong to you. This is an archive of hacker news that fits in your browser. When I made HN Made of Primes I realized I could probably do this offline <em>sqlite</em>/wasm thing with the whole GBs of archive. The whole dataset. So I tried it, and this is it. Have Hacker News on your device.<p>Go to this repo (<a href=\\\"https://github.com/DOSAYGO-STUDIO/HackerBook\\\" rel=\\\"nofollow\\\">https://github.com/DOSAYGO-STUDIO/HackerBook</a>): you can download it. Big Query -&gt; ETL -&gt; npx serve docs - that's it. 20 years of HN arguments and beauty, can be yours forever. So they'll never die. Ever. It's the unkillable static archive of HN and it's your hands. That's my Year End gift to you all. Thank you for a wonderful year, have happy and wonderful 2026. make something of it.\",\"matchLevel\":\"full\",\"fullyHighlighted\":false,\"matchedWords\":[\"sqlite\"]}},\"_rankingInfo\":{\"nbTypos\":0,\"firstMatchedWord\":0,\"proximityDistance\":0,\"userScore\":83,\"geoDistance\":0,\"geoPrecision\":1,\"nbExactWords\":0,\"words\":1,\"filters\":1}},{\"title\":\"SQLite Critical CVEs or LLM Slop?\",\"url\":\"https://research.jfrog.com/post/sqlite-critical-cves-or-llm-slops/\",\"author\":\"ymir_e\",\"points\":727,\"num_comments\":372,\"story_id\":49154332,\"created_at_i\":1785756534,\"created_at\":\"2026-08-03T11:28:54Z\",\"updated_at\":\"2026-09-30T00:12:55Z\",\"_tags\":[\"story\",\"author_ymir_e\",\"story_49154332\"],\"children\":[49154469,49154473,49154534,49154545,49154555,49154579,49154630,49154645,49154649,49154776,49154781,49154802,49154843,49154877,49154994,49155014,49155075,49155089,49155118,49155138,49155219,49155246,49155459,49155647,49155952,49155979,49156036,49156263,49156490,49156828,49157062,49157186,49157320,49157361,49157362,49157522,49157580,49157777,49157938,49157988,49158005,49158103,49158151,49158357,49158422,49158693,49158866,49159304,49160235,49161245,49164697,49172599],\"objectID\":\"49154332\",\"_highlightResult\":{\"title\":{\"value\":\"<em>SQLite</em> Critical CVEs or LLM Slop?\",\"matchLevel\":\"full\",\"fullyHighlighted\":false,\"matchedWords\":[\"sqlite\"]},\"url\":{\"value\":\"https://research.jfrog.com/post/<em>sqlite</em>-critical-cves-or-llm-slops/\",\"matchLevel\":\"full\",\"fullyHighlighted\":false,\"matchedWords\":[\"sqlite\"]},\"author\":{\"value\":\"ymir_e\",\"matchLevel\":\"none\",\"matchedWords\":[]}},\"_rankingInfo\":{\"nbTypos\":0,\"firstMatchedWord\":0,\"proximityDistance\":0,\"userScore\":82,\"geoDistance\":0,\"geoPrecision\":1,\"nbExactWords\":0,\"words\":1,\"filters\":1}},{\"title\":\"SQLite is all you need for durable workflows\",\"url\":\"https://obeli.sk/blog/sqlite-is-all-you-need-for-durable-workflows/\",\"author\":\"tomasol\",\"points\":720,\"num_comments\":387,\"story_id\":48326802,\"created_at_i\":1780077290,\"created_at\":\"2026-05-29T17:54:50Z\",\"updated_at\":\"2026-09-08T20:17:48Z\",\"_tags\":[\"story\",\"author_tomasol\",\"story_48326802\"],\"children\":[48326924,48326943,48326956,48327076,48327121,48327260,48327584,48327679,48327763,48327797,48327849,48327859,48327872,48327995,48328009,48328096,48328124,48328239,48328415,48328583,48328601,48328728,48328974,48329144,48329406,48329476,48329530,48329550,48329679,48329730,48329844,48329897,48330119,48330279,48330282,48330336,48330627,48330878,48331115,48331209,48331397,48331472,48331553,48331627,48332011,48333126,48333371,48333391,48333446,48333482,48333608,48333778,48333796,48334001,48334034,48334095,48334399,48334478,48334934,48335324,48335582,48335749,48335933,48337285,48337479,48337621,48337888,48338416,48340164,48340734,48342058,48344778,48345005,48348963],\"objectID\":\"48326802\",\"_highlightResult\":{\"title\":{\"value\":\"<em>SQLite</em> is all you need for durable workflows\",\"matchLevel\":\"full\",\"fullyHighlighted\":false,\"matchedWords\":[\"sqlite\"]},\"url\":{\"value\":\"https://obeli.sk/blog/<em>sqlite</em>-is-all-you-need-for-durable-workflows/\",\"matchLevel\":\"full\",\"fullyHighlighted\":false,\"matchedWords\":[\"sqlite\"]},\"author\":{\"value\":\"tomasol\",\"matchLevel\":\"none\",\"matchedWords\":[]}},\"_rankingInfo\":{\"nbTypos\":0,\"firstMatchedWord\":0,\"proximityDistance\":0,\"userScore\":81,\"geoDistance\":0,\"geoPrecision\":1,\"nbExactWords\":0,\"words\":1,\"filters\":1}},{\"title\":\"Show HN: Base, an SQLite database editor for macOS\",\"url\":\"https://menial.co.uk/base/\",\"author\":\"__bb\",\"points\":693,\"story_text\":\"I recently released v3 of Base, my SQLite editor for macOS.<p>The goal of this app is to provide a comfortable native GUI for SQLite, without it turning into a massive IDE-style app.<p>The coolest features are<p>- That it can handle full altering of tables, which is quite finicky to do manually with SQLite.<p>- It has a more detailed display of column constraints than most editors. Each constraint is shown as an icon if active, with full details available on clicking the icon.<p>This update also adds support for attaching databases, which is a bit fiddly with macOS sandboxing.<p>I&#x27;d love to hear any feedback or answer any questions.\",\"num_comments\":181,\"story_id\":45014131,\"created_at_i\":1756131458,\"created_at\":\"2025-08-25T14:17:38Z\",\"updated_at\":\"2026-09-11T16:21:12Z\",\"_tags\":[\"story\",\"author___bb\",\"story_45014131\",\"show_hn\"],\"children\":[45014439,45014534,45014558,45014568,45014625,45014671,45014680,45014690,45014693,45014706,45014744,45014761,45014789,45014799,45014822,45014852,45014862,45014937,45014943,45014961,45015098,45015190,45015316,45015379,45015724,45016031,45016037,45016276,45016311,45016346,45016391,45016552,45016910,45017535,45017588,45017695,45018190,45018192,45018410,45018720,45018722,45018730,45018737,45018799,45018830,45019250,45019438,45020095,45020181,45020223,45020261,45020511,45020700,45021420,45021683,45023183,45023489,45025265,45026470,45078561,45088948],\"objectID\":\"45014131\",\"_highlightResult\":{\"title\":{\"value\":\"Show HN: Base, an <em>SQLite</em> database editor for macOS\",\"matchLevel\":\"full\",\"fullyHighlighted\":false,\"matchedWords\":[\"sqlite\"]},\"url\":{\"value\":\"https://menial.co.uk/base/\",\"matchLevel\":\"none\",\"matchedWords\":[]},\"author\":{\"value\":\"__bb\",\"matchLevel\":\"none\",\"matchedWords\":[]},\"story_text\":{\"value\":\"I recently released v3 of Base, my <em>SQLite</em> editor for macOS.<p>The goal of this app is to provide a comfortable native GUI for <em>SQLite</em>, without it turning into a massive IDE-style app.<p>The coolest features are<p>- That it can handle full altering of tables, which is quite finicky to do manually with <em>SQLite</em>.<p>- It has a more detailed display of column constraints than most editors. Each constraint is shown as an icon if active, with full details available on clicking the icon.<p>This update also adds support for attaching databases, which is a bit fiddly with macOS sandboxing.<p>I'd love to hear any feedback or answer any questions.\",\"matchLevel\":\"full\",\"fullyHighlighted\":false,\"matchedWords\":[\"sqlite\"]}},\"_rankingInfo\":{\"nbTypos\":0,\"firstMatchedWord\":0,\"proximityDistance\":0,\"userScore\":80,\"geoDistance\":0,\"geoPrecision\":1,\"nbExactWords\":0,\"words\":1,\"filters\":1}},{\"title\":\"SQLite Internals: Pages and B-trees\",\"url\":\"https://fly.io/blog/sqlite-internals-btree/\",\"author\":\"eatonphil\",\"points\":667,\"num_comments\":142,\"story_id\":32250426,\"created_at_i\":1658930154,\"created_at\":\"2022-07-27T13:55:54Z\",\"updated_at\":\"2026-07-18T01:33:14Z\",\"_tags\":[\"story\",\"author_eatonphil\",\"story_32250426\"],\"children\":[32251020,32251349,32251586,32251654,32251721,32252454,32253274,32254942,32255223,32255401,32256071,32257016,32258208,32259244,32260274,32260349,32260592,32261822],\"objectID\":\"32250426\",\"_highlightResult\":{\"title\":{\"value\":\"<em>SQLite</em> Internals: Pages and B-trees\",\"matchLevel\":\"full\",\"fullyHighlighted\":false,\"matchedWords\":[\"sqlite\"]},\"url\":{\"value\":\"https://fly.io/blog/<em>sqlite</em>-internals-btree/\",\"matchLevel\":\"full\",\"fullyHighlighted\":false,\"matchedWords\":[\"sqlite\"]},\"author\":{\"value\":\"eatonphil\",\"matchLevel\":\"none\",\"matchedWords\":[]}},\"_rankingInfo\":{\"nbTypos\":0,\"firstMatchedWord\":0,\"proximityDistance\":0,\"userScore\":79,\"geoDistance\":0,\"geoPrecision\":1,\"nbExactWords\":0,\"words\":1,\"filters\":1}},{\"title\":\"SQLite Is a Library of Congress Recommended Storage Format\",\"url\":\"https://sqlite.org/locrsf.html\",\"author\":\"whatisabcdefgh\",\"points\":663,\"num_comments\":192,\"story_id\":48042434,\"created_at_i\":1778104723,\"created_at\":\"2026-05-06T21:58:43Z\",\"updated_at\":\"2026-06-28T09:59:48Z\",\"_tags\":[\"story\",\"author_whatisabcdefgh\",\"story_48042434\"],\"children\":[48044400,48044600,48044819,48045122,48045160,48045275,48045505,48045580,48045670,48045867,48046007,48046019,48046384,48046791,48047306,48047393,48047913,48048075,48048560,48048729,48048887,48049483,48050083,48051112,48051132,48051246,48051878,48053632,48054167,48055465,48058432,48058516,48061013],\"objectID\":\"48042434\",\"_highlightResult\":{\"title\":{\"value\":\"<em>SQLite</em> Is a Library of Congress Recommended Storage Format\",\"matchLevel\":\"full\",\"fullyHighlighted\":false,\"matchedWords\":[\"sqlite\"]},\"url\":{\"value\":\"https://<em>sqlite</em>.org/locrsf.html\",\"matchLevel\":\"full\",\"fullyHighlighted\":false,\"matchedWords\":[\"sqlite\"]},\"author\":{\"value\":\"whatisabcdefgh\",\"matchLevel\":\"none\",\"matchedWords\":[]}},\"_rankingInfo\":{\"nbTypos\":0,\"firstMatchedWord\":0,\"proximityDistance\":0,\"userScore\":78,\"geoDistance\":0,\"geoPrecision\":1,\"nbExactWords\":0,\"words\":1,\"filters\":1}},{\"title\":\"Show HN: Drop-in SQS replacement based on SQLite\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/poundifdef/SmoothMQ\",\"author\":\"memset\",\"points\":656,\"story_text\":\"Hi! I wanted to share an open source API-compatible replacement for SQS. It&#x27;s written in Go, distributes as a single binary, and uses SQLite for underlying storage.<p>I wrote this because I wanted a queue with all the bells and whistles - searching, scheduling into the future, observability, and rate limiting - all the things that many modern task queue systems have.<p>But I didn&#x27;t want to rewrite my app, which was already using SQS. And I was frustrated that many of the best solutions out there (BullMQ, Oban, Sidekiq) were language-specific.<p>So I made an SQS-compatible replacement. All you have to do is replace the endpoint using AWS&#x27; native library in your language of choice.<p>For example, the queue works with Celery - you just change the connection string. From there, you can see all of your messages and their status, which is hard today in the SQS console (and flower doesn&#x27;t support SQS.)<p>It is written to be pluggable. The queue implementation uses SQLite, but I&#x27;ve been experimenting with RocksDB as a backend and you could even write one that uses Postgres. Similarly, you could implement multiple protocols (AMQP, PubSub, etc) on top of the underlying queue. I started with SQS because it is simple and I use it a lot.<p>It is written to be as easy to deploy as possible - a single go binary. I&#x27;m working on adding distributed and autoscale functionality as the next layer.<p>Today I have search, observability (via prometheus), unlimited message sizes, and the ability to schedule messages arbitrarily in the future.<p>In terms of monetization, the goal is to just have a hosted queue system. I believe this can be cheaper than SQS without sacrificing performance. Just as Backblaze and Minio have had success competing in the S3 space, I wanted to take a crack at queues.<p>I&#x27;d love your feedback!\",\"num_comments\":156,\"story_id\":40837610,\"created_at_i\":1719760268,\"created_at\":\"2024-06-30T15:11:08Z\",\"updated_at\":\"2025-03-16T02:20:39Z\",\"_tags\":[\"story\",\"author_memset\",\"story_40837610\",\"show_hn\"],\"children\":[40837943,40837985,40838032,40838079,40838082,40838096,40838115,40838150,40838206,40838257,40838269,40838357,40838372,40838384,40838385,40838440,40838598,40838606,40838725,40838832,40838862,40839227,40839282,40839342,40840024,40840044,40840183,40840223,40840683,40840839,40840916,40841227,40841750,40841872,40842654,40843354,40844083,40845111,40884753],\"objectID\":\"40837610\",\"_highlightResult\":{\"title\":{\"value\":\"Show HN: Drop-in SQS replacement based on <em>SQLite</em>\",\"matchLevel\":\"full\",\"fullyHighlighted\":false,\"matchedWords\":[\"sqlite\"]},\"url\":{\"value\":\"https://github.com/poundifdef/SmoothMQ\",\"matchLevel\":\"none\",\"matchedWords\":[]},\"author\":{\"value\":\"memset\",\"matchLevel\":\"none\",\"matchedWords\":[]},\"story_text\":{\"value\":\"Hi! I wanted to share an open source API-compatible replacement for SQS. It's written in Go, distributes as a single binary, and uses <em>SQLite</em> for underlying storage.<p>I wrote this because I wanted a queue with all the bells and whistles - searching, scheduling into the future, observability, and rate limiting - all the things that many modern task queue systems have.<p>But I didn't want to rewrite my app, which was already using SQS. And I was frustrated that many of the best solutions out there (BullMQ, Oban, Sidekiq) were language-specific.<p>So I made an SQS-compatible replacement. All you have to do is replace the endpoint using AWS' native library in your language of choice.<p>For example, the queue works with Celery - you just change the connection string. From there, you can see all of your messages and their status, which is hard today in the SQS console (and flower doesn't support SQS.)<p>It is written to be pluggable. The queue implementation uses <em>SQLite</em>, but I've been experimenting with RocksDB as a backend and you could even write one that uses Postgres. Similarly, you could implement multiple protocols (AMQP, PubSub, etc) on top of the underlying queue. I started with SQS because it is simple and I use it a lot.<p>It is written to be as easy to deploy as possible - a single go binary. I'm working on adding distributed and autoscale functionality as the next layer.<p>Today I have search, observability (via prometheus), unlimited message sizes, and the ability to schedule messages arbitrarily in the future.<p>In terms of monetization, the goal is to just have a hosted queue system. I believe this can be cheaper than SQS without sacrificing performance. Just as Backblaze and Minio have had success competing in the S3 space, I wanted to take a crack at queues.<p>I'd love your feedback!\",\"matchLevel\":\"full\",\"fullyHighlighted\":false,\"matchedWords\":[\"sqlite\"]}},\"_rankingInfo\":{\"nbTypos\":0,\"firstMatchedWord\":0,\"proximityDistance\":0,\"userScore\":77,\"geoDistance\":0,\"geoPrecision\":1,\"nbExactWords\":0,\"words\":1,\"filters\":1}},{\"title\":\"SQLiteStudio: Create, edit, browse SQLite databases\",\"url\":\"https://sqlitestudio.pl/\",\"author\":\"thunderbong\",\"points\":626,\"num_comments\":123,\"story_id\":42232000,\"created_at_i\":1732495006,\"created_at\":\"2024-11-25T00:36:46Z\",\"updated_at\":\"2026-01-08T23:02:05Z\",\"_tags\":[\"story\",\"author_thunderbong\",\"story_42232000\"],\"children\":[42232671,42232793,42232878,42233390,42233503,42233637,42233974,42234100,42234398,42234401,42234654,42234782,42235035,42235043,42235482,42236253,42236415,42236754,42236756,42237065,42237098,42237145,42239732,42240341,42241753,42250377,42253210,42256705],\"objectID\":\"42232000\",\"_highlightResult\":{\"title\":{\"value\":\"SQLiteStudio: Create, edit, browse <em>SQLite</em> databases\",\"matchLevel\":\"full\",\"fullyHighlighted\":false,\"matchedWords\":[\"sqlite\"]},\"url\":{\"value\":\"https://sqlitestudio.pl/\",\"matchLevel\":\"none\",\"matchedWords\":[]},\"author\":{\"value\":\"thunderbong\",\"matchLevel\":\"none\",\"matchedWords\":[]}},\"_rankingInfo\":{\"nbTypos\":0,\"firstMatchedWord\":0,\"proximityDistance\":0,\"userScore\":76,\"geoDistance\":0,\"geoPrecision\":1,\"nbExactWords\":0,\"words\":1,\"filters\":1}},{\"title\":\"Show HN: ElectricSQL, Postgres to SQLite active-active sync for local-first apps\",\"url\":\"https://electric-sql.com/\",\"author\":\"samwillis\",\"points\":617,\"story_text\":\"Hi HN, James, Valter, Sam and the team from ElectricSQL here.<p>We&#x27;re really excited to be sharing ElectricSQL with you today. It&#x27;s an open source, local-first sync layer that can be used to build reactive, realtime, offline-capable apps directly on Postgres with two way active-active sync to SQLite (including with WASM in the browser).<p>Electric comprises a sync layer (built with Elixir) placed in front of your Postgres database and a type safe client that allows you to bidirectionally sync data from your Postgres to local SQLite databases. This sync is CRDT-based, resilient to conflicting edits from multiple nodes at the same time, and works after being offline for extended periods.<p>Some good links to get started:<p>- website: <a href=\\\"https:&#x2F;&#x2F;electric-sql.com\\\" rel=\\\"nofollow noreferrer\\\">https:&#x2F;&#x2F;electric-sql.com</a><p>- docs: <a href=\\\"https:&#x2F;&#x2F;electric-sql.com&#x2F;docs\\\" rel=\\\"nofollow noreferrer\\\">https:&#x2F;&#x2F;electric-sql.com&#x2F;docs</a><p>- code: <a href=\\\"https:&#x2F;&#x2F;github.com&#x2F;electric-sql&#x2F;electric\\\">https:&#x2F;&#x2F;github.com&#x2F;electric-sql&#x2F;electric</a><p>- introducing post: <a href=\\\"https:&#x2F;&#x2F;electric-sql.com&#x2F;blog&#x2F;2023&#x2F;09&#x2F;20&#x2F;introducing-electricsql-v0.6\\\" rel=\\\"nofollow noreferrer\\\">https:&#x2F;&#x2F;electric-sql.com&#x2F;blog&#x2F;2023&#x2F;09&#x2F;20&#x2F;introducing-electri...</a><p>You can also see some demo applications:<p>- Linear clone: <a href=\\\"https:&#x2F;&#x2F;linear-lite.electric-sql.com\\\" rel=\\\"nofollow noreferrer\\\">https:&#x2F;&#x2F;linear-lite.electric-sql.com</a><p>- Realtime demo: <a href=\\\"https:&#x2F;&#x2F;electric-sql.com&#x2F;docs&#x2F;intro&#x2F;multi-user\\\" rel=\\\"nofollow noreferrer\\\">https:&#x2F;&#x2F;electric-sql.com&#x2F;docs&#x2F;intro&#x2F;multi-user</a><p>- Conflict-free offline: <a href=\\\"https:&#x2F;&#x2F;electric-sql.com&#x2F;docs&#x2F;intro&#x2F;offline\\\" rel=\\\"nofollow noreferrer\\\">https:&#x2F;&#x2F;electric-sql.com&#x2F;docs&#x2F;intro&#x2F;offline</a><p>The Electric team actually includes two of the inventors of CRDTs, Marc Shapiro and Nuno Preguiça, and a number of their collaborators who&#x27;ve pioneered a lot of tech underpinning local-first software. We are privileged to be building on their research and delighted to be surfacing so much work in a product you can now try out.\",\"num_comments\":170,\"story_id\":37584049,\"created_at_i\":1695217615,\"created_at\":\"2023-09-20T13:46:55Z\",\"updated_at\":\"2025-12-16T05:16:35Z\",\"_tags\":[\"story\",\"author_samwillis\",\"story_37584049\",\"show_hn\"],\"children\":[37584202,37584232,37584246,37584248,37584293,37584329,37584395,37584800,37584945,37585073,37585220,37585357,37585363,37585423,37585510,37585535,37585798,37586036,37586111,37586227,37586255,37586306,37586430,37586473,37586547,37586630,37586643,37586645,37586701,37586779,37587661,37587814,37587852,37587863,37587998,37588066,37588088,37588212,37588813,37588864,37588923,37589088,37589478,37590034,37590228,37590480,37590659,37591138,37591402,37591442,37591514,37591762,37591975,37592069,37592347,37592445,37592707,37592749,37592993,37593214,37593363,37593570,37593649,37593697,37594074,37594609,37596361,37597380,37599304,37603496,37624813],\"objectID\":\"37584049\",\"_highlightResult\":{\"title\":{\"value\":\"Show HN: ElectricSQL, Postgres to <em>SQLite</em> active-active sync for local-first apps\",\"matchLevel\":\"full\",\"fullyHighlighted\":false,\"matchedWords\":[\"sqlite\"]},\"url\":{\"value\":\"https://electric-sql.com/\",\"matchLevel\":\"none\",\"matchedWords\":[]},\"author\":{\"value\":\"samwillis\",\"matchLevel\":\"none\",\"matchedWords\":[]},\"story_text\":{\"value\":\"Hi HN, James, Valter, Sam and the team from ElectricSQL here.<p>We're really excited to be sharing ElectricSQL with you today. It's an open source, local-first sync layer that can be used to build reactive, realtime, offline-capable apps directly on Postgres with two way active-active sync to <em>SQLite</em> (including with WASM in the browser).<p>Electric comprises a sync layer (built with Elixir) placed in front of your Postgres database and a type safe client that allows you to bidirectionally sync data from your Postgres to local <em>SQLite</em> databases. This sync is CRDT-based, resilient to conflicting edits from multiple nodes at the same time, and works after being offline for extended periods.<p>Some good links to get started:<p>- website: <a href=\\\"https://electric-sql.com\\\" rel=\\\"nofollow noreferrer\\\">https://electric-sql.com</a><p>- docs: <a href=\\\"https://electric-sql.com/docs\\\" rel=\\\"nofollow noreferrer\\\">https://electric-sql.com/docs</a><p>- code: <a href=\\\"https://github.com/electric-sql/electric\\\">https://github.com/electric-sql/electric</a><p>- introducing post: <a href=\\\"https://electric-sql.com/blog/2023/09/20/introducing-electricsql-v0.6\\\" rel=\\\"nofollow noreferrer\\\">https://electric-sql.com/blog/2023/09/20/introducing-electri...</a><p>You can also see some demo applications:<p>- Linear clone: <a href=\\\"https://linear-lite.electric-sql.com\\\" rel=\\\"nofollow noreferrer\\\">https://linear-lite.electric-sql.com</a><p>- Realtime demo: <a href=\\\"https://electric-sql.com/docs/intro/multi-user\\\" rel=\\\"nofollow noreferrer\\\">https://electric-sql.com/docs/intro/multi-user</a><p>- Conflict-free offline: <a href=\\\"https://electric-sql.com/docs/intro/offline\\\" rel=\\\"nofollow noreferrer\\\">https://electric-sql.com/docs/intro/offline</a><p>The Electric team actually includes two of the inventors of CRDTs, Marc Shapiro and Nuno Preguiça, and a number of their collaborators who've pioneered a lot of tech underpinning local-first software. We are privileged to be building on their research and delighted to be surfacing so much work in a product you can now try out.\",\"matchLevel\":\"full\",\"fullyHighlighted\":false,\"matchedWords\":[\"sqlite\"]}},\"_rankingInfo\":{\"nbTypos\":0,\"firstMatchedWord\":0,\"proximityDistance\":0,\"userScore\":75,\"geoDistance\":0,\"geoPrecision\":1,\"nbExactWords\":0,\"words\":1,\"filters\":1}},{\"title\":\"SQLite small blob storage: 35% Faster Than the Filesystem\",\"url\":\"https://www.sqlite.org/fasterthanfs.html\",\"author\":\"i_feel_great\",\"points\":612,\"num_comments\":202,\"story_id\":14550060,\"created_at_i\":1497402660,\"created_at\":\"2017-06-14T01:11:00Z\",\"updated_at\":\"2025-04-25T19:12:08Z\",\"_tags\":[\"story\",\"author_i_feel_great\",\"story_14550060\"],\"children\":[14550377,14550394,14550415,14550417,14550477,14550488,14550503,14550584,14550675,14550681,1