      "text": "{\"hits\":[{\"author\":\"dsacco\",\"comment_text\":\"Bingo. I&#x27;m not talking about &quot;cybersecurity&quot; positions. I&#x27;m talking about people doing advanced work in exploitation, reverse engineering and cryptography on a consulting basis or those who work in boutique shops.<p>I pass the $300k mark annually through AppSec consulting, and this is not at maximum utilization, nor is it a particularly &quot;hard&quot; discipline in security.\",\"story_id\":9892724,\"story_title\":\"Malware and Hacking Forum Darkode Is Shut Down; Dozens Arrested\",\"story_url\":\"http://www.npr.org/sections/thetwo-way/2015/07/15/423196810/malware-and-hacking-forum-darkode-is-shut-down-dozens-arrested\",\"parent_id\":9894075,\"created_at_i\":1437002693,\"created_at\":\"2015-07-15T23:24:53Z\",\"updated_at\":\"2024-09-19T22:05:06Z\",\"_tags\":[\"comment\",\"author_dsacco\",\"story_9892724\"],\"objectID\":\"9894654\",\"_highlightResult\":{\"author\":{\"value\":\"dsacco\",\"matchLevel\":\"none\",\"matchedWords\":[]},\"comment_text\":{\"value\":\"Bingo. I'm not talking about &quot;cybersecurity&quot; positions. I'm talking about people doing advanced work in exploitation, <em>reverse</em> <em>engineering</em> and cryptography on a consulting basis or those who work in boutique shops.<p>I pass the $300k mark annually through AppSec consulting, and this is not at maximum utilization, nor is it a particularly &quot;hard&quot; discipline in security.\",\"matchLevel\":\"full\",\"fullyHighlighted\":false,\"matchedWords\":[\"reverse\",\"engineering\"]},\"story_title\":{\"value\":\"Malware and Hacking Forum Darkode Is Shut Down; Dozens Arrested\",\"matchLevel\":\"none\",\"matchedWords\":[]},\"story_url\":{\"value\":\"http://www.npr.org/sections/thetwo-way/2015/07/15/423196810/malware-and-hacking-forum-darkode-is-shut-down-dozens-arrested\",\"matchLevel\":\"none\",\"matchedWords\":[]}},\"_rankingInfo\":{\"nbTypos\":0,\"firstMatchedWord\":2000,\"proximityDistance\":1,\"userScore\":179,\"geoDistance\":0,\"geoPrecision\":1,\"nbExactWords\":2,\"words\":2,\"filters\":1}},{\"author\":\"choppaface\",\"comment_text\":\"So is the major advantage of Eddystone that Google will implement it and won&#x27;t sue you for using it in ways they don&#x27;t like?<p>Eddystone isn&#x27;t alone; there&#x27;s AltBeacon, which is also an &#x27;open&#x27; alternative: <a href=\\\"http:&#x2F;&#x2F;altbeacon.org&#x2F;\\\" rel=\\\"nofollow\\\">http:&#x2F;&#x2F;altbeacon.org&#x2F;</a>  Though it looks like they might be &#x27;merging&#x27; with Eddystone.<p>AltBeacon was born out of Apple giving Radius Networks a cease &amp; desist about a year ago for reverse-engineering the iBeacon protocol and distributing an open-source Android toolkit (with paid enhancements) for iBeacons: <a href=\\\"http:&#x2F;&#x2F;beekn.net&#x2F;2014&#x2F;07&#x2F;ibeacon-for-android&#x2F;\\\" rel=\\\"nofollow\\\">http:&#x2F;&#x2F;beekn.net&#x2F;2014&#x2F;07&#x2F;ibeacon-for-android&#x2F;</a><p>The Android iBeacon code used to be on Github but is no longer there.  IMO the core &quot;infringement&quot; (if there really is any) is about 10-20 lines of Java that unpacks an iBeacon (major, minor, uuid) from a byte buffer and another bit of Java that aims to map RSSI to Apple&#x27;s simple model for estimating iBeacon distances.  The library did wrap the functionality in a nice background-threaded interface, and their API is distinct from Apple&#x27;s.  On the iPhone, there&#x27;s a BLE daemon that powers iBeacon and is rather crashy (at least as of iOS8).  The Radius Networks solution was clearly distinct.  The story would be a textbook example of Apple&#x27;s legal department hurting open innovation except for the detail that Radius Networks was offering a paid developer product atop the open source offering.  (But I doubt they were making much money from it).<p>There are a lot of problems with iBeacon technology:<p>* A lot of people have bluetooth off.  The surveys out there have a lot of variance, and you typically have a 50&#x2F;50 chance that a user with a very modern smartphone will have BLE enabled.<p>* Droid battery usage isn&#x27;t <i>too</i> bad, but Apple&#x27;s solution has a distinct advantage because the BLE daemon powering the feature can interop more closely with iOS and eat less CPU.  It&#x27;s really hard to get a consistent experience on both platforms.<p>* To do anything productive with the iBeacon (major, minor, UUID), you probably want to ping a webservice, which will be hard unless you have free wifi or you&#x27;ve made fancy deals with carriers (or Apple).  And if there&#x27;s wifi, you might as well just try to use <i>that</i> as a beacon (ala PayPal&#x27;s product).<p>* You probably &#x2F;dont&#x2F; want to have iBeacons trigger any sort of immediate advert or notification unless the user has previously opted in to the service.  A lot of users are starting to <i>not</i> grant notification and other privs by default.  Most users also do NOT like background location stuff.  There are definitely user segments that differ, but you&#x27;ll likely need to do a great deal of customer research to validate using iBeacon.  If your app is payments, there&#x27;s already NFC.  If your app is marketing, you might get higher engagement with Augmented Reality or something tied to some other location service (ala Amex &amp; 4sq).\",\"story_id\":9885896,\"story_title\":\"Google’s Eddystone – a flexible, open-source iBeacon alternative\",\"story_url\":\"http://arstechnica.com/gadgets/2015/07/meet-googles-eddystone-a-flexible-open-source-ibeacon-fighter/\",\"parent_id\":9885896,\"created_at_i\":1436938821,\"created_at\":\"2015-07-15T05:40:21Z\",\"updated_at\":\"2023-09-07T03:12:36Z\",\"_tags\":[\"comment\",\"author_choppaface\",\"story_9885896\"],\"points\":null,\"objectID\":\"9889884\",\"_highlightResult\":{\"author\":{\"value\":\"choppaface\",\"matchLevel\":\"none\",\"matchedWords\":[]},\"comment_text\":{\"value\":\"So is the major advantage of Eddystone that Google will implement it and won't sue you for using it in ways they don't like?<p>Eddystone isn't alone; there's AltBeacon, which is also an 'open' alternative: <a href=\\\"http://altbeacon.org/\\\" rel=\\\"nofollow\\\">http://altbeacon.org/</a>  Though it looks like they might be 'merging' with Eddystone.<p>AltBeacon was born out of Apple giving Radius Networks a cease &amp; desist about a year ago for <em>reverse</em>-<em>engineering</em> the iBeacon protocol and distributing an open-source Android toolkit (with paid enhancements) for iBeacons: <a href=\\\"http://beekn.net/2014/07/ibeacon-for-android/\\\" rel=\\\"nofollow\\\">http://beekn.net/2014/07/ibeacon-for-android/</a><p>The Android iBeacon code used to be on Github but is no longer there.  IMO the core &quot;infringement&quot; (if there really is any) is about 10-20 lines of Java that unpacks an iBeacon (major, minor, uuid) from a byte buffer and another bit of Java that aims to map RSSI to Apple's simple model for estimating iBeacon distances.  The library did wrap the functionality in a nice background-threaded interface, and their API is distinct from Apple's.  On the iPhone, there's a BLE daemon that powers iBeacon and is rather crashy (at least as of iOS8).  The Radius Networks solution was clearly distinct.  The story would be a textbook example of Apple's legal department hurting open innovation except for the detail that Radius Networks was offering a paid developer product atop the open source offering.  (But I doubt they were making much money from it).<p>There are a lot of problems with iBeacon technology:<p>* A lot of people have bluetooth off.  The surveys out there have a lot of variance, and you typically have a 50/50 chance that a user with a very modern smartphone will have BLE enabled.<p>* Droid battery usage isn't <i>too</i> bad, but Apple's solution has a distinct advantage because the BLE daemon powering the feature can interop more closely with iOS and eat less CPU.  It's really hard to get a consistent experience on both platforms.<p>* To do anything productive with the iBeacon (major, minor, UUID), you probably want to ping a webservice, which will be hard unless you have free wifi or you've made fancy deals with carriers (or Apple).  And if there's wifi, you might as well just try to use <i>that</i> as a beacon (ala PayPal's product).<p>* You probably /dont/ want to have iBeacons trigger any sort of immediate advert or notification unless the user has previously opted in to the service.  A lot of users are starting to <i>not</i> grant notification and other privs by default.  Most users also do NOT like background location stuff.  There are definitely user segments that differ, but you'll likely need to do a great deal of customer research to validate using iBeacon.  If your app is payments, there's already NFC.  If your app is marketing, you might get higher engagement with Augmented Reality or something tied to some other location service (ala Amex &amp; 4sq).\",\"matchLevel\":\"full\",\"fullyHighlighted\":false,\"matchedWords\":[\"reverse\",\"engineering\"]},\"story_title\":{\"value\":\"Google’s Eddystone – a flexible, open-source iBeacon alternative\",\"matchLevel\":\"none\",\"matchedWords\":[]},\"story_url\":{\"value\":\"http://arstechnica.com/gadgets/2015/07/meet-googles-eddystone-a-flexible-open-source-ibeacon-fighter/\",\"matchLevel\":\"none\",\"matchedWords\":[]}},\"_rankingInfo\":{\"nbTypos\":0,\"firstMatchedWord\":2000,\"proximityDistance\":1,\"userScore\":178,\"geoDistance\":0,\"geoPrecision\":1,\"nbExactWords\":2,\"words\":2,\"filters\":1}},{\"author\":\"TD-Linux\",\"comment_text\":\"Wow, that&#x27;s a ridiculously defeatist attitude. Comparing reverse engineering a FPGA to landing on the moon?<p>First of all, it&#x27;s a relatively new field for open source. The icestorm project only got a complete synthesis to bitstream pipeline working last month. So although you see &quot;comments all the time&quot;, it&#x27;s not like development is stagnating. In fact, it got off the ground for the first time. It&#x27;s not surprising it lacks support for hard peripherals at this point in development. Lack of timing analysis is a showstopper for any serious design as well, but it&#x27;s not like it&#x27;s an unsolvable problem.<p>&gt;There&#x27;s zero incentive for Xilinx to allow a free software competitor\\nWhat? How is another system that sells their chips a competitor? AMD reacted to open source driver development by providing documentation for their GPUs. The exact opposite seems kind of an extreme position. Xilinx is already pretty open source friendly on the software side with their Linux kernel contributions. And it&#x27;s not like they are the only vendor in existence.<p>The security features on FPGAs right now are to prevent people from getting the bitstreams out. Just like on virtually every modern microcontroller. That hasn&#x27;t stopped open source toolchains one bit.<p>FWIW I&#x27;ve done designs on the Zynq too, it&#x27;s certainly one of the most complicated cases and I doubt it will have open source support any time soon. But most of the critical stuff is in the hard blocks (e.g. ddr timing and the like), and it&#x27;s actually the easiest because there is not really any p&amp;r work to do there, it&#x27;s just figuring out what the configuration bits are.<p>(sorry if I sound overly defensive, I work on free video codecs and get a very similar attitude out of many other people who feel that performant video codecs are not possible outside of MPEG. it gets incredibly frustrating)\",\"story_id\":9881186,\"story_title\":\"An open source Xilinx Spartan 6 miniPCIe development board\",\"story_url\":\"https://github.com/polysome/aggregate-1\",\"parent_id\":9883201,\"created_at_i\":1436858822,\"created_at\":\"2015-07-14T07:27:02Z\",\"updated_at\":\"2024-09-19T22:04:01Z\",\"_tags\":[\"comment\",\"author_TD-Linux\",\"story_9881186\"],\"objectID\":\"9883300\",\"_highlightResult\":{\"author\":{\"value\":\"TD-Linux\",\"matchLevel\":\"none\",\"matchedWords\":[]},\"comment_text\":{\"value\":\"Wow, that's a ridiculously defeatist attitude. Comparing <em>reverse</em> <em>engineering</em> a FPGA to landing on the moon?<p>First of all, it's a relatively new field for open source. The icestorm project only got a complete synthesis to bitstream pipeline working last month. So although you see &quot;comments all the time&quot;, it's not like development is stagnating. In fact, it got off the ground for the first time. It's not surprising it lacks support for hard peripherals at this point in development. Lack of timing analysis is a showstopper for any serious design as well, but it's not like it's an unsolvable problem.<p>&gt;There's zero incentive for Xilinx to allow a free software competitor\\nWhat? How is another system that sells their chips a competitor? AMD reacted to open source driver development by providing documentation for their GPUs. The exact opposite seems kind of an extreme position. Xilinx is already pretty open source friendly on the software side with their Linux kernel contributions. And it's not like they are the only vendor in existence.<p>The security features on FPGAs right now are to prevent people from getting the bitstreams out. Just like on virtually every modern microcontroller. That hasn't stopped open source toolchains one bit.<p>FWIW I've done designs on the Zynq too, it's certainly one of the most complicated cases and I doubt it will have open source support any time soon. But most of the critical stuff is in the hard blocks (e.g. ddr timing and the like), and it's actually the easiest because there is not really any p&amp;r work to do there, it's just figuring out what the configuration bits are.<p>(sorry if I sound overly defensive, I work on free video codecs and get a very similar attitude out of many other people who feel that performant video codecs are not possible outside of MPEG. it gets incredibly frustrating)\",\"matchLevel\":\"full\",\"fullyHighlighted\":false,\"matchedWords\":[\"reverse\",\"engineering\"]},\"story_title\":{\"value\":\"An open source Xilinx Spartan 6 miniPCIe development board\",\"matchLevel\":\"none\",\"matchedWords\":[]},\"story_url\":{\"value\":\"https://github.com/polysome/aggregate-1\",\"matchLevel\":\"none\",\"matchedWords\":[]}},\"_rankingInfo\":{\"nbTypos\":0,\"firstMatchedWord\":2000,\"proximityDistance\":1,\"userScore\":177,\"geoDistance\":0,\"geoPrecision\":1,\"nbExactWords\":2,\"words\":2,\"filters\":1}},{\"author\":\"wtallis\",\"comment_text\":\"Patent threats don&#x27;t really hinder community reverse engineering efforts, but they do discourage the major vendors from being open-source friendly, because nobody wants to open up their tech only to get sued over the details you just disclosed.\",\"story_id\":9881186,\"story_title\":\"An open source Xilinx Spartan 6 miniPCIe development board\",\"story_url\":\"https://github.com/polysome/aggregate-1\",\"parent_id\":9883042,\"created_at_i\":1436855083,\"created_at\":\"2015-07-14T06:24:43Z\",\"updated_at\":\"2024-09-19T22:04:01Z\",\"_tags\":[\"comment\",\"author_wtallis\",\"story_9881186\"],\"objectID\":\"9883174\",\"_highlightResult\":{\"author\":{\"value\":\"wtallis\",\"matchLevel\":\"none\",\"matchedWords\":[]},\"comment_text\":{\"value\":\"Patent threats don't really hinder community <em>reverse</em> <em>engineering</em> efforts, but they do discourage the major vendors from being open-source friendly, because nobody wants to open up their tech only to get sued over the details you just disclosed.\",\"matchLevel\":\"full\",\"fullyHighlighted\":false,\"matchedWords\":[\"reverse\",\"engineering\"]},\"story_title\":{\"value\":\"An open source Xilinx Spartan 6 miniPCIe development board\",\"matchLevel\":\"none\",\"matchedWords\":[]},\"story_url\":{\"value\":\"https://github.com/polysome/aggregate-1\",\"matchLevel\":\"none\",\"matchedWords\":[]}},\"_rankingInfo\":{\"nbTypos\":0,\"firstMatchedWord\":2000,\"proximityDistance\":1,\"userScore\":176,\"geoDistance\":0,\"geoPrecision\":1,\"nbExactWords\":2,\"words\":2,\"filters\":1}},{\"author\":\"jakemask\",\"comment_text\":\"I&#x27;m excited that all the AVR I learned reverse engineering mouse firmware isn&#x27;t going to go to waste. I honestly thought it was knowledge I would never use again.\",\"story_id\":9882587,\"story_title\":\"Starfighter, Summer 2015\",\"story_url\":\"http://sockpuppet.org/blog/2015/07/13/starfighter\",\"parent_id\":9882587,\"created_at_i\":1436843731,\"created_at\":\"2015-07-14T03:15:31Z\",\"updated_at\":\"2023-09-07T03:12:03Z\",\"_tags\":[\"comment\",\"author_jakemask\",\"story_9882587\"],\"children\":[9882846],\"points\":null,\"objectID\":\"9882662\",\"_highlightResult\":{\"author\":{\"value\":\"jakemask\",\"matchLevel\":\"none\",\"matchedWords\":[]},\"comment_text\":{\"value\":\"I'm excited that all the AVR I learned <em>reverse</em> <em>engineering</em> mouse firmware isn't going to go to waste. I honestly thought it was knowledge I would never use again.\",\"matchLevel\":\"full\",\"fullyHighlighted\":false,\"matchedWords\":[\"reverse\",\"engineering\"]},\"story_title\":{\"value\":\"Starfighter, Summer 2015\",\"matchLevel\":\"none\",\"matchedWords\":[]},\"story_url\":{\"value\":\"http://sockpuppet.org/blog/2015/07/13/starfighter\",\"matchLevel\":\"none\",\"matchedWords\":[]}},\"_rankingInfo\":{\"nbTypos\":0,\"firstMatchedWord\":2000,\"proximityDistance\":1,\"userScore\":175,\"geoDistance\":0,\"geoPrecision\":1,\"nbExactWords\":2,\"words\":2,\"filters\":1}},{\"author\":\"n42\",\"comment_text\":\"Back in the early&#x2F;mid 2000s I was involved with the PK Hack community, which was&#x2F;is a group of people who focus on reverse engineering the EarthBound ROM (documenting and building editors for it, etc). I remember a guy named Flynman was actually putting together a sort of EarthBound scripting language that compiled down into the text engine&#x27;s control codes. I&#x27;m not sure if that ever led anywhere, but it sounded pretty cool at the time. I might dig around to see if I can find it somewhere on the Starmen.net forums<p>edit: the original was called CCBasic, but I guess someone took the concept and reworked it here: <a href=\\\"https:&#x2F;&#x2F;forum.starmen.net&#x2F;forum&#x2F;Community&#x2F;PKHack&#x2F;CCScript-A-new-revolution-in-text-editing-D&#x2F;page&#x2F;1&#x2F;\\\" rel=\\\"nofollow\\\">https:&#x2F;&#x2F;forum.starmen.net&#x2F;forum&#x2F;Community&#x2F;PKHack&#x2F;CCScript-A-...</a><p>and the language docs: <a href=\\\"http:&#x2F;&#x2F;starmen.net&#x2F;pkhack&#x2F;ccscript&#x2F;Tutorial.html\\\" rel=\\\"nofollow\\\">http:&#x2F;&#x2F;starmen.net&#x2F;pkhack&#x2F;ccscript&#x2F;Tutorial.html</a>\",\"story_id\":9878061,\"story_title\":\"A look at EarthBound's text system and scripting language (2011)\",\"story_url\":\"http://earthboundcentral.com/2011/04/a-look-at-the-mother-2-side/\",\"parent_id\":9880572,\"created_at_i\":1436822529,\"created_at\":\"2015-07-13T21:22:09Z\",\"updated_at\":\"2023-09-07T03:11:58Z\",\"_tags\":[\"comment\",\"author_n42\",\"story_9878061\"],\"points\":null,\"objectID\":\"9881204\",\"_highlightResult\":{\"author\":{\"value\":\"n42\",\"matchLevel\":\"none\",\"matchedWords\":[]},\"comment_text\":{\"value\":\"Back in the early/mid 2000s I was involved with the PK Hack community, which was/is a group of people who focus on <em>reverse</em> <em>engineering</em> the EarthBound ROM (documenting and building editors for it, etc). I remember a guy named Flynman was actually putting together a sort of EarthBound scripting language that compiled down into the text engine's control codes. I'm not sure if that ever led anywhere, but it sounded pretty cool at the time. I might dig around to see if I can find it somewhere on the Starmen.net forums<p>edit: the original was called CCBasic, but I guess someone took the concept and reworked it here: <a href=\\\"https://forum.starmen.net/forum/Community/PKHack/CCScript-A-new-revolution-in-text-editing-D/page/1/\\\" rel=\\\"nofollow\\\">https://forum.starmen.net/forum/Community/PKHack/CCScript-A-...</a><p>and the language docs: <a href=\\\"http://starmen.net/pkhack/ccscript/Tutorial.html\\\" rel=\\\"nofollow\\\">http://starmen.net/pkhack/ccscript/Tutorial.html</a>\",\"matchLevel\":\"full\",\"fullyHighlighted\":false,\"matchedWords\":[\"reverse\",\"engineering\"]},\"story_title\":{\"value\":\"A look at EarthBound's text system and scripting language (2011)\",\"matchLevel\":\"none\",\"matchedWords\":[]},\"story_url\":{\"value\":\"http://earthboundcentral.com/2011/04/a-look-at-the-mother-2-side/\",\"matchLevel\":\"none\",\"matchedWords\":[]}},\"_rankingInfo\":{\"nbTypos\":0,\"firstMatchedWord\":2000,\"proximityDistance\":1,\"userScore\":174,\"geoDistance\":0,\"geoPrecision\":1,\"nbExactWords\":2,\"words\":2,\"filters\":1}},{\"author\":\"WesleyJohnson\",\"comment_text\":\"Carmageddon was my first exposure to what I would call \\\"tinkering\\\" with games. I was amazed at the things you could get the cards to do by changing the cars' config files for speed, weight, wheel diameter and etc. I credit this game and \\\"reverse engineering\\\" Geocities sites as what sealed my fate in being some sort of developer or programmer. Not to mention it was a terribly fun game to play, regardless of the customization ability. I knew it had some controversy when it came out, but it's interesting to see just what they went through in getting it made.\",\"story_id\":986771,\"story_title\":\"The Making Of… Carmageddon\",\"story_url\":\"http://www.edge-online.com/magazine/the-making-of%E2%80%A6-carmageddon\",\"parent_id\":986771,\"created_at_i\":1260404725,\"created_at\":\"2009-12-10T00:25:25Z\",\"updated_at\":\"2024-09-19T16:52:48Z\",\"_tags\":[\"comment\",\"author_WesleyJohnson\",\"story_986771\"],\"objectID\":\"986975\",\"_highlightResult\":{\"author\":{\"value\":\"WesleyJohnson\",\"matchLevel\":\"none\",\"matchedWords\":[]},\"comment_text\":{\"value\":\"Carmageddon was my first exposure to what I would call \\\"tinkering\\\" with games. I was amazed at the things you could get the cards to do by changing the cars' config files for speed, weight, wheel diameter and etc. I credit this game and \\\"<em>reverse</em> <em>engineering</em>\\\" Geocities sites as what sealed my fate in being some sort of developer or programmer. Not to mention it was a terribly fun game to play, regardless of the customization ability. I knew it had some controversy when it came out, but it's interesting to see just what they went through in getting it made.\",\"matchLevel\":\"full\",\"fullyHighlighted\":false,\"matchedWords\":[\"reverse\",\"engineering\"]},\"story_title\":{\"value\":\"The Making Of… Carmageddon\",\"matchLevel\":\"none\",\"matchedWords\":[]},\"story_url\":{\"value\":\"http://www.edge-online.com/magazine/the-making-of%E2%80%A6-carmageddon\",\"matchLevel\":\"none\",\"matchedWords\":[]}},\"_rankingInfo\":{\"nbTypos\":0,\"firstMatchedWord\":2000,\"proximityDistance\":1,\"userScore\":173,\"geoDistance\":0,\"geoPrecision\":1,\"nbExactWords\":2,\"words\":2,\"filters\":1}},{\"author\":\"emcrazyone\",\"comment_text\":\"I do too; I was a lead on Ford SYNC, GM&#x27;s Cadillac CUE, and a slew of other OEM ECU modules.  First let me say that the book is dated.  Example, there is now CAN-FD but they only talk about CAN and Extended CAN in the book.<p>The attacks in the book are low grade attacks just about anyone with just a basic curiosity could probably pull off - like making up a cable.  Ford SYNC, for example, required signed payloads.<p>Infotainment systems, generally speaking, are not even on the same CAN bus as the engine control unit.<p>The book spends an inordinate amount of its pages talking about stuff you can easily google and get much more detailed and more accurate information like LIN, ODB2, etc...<p>Why not talk about CAN arbitration?  The book fails to mention a simple attack vector everyone in automotive knows about.  ArbID on CAN is not only unique to CAN frame but also used to win arbitration.  You can flood a CAN bus with CAN frames using an ArbID of 0x01 or 0x00 to kick off a sort of denial of service attack.<p>The UDS hacks they talk about are not really hacks at all.  They are part of what is known as right to service.  Automotive manufacturers are not allowed to lock out small mom &amp; pop service shops or 3rd party tools.  The really sensitive stuff typically requires what is known as a VIN unlocker.  For example, you can&#x27;t easily change the ODO (odometer) value.  With Ford ECUs, you get a DLL from Ford Motor and a key.  You then take CAN data off the bus, pass it through the provided DLL and along with the key, get back a value that you send back out to &quot;unlock&quot; the ECU to program it.  Why not talk about reverse engineering this?<p>They talk about CANiBUS which is a nice tool but a better one is Vehicle Spy which does the same thing and more.  Chip tuners use this to reverse engineer the CAN signals.<p>In the industry, all these CAN bus signals can be decoded if you have what is called a DBC file.  DBC file is file format used to lookup values to translate into human readable descriptions.  The format is owned by Vector which is another company that makes, over priced, CAN diagnostic and simulation tools that everyone uses.<p>The Ethernet metasploit looks like pie in the sky talk.  Every Ethernet system in a car today is basically infotainment system and benign data like album art, Mirrorlink, and simple data sharing between say a center stack and a cluster.  There&#x27;s nothing there... On top of this, every automotive ethernet is Broad-R-Reach which is Broadcom&#x27;s 2-Wire Ethernet and to tap into it requires expensive demo boards from Broadcom.  It&#x27;s not like you can simply take a 2-wire Ethernet and put into a Linksys switch to see the packets.  More misinformation.<p>The Keypad for the passive entry looks like good material but it, too, looks very dated.<p>sorry to be such a downer but felt after reading through the material they should be called out.. buyer beware.\",\"story_id\":9864243,\"story_title\":\"Car Hacker's Handbook\",\"story_url\":\"http://opengarages.org/handbook/\",\"parent_id\":9867178,\"created_at_i\":1436590919,\"created_at\":\"2015-07-11T05:01:59Z\",\"updated_at\":\"2023-09-07T03:10:52Z\",\"_tags\":[\"comment\",\"author_emcrazyone\",\"story_9864243\"],\"children\":[9869691],\"points\":null,\"objectID\":\"9868594\",\"_highlightResult\":{\"author\":{\"value\":\"emcrazyone\",\"matchLevel\":\"none\",\"matchedWords\":[]},\"comment_text\":{\"value\":\"I do too; I was a lead on Ford SYNC, GM's Cadillac CUE, and a slew of other OEM ECU modules.  First let me say that the book is dated.  Example, there is now CAN-FD but they only talk about CAN and Extended CAN in the book.<p>The attacks in the book are low grade attacks just about anyone with just a basic curiosity could probably pull off - like making up a cable.  Ford SYNC, for example, required signed payloads.<p>Infotainment systems, generally speaking, are not even on the same CAN bus as the engine control unit.<p>The book spends an inordinate amount of its pages talking about stuff you can easily google and get much more detailed and more accurate information like LIN, ODB2, etc...<p>Why not talk about CAN arbitration?  The book fails to mention a simple attack vector everyone in automotive knows about.  ArbID on CAN is not only unique to CAN frame but also used to win arbitration.  You can flood a CAN bus with CAN frames using an ArbID of 0x01 or 0x00 to kick off a sort of denial of service attack.<p>The UDS hacks they talk about are not really hacks at all.  They are part of what is known as right to service.  Automotive manufacturers are not allowed to lock out small mom &amp; pop service shops or 3rd party tools.  The really sensitive stuff typically requires what is known as a VIN unlocker.  For example, you can't easily change the ODO (odometer) value.  With Ford ECUs, you get a DLL from Ford Motor and a key.  You then take CAN data off the bus, pass it through the provided DLL and along with the key, get back a value that you send back out to &quot;unlock&quot; the ECU to program it.  Why not talk about <em>reverse</em> <em>engineering</em> this?<p>They talk about CANiBUS which is a nice tool but a better one is Vehicle Spy which does the same thing and more.  Chip tuners use this to <em>reverse</em> engineer the CAN signals.<p>In the industry, all these CAN bus signals can be decoded if you have what is called a DBC file.  DBC file is file format used to lookup values to translate into human readable descriptions.  The format is owned by Vector which is another company that makes, over priced, CAN diagnostic and simulation tools that everyone uses.<p>The Ethernet metasploit looks like pie in the sky talk.  Every Ethernet system in a car today is basically infotainment system and benign data like album art, Mirrorlink, and simple data sharing between say a center stack and a cluster.  There's nothing there... On top of this, every automotive ethernet is Broad-R-Reach which is Broadcom's 2-Wire Ethernet and to tap into it requires expensive demo boards from Broadcom.  It's not like you can simply take a 2-wire Ethernet and put into a Linksys switch to see the packets.  More misinformation.<p>The Keypad for the passive entry looks like good material but it, too, looks very dated.<p>sorry to be such a downer but felt after reading through the material they should be called out.. buyer beware.\",\"matchLevel\":\"full\",\"fullyHighlighted\":false,\"matchedWords\":[\"reverse\",\"engineering\"]},\"story_title\":{\"value\":\"Car Hacker's Handbook\",\"matchLevel\":\"none\",\"matchedWords\":[]},\"story_url\":{\"value\":\"http://opengarages.org/handbook/\",\"matchLevel\":\"none\",\"matchedWords\":[]}},\"_rankingInfo\":{\"nbTypos\":0,\"firstMatchedWord\":2000,\"proximityDistance\":1,\"userScore\":172,\"geoDistance\":0,\"geoPrecision\":1,\"nbExactWords\":2,\"words\":2,\"filters\":1}},{\"author\":\"kw71\",\"comment_text\":\"Here in the USA the carmakers are bound by law to not act in a way that prevents you from repairing your own car.<p>The article that you cited does not seem to advance the argument in your comment, even though it opens with a story of a company getting sued for actual copyright infringement.  (Ford has not sued the &quot;ForSCAN&quot; team.)<p>The carmakers are bound by law to implement the OBD2 application with an acceptable OBD2 PHY.  They are also bound by law to provide their dealer system for flash-programming and for operations that cannot be carried out using the OBD2 application.  Anyone can obtain a J2534 gateway to use these tools, and anyone can obtain access to these tools.<p>This is necessary to resolve antitrust issues and because a broken car is a potential emissions problem.<p>The carmakers have not stopped thirdparty diagnostic providers from reverse engineering the carmakers&#x27; tools to develop their own tools for sale.  Autoenginuity, Launch X431, Snap-On are examples of companies that do this and who have no connections to the vehicle manufacturer supply chain the way that Bosch, Actia, and Continental do.\",\"story_id\":9864243,\"story_title\":\"Car Hacker's Handbook\",\"story_url\":\"http://opengarages.org/handbook/\",\"parent_id\":9865772,\"created_at_i\":1436556486,\"created_at\":\"2015-07-10T19:28:06Z\",\"updated_at\":\"2023-09-07T03:10:43Z\",\"_tags\":[\"comment\",\"author_kw71\",\"story_9864243\"],\"children\":[9866915],\"points\":null,\"objectID\":\"9866209\",\"_highlightResult\":{\"author\":{\"value\":\"kw71\",\"matchLevel\":\"none\",\"matchedWords\":[]},\"comment_text\":{\"value\":\"Here in the USA the carmakers are bound by law to not act in a way that prevents you from repairing your own car.<p>The article that you cited does not seem to advance the argument in your comment, even though it opens with a story of a company getting sued for actual copyright infringement.  (Ford has not sued the &quot;ForSCAN&quot; team.)<p>The carmakers are bound by law to implement the OBD2 application with an acceptable OBD2 PHY.  They are also bound by law to provide their dealer system for flash-programming and for operations that cannot be carried out using the OBD2 application.  Anyone can obtain a J2534 gateway to use these tools, and anyone can obtain access to these tools.<p>This is necessary to resolve antitrust issues and because a broken car is a potential emissions problem.<p>The carmakers have not stopped thirdparty diagnostic providers from <em>reverse</em> <em>engineering</em> the carmakers' tools to develop their own tools for sale.  Autoenginuity, Launch X431, Snap-On are examples of companies that do this and who have no connections to the vehicle manufacturer supply chain the way that Bosch, Actia, and Continental do.\",\"matchLevel\":\"full\",\"fullyHighlighted\":false,\"matchedWords\":[\"reverse\",\"engineering\"]},\"story_title\":{\"value\":\"Car Hacker's Handbook\",\"matchLevel\":\"none\",\"matchedWords\":[]},\"story_url\":{\"value\":\"http://opengarages.org/handbook/\",\"matchLevel\":\"none\",\"matchedWords\":[]}},\"_rankingInfo\":{\"nbTypos\":0,\"firstMatchedWord\":2000,\"proximityDistance\":1,\"userScore\":171,\"geoDistance\":0,\"geoPrecision\":1,\"nbExactWords\":2,\"words\":2,\"filters\":1}},{\"author\":\"vixsomnis\",\"comment_text\":\"Would proguard be able to prevent (or at least make much more difficult) this kind of reverse engineering?\",\"story_id\":9863515,\"story_title\":\"Reverse Engineering the Subway Android App\",\"story_url\":\"http://randywestergren.com/reverse-engineering-the-subway-android-app/\",\"parent_id\":9863515,\"created_at_i\":1436543106,\"created_at\":\"2015-07-10T15:45:06Z\",\"updated_at\":\"2024-09-19T22:02:11Z\",\"_tags\":[\"comment\",\"author_vixsomnis\",\"story_9863515\"],\"objectID\":\"9864582\",\"_highlightResult\":{\"author\":{\"value\":\"vixsomnis\",\"matchLevel\":\"none\",\"matchedWords\":[]},\"comment_text\":{\"value\":\"Would proguard be able to prevent (or at least make much more difficult) this kind of <em>reverse</em> <em>engineering</em>?\",\"matchLevel\":\"full\",\"fullyHighlighted\":false,\"matchedWords\":[\"reverse\",\"engineering\"]},\"story_title\":{\"value\":\"<em>Reverse</em> <em>Engineering</em> the Subway Android App\",\"matchLevel\":\"full\",\"fullyHighlighted\":false,\"matchedWords\":[\"reverse\",\"engineering\"]},\"story_url\":{\"value\":\"http://randywestergren.com/<em>reverse</em>-<em>engineering</em>-the-subway-android-app/\",\"matchLevel\":\"full\",\"fullyHighlighted\":false,\"matchedWords\":[\"reverse\",\"engineering\"]}},\"_rankingInfo\":{\"nbTypos\":0,\"firstMatchedWord\":2000,\"proximityDistance\":1,\"userScore\":170,\"geoDistance\":0,\"geoPrecision\":1,\"nbExactWords\":2,\"words\":2,\"filters\":1}},{\"author\":\"vezzy-fnord\",\"comment_text\":\"I understand this. I&#x27;m also saying this is done pretty routinely in the video game industry from everything to the most basic pathfinding AI to more complex agents that factor in the terrain, particle effects and other aspects of the environment hardcoded into their processes to exhibit &quot;intelligent&quot; behavior, though typically only within that box. Game modders then engage in plenty of work from reverse engineering to what can realistically even be called AI programming.<p>MC-AIXI seems to be more generic because it factors this out into a reinforcement learner, but this in of itself is hardly a cause for fear and that AGI is coming, as the GP alluded. I&#x27;d wager today&#x27;s AAA titles have some damned complicated heuristics of their own.\",\"story_id\":9863832,\"story_title\":\"Is there a simple algorithm for intelligence?\",\"story_url\":\"http://neuralnetworksanddeeplearning.com/sai.html\",\"parent_id\":9864231,\"created_at_i\":1436540524,\"created_at\":\"2015-07-10T15:02:04Z\",\"updated_at\":\"2024-09-19T22:02:11Z\",\"_tags\":[\"comment\",\"author_vezzy-fnord\",\"story_9863832\"],\"objectID\":\"9864309\",\"_highlightResult\":{\"author\":{\"value\":\"vezzy-fnord\",\"matchLevel\":\"none\",\"matchedWords\":[]},\"comment_text\":{\"value\":\"I understand this. I'm also saying this is done pretty routinely in the video game industry from everything to the most basic pathfinding AI to more complex agents that factor in the terrain, particle effects and other aspects of the environment hardcoded into their processes to exhibit &quot;intelligent&quot; behavior, though typically only within that box. Game modders then engage in plenty of work from <em>reverse</em> <em>engineering</em> to what can realistically even be called AI programming.<p>MC-AIXI seems to be more generic because it factors this out into a reinforcement learner, but this in of itself is hardly a cause for fear and that AGI is coming, as the GP alluded. I'd wager today's AAA titles have some damned complicated heuristics of their own.\",\"matchLevel\":\"full\",\"fullyHighlighted\":false,\"matchedWords\":[\"reverse\",\"engineering\"]},\"story_title\":{\"value\":\"Is there a simple algorithm for intelligence?\",\"matchLevel\":\"none\",\"matchedWords\":[]},\"story_url\":{\"value\":\"http://neuralnetworksanddeeplearning.com/sai.html\",\"matchLevel\":\"none\",\"matchedWords\":[]}},\"_rankingInfo\":{\"nbTypos\":0,\"firstMatchedWord\":2000,\"proximityDistance\":1,\"userScore\":169,\"geoDistance\":0,\"geoPrecision\":1,\"nbExactWords\":2,\"words\":2,\"filters\":1}},{\"author\":\"JupiterMoon\",\"comment_text\":\"Actually have another look at that before you break the law on this yourself.<p>As far as I can see that article says that reverse engineering is legal in the case that: (1) the EULA doesn&#x27;t mention it (I&#x27;ve no idea what Whatsapp EULA says - do you?). (2) it is done for the purpose of interoperability. What is being proposed by the GP is in fact not interoperability but security testing.<p>As I said before I think that the laws on this are stupid. But why worry about this when there is a great FOSS program in the same space?\",\"story_id\":9861793,\"story_title\":\"The Coder Who Encrypted Your Texts\",\"story_url\":\"http://www.wsj.com/articles/moxie-marlinspike-the-coder-who-encrypted-your-texts-1436486274\",\"parent_id\":9862654,\"created_at_i\":1436537509,\"created_at\":\"2015-07-10T14:11:49Z\",\"updated_at\":\"2024-09-19T22:02:11Z\",\"_tags\":[\"comment\",\"author_JupiterMoon\",\"story_9861793\"],\"objectID\":\"9864028\",\"_highlightResult\":{\"author\":{\"value\":\"JupiterMoon\",\"matchLevel\":\"none\",\"matchedWords\":[]},\"comment_text\":{\"value\":\"Actually have another look at that before you break the law on this yourself.<p>As far as I can see that article says that <em>reverse</em> <em>engineering</em> is legal in the case that: (1) the EULA doesn't mention it (I've no idea what Whatsapp EULA says - do you?). (2) it is done for the purpose of interoperability. What is being proposed by the GP is in fact not interoperability but security testing.<p>As I said before I think that the laws on this are stupid. But why worry about this when there is a great FOSS program in the same space?\",\"matchLevel\":\"full\",\"fullyHighlighted\":false,\"matchedWords\":[\"reverse\",\"engineering\"]},\"story_title\":{\"value\":\"The Coder Who Encrypted Your Texts\",\"matchLevel\":\"none\",\"matchedWords\":[]},\"story_url\":{\"value\":\"http://www.wsj.com/articles/moxie-marlinspike-the-coder-who-encrypted-your-texts-1436486274\",\"matchLevel\":\"none\",\"matchedWords\":[]}},\"_rankingInfo\":{\"nbTypos\":0,\"firstMatchedWord\":2000,\"proximityDistance\":1,\"userScore\":168,\"geoDistance\":0,\"geoPrecision\":1,\"nbExactWords\":2,\"words\":2,\"filters\":1}},{\"author\":\"nitrogen\",\"comment_text\":\"According to Wikipedia[0], reverse engineering is generally legal in the US:<p><i>In the United States even if an artifact or process is protected by trade secrets, reverse-engineering the artifact or process is often lawful as long as it has been legitimately obtained.</i><p>[0] <a href=\\\"https:&#x2F;&#x2F;en.wikipedia.org&#x2F;wiki&#x2F;Reverse_engineering#United_States\\\" rel=\\\"nofollow\\\">https:&#x2F;&#x2F;en.wikipedia.org&#x2F;wiki&#x2F;Reverse_engineering#United_Sta...</a>\",\"story_id\":9861793,\"story_title\":\"The Coder Who Encrypted Your Texts\",\"story_url\":\"http://www.wsj.com/articles/moxie-marlinspike-the-coder-who-encrypted-your-texts-1436486274\",\"parent_id\":9862636,\"created_at_i\":1436511836,\"created_at\":\"2015-07-10T07:03:56Z\",\"updated_at\":\"2024-09-19T22:01:59Z\",\"_tags\":[\"comment\",\"author_nitrogen\",\"story_9861793\"],\"children\":[9862753,9862881,9864028],\"objectID\":\"9862654\",\"_highlightResult\":{\"author\":{\"value\":\"nitrogen\",\"matchLevel\":\"none\",\"matchedWords\":[]},\"comment_text\":{\"value\":\"According to Wikipedia[0], <em>reverse</em> <em>engineering</em> is generally legal in the US:<p><i>In the United States even if an artifact or process is protected by trade secrets, <em>reverse</em>-<em>engineering</em> the artifact or process is often lawful as long as it has been legitimately obtained.</i><p>[0] <a href=\\\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Reverse_engineering#United_States\\\" rel=\\\"nofollow\\\">https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/<em>Reverse</em>_<em>engineering</em>#United_Sta...</a>\",\"matchLevel\":\"full\",\"fullyHighlighted\":false,\"matchedWords\":[\"reverse\",\"engineering\"]},\"story_title\":{\"value\":\"The Coder Who Encrypted Your Texts\",\"matchLevel\":\"none\",\"matchedWords\":[]},\"story_url\":{\"value\":\"http://www.wsj.com/articles/moxie-marlinspike-the-coder-who-encrypted-your-texts-1436486274\",\"matchLevel\":\"none\",\"matchedWords\":[]}},\"_rankingInfo\":{\"nbTypos\":0,\"firstMatchedWord\":2000,\"proximityDistance\":1,\"userScore\":167,\"geoDistance\":0,\"geoPrecision\":1,\"nbExactWords\":2,\"words\":2,\"filters\":1}},{\"author\":\"JupiterMoon\",\"comment_text\":\"Many countries have laws against reverse engineering programs. Whilst I think these laws are stupid I would prefer to just use the open source program than mess around with the closed source alternative.\",\"story_id\":9861793,\"story_title\":\"The Coder Who Encrypted Your Texts\",\"story_url\":\"http://www.wsj.com/articles/moxie-marlinspike-the-coder-who-encrypted-your-texts-1436486274\",\"parent_id\":9862464,\"created_at_i\":1436511192,\"created_at\":\"2015-07-10T06:53:12Z\",\"updated_at\":\"2024-09-19T22:01:59Z\",\"_tags\":[\"comment\",\"author_JupiterMoon\",\"story_9861793\"],\"children\":[9862654],\"objectID\":\"9862636\",\"_highlightResult\":{\"author\":{\"value\":\"JupiterMoon\",\"matchLevel\":\"none\",\"matchedWords\":[]},\"comment_text\":{\"value\":\"Many countries have laws against <em>reverse</em> <em>engineering</em> programs. Whilst I think these laws are stupid I would prefer to just use the open source program than mess around with the closed source alternative.\",\"matchLevel\":\"full\",\"fullyHighlighted\":false,\"matchedWords\":[\"reverse\",\"engineering\"]},\"story_title\":{\"value\":\"The Coder Who Encrypted Your Texts\",\"matchLevel\":\"none\",\"matchedWords\":[]},\"story_url\":{\"value\":\"http://www.wsj.com/articles/moxie-marlinspike-the-coder-who-encrypted-your-texts-1436486274\",\"matchLevel\":\"none\",\"matchedWords\":[]}},\"_rankingInfo\":{\"nbTypos\":0,\"firstMatchedWord\":2000,\"proximityDistance\":1,\"userScore\":166,\"geoDistance\":0,\"geoPrecision\":1,\"nbExactWords\":2,\"words\":2,\"filters\":1}},{\"author\":\"david-given\",\"comment_text\":\"The GUI was amazing, and is still one of the easiest-to-use GUIs I&#x27;ve ever encountered, even though there were some major omissions, the main one being total lack of accessibility --- you couldn&#x27;t drive it from the keyboard <i>at all</i>. (Win 3.1, OTOH, had amazingly good accessibility and still beats a lot of modern software.)<p>The underlying OS was not amazing. There were some good parts; the relocatable module system was very elegant, if rather alien by modern standards; the ability to resize nearly all memory areas on the fly (by dragging bars in the GUI!) was great; proper pluggable filesystem modules in the 1980s were a revelation; the built-in BASIC was super fast and reasonably comfortable to program in, even though it didn&#x27;t have structured types (some of the built-in ROM software was written in Basic!)...<p>But the bad parts are bad. The underlying technology is really primitive, being a non-preemptive OS with no memory protection that&#x27;s worth anything, and then it&#x27;s suffered from many years of ad-hoc organic growth... e.g. a lot of the core APIs pass flags in the top 8 bits of addresses, because back then the ARM had a 24 bit address bus. Running on a modern machine with more than 16MB of RAM? Good luck with that. APIs are duplicated everywhere with slight changes. There are lots of undeclared dependencies between modules (including recursively, which isn&#x27;t really supported). Platform independence isn&#x27;t really a thing, except when it was crudely bolted on for some platforms.<p>Plus there are some... questionable... design decisions. My favourite is the big chunk of myserious code in the main system allocator which gropes up the call stack every time you try to allocate memory. Why? It&#x27;s looking for stack frames belonging to the system allocator, so that it can tell whether it&#x27;s being called reentrantly. Why is it doing this? So it can detect whether it&#x27;s being called from an interrupt, at which point it goes through an alternative call path and return memory from a different pool!<p>If anyone&#x27;s interested, a few years back I wrote a proof-of-concept RISC OS kernel reimplementation called R2: <a href=\\\"http:&#x2F;&#x2F;cowlark.com&#x2F;r2&#x2F;index.html\\\" rel=\\\"nofollow\\\">http:&#x2F;&#x2F;cowlark.com&#x2F;r2&#x2F;index.html</a><p>Unlike the real thing, it runs everything in user mode except for a tiny stub to handle SWIs. (There&#x27;s a built in ARM emulator.) It&#x27;s complete enough to run Basic. While reverse engineering the operating system I found out way, <i>way</i> too much about how RISC OS worked inside. <i>shudder</i>\",\"story_id\":9844244,\"story_title\":\"Sophie Wilson, Acorn and the development of ARM\",\"story_url\":\"http://www.linuxvoice.com/sophie-wilson-acorn-and-the-development-of-arm/\",\"parent_id\":9845101,\"created_at_i\":1436277362,\"created_at\":\"2015-07-07T13:56:02Z\",\"updated_at\":\"2023-09-07T03:08:58Z\",\"_tags\":[\"comment\",\"author_david-given\",\"story_9844244\"],\"children\":[9846972],\"points\":null,\"objectID\":\"9845423\",\"_highlightResult\":{\"author\":{\"value\":\"david-given\",\"matchLevel\":\"none\",\"matchedWords\":[]},\"comment_text\":{\"value\":\"The GUI was amazing, and is still one of the easiest-to-use GUIs I've ever encountered, even though there were some major omissions, the main one being total lack of accessibility --- you couldn't drive it from the keyboard <i>at all</i>. (Win 3.1, OTOH, had amazingly good accessibility and still beats a lot of modern software.)<p>The underlying OS was not amazing. There were some good parts; the relocatable module system was very elegant, if rather alien by modern standards; the ability to resize nearly all memory areas on the fly (by dragging bars in the GUI!) was great; proper pluggable filesystem modules in the 1980s were a revelation; the built-in BASIC was super fast and reasonably comfortable to program in, even though it didn't have structured types (some of the built-in ROM software was written in Basic!)...<p>But the bad parts are bad. The underlying technology is really primitive, being a non-preemptive OS with no memory protection that's worth anything, and then it's suffered from many years of ad-hoc organic growth... e.g. a lot of the core APIs pass flags in the top 8 bits of addresses, because back then the ARM had a 24 bit address bus. Running on a modern machine with more than 16MB of RAM? Good luck with that. APIs are duplicated everywhere with slight changes. There are lots of undeclared dependencies between modules (including recursively, which isn't really supported). Platform independence isn't really a thing, except when it was crudely bolted on for some platforms.<p>Plus there are some... questionable... design decisions. My favourite is the big chunk of myserious code in the main system allocator which gropes up the call stack every time you try to allocate memory. Why? It's looking for stack frames belonging to the system allocator, so that it can tell whether it's being called reentrantly. Why is it doing this? So it can detect whether it's being called from an interrupt, at which point it goes through an alternative call path and return memory from a different pool!<p>If anyone's interested, a few years back I wrote a proof-of-concept RISC OS kernel reimplementation called R2: <a href=\\\"http://cowlark.com/r2/index.html\\\" rel=\\\"nofollow\\\">http://cowlark.com/r2/index.html</a><p>Unlike the real thing, it runs everything in user mode except for a tiny stub to handle SWIs. (There's a built in ARM emulator.) It's complete enough to run Basic. While <em>reverse</em> <em>engineering</em> the operating system I found out way, <i>way</i> too much about how RISC OS worked inside. <i>shudder</i>\",\"matchLevel\":\"full\",\"fullyHighlighted\":false,\"matchedWords\":[\"reverse\",\"engineering\"]},\"story_title\":{\"value\":\"Sophie Wilson, Acorn and the development of ARM\",\"matchLevel\":\"none\",\"matchedWords\":[]},\"story_url\":{\"value\":\"http://www.linuxvoice.com/sophie-wilson-acorn-and-the-development-of-arm/\",\"matchLevel\":\"none\",\"matchedWords\":[]}},\"_rankingInfo\":{\"nbTypos\":0,\"firstMatchedWord\":2000,\"proximityDistance\":1,\"userScore\":165,\"geoDistance\":0,\"geoPrecision\":1,\"nbExactWords\":2,\"words\":2,\"filters\":1}},{\"author\":\"ReadingInBed\",\"comment_text\":\"I think we can agree that there are genetic differences between men and women. The issue is reverse engineering them by cultural results is really hard. We can see the start point (DNA) and the measuring point ( For example: Higher IQ), but how we got there is really complex. Do the genetic differences really mean that much? I honestly don&#x27;t know, and I think the key here is to study genetics not a genders achievements or test results. DNA is a lot less biased than the culture and world we have created.\",\"story_id\":9843654,\"story_title\":\"How Can There Still Be a Sex Difference, Even When There Is No Sex Difference?\",\"story_url\":\"https://www.psychologytoday.com/blog/the-how-and-why-sex-differences/201101/how-can-there-still-be-sex-difference-even-when-there-is\",\"parent_id\":9843654,\"created_at_i\":1436270368,\"created_at\":\"2015-07-07T11:59:28Z\",\"updated_at\":\"2023-09-07T03:08:53Z\",\"_tags\":[\"comment\",\"author_ReadingInBed\",\"story_9843654\"],\"points\":null,\"objectID\":\"9844851\",\"_highlightResult\":{\"author\":{\"value\":\"ReadingInBed\",\"matchLevel\":\"none\",\"matchedWords\":[]},\"comment_text\":{\"value\":\"I think we can agree that there are genetic differences between men and women. The issue is <em>reverse</em> <em>engineering</em> them by cultural results is really hard. We can see the start point (DNA) and the measuring point ( For example: Higher IQ), but how we got there is really complex. Do the genetic differences really mean that much? I honestly don't know, and I think the key here is to study genetics not a genders achievements or test results. DNA is a lot less biased than the culture and world we have created.\",\"matchLevel\":\"full\",\"fullyHighlighted\":false,\"matchedWords\":[\"reverse\",\"engineering\"]},\"story_title\":{\"value\":\"How Can There Still Be a Sex Difference, Even When There Is No Sex Difference?\",\"matchLevel\":\"none\",\"matchedWords\":[]},\"story_url\":{\"value\":\"https://www.psychologytoday.com/blog/the-how-and-why-sex-differences/201101/how-can-there-still-be-sex-difference-even-when-there-is\",\"matchLevel\":\"none\",\"matchedWords\":[]}},\"_rankingInfo\":{\"nbTypos\":0,\"firstMatchedWord\":2000,\"proximityDistance\":1,\"userScore\":164,\"geoDistance\":0,\"geoPrecision\":1,\"nbExactWords\":2,\"words\":2,\"filters\":1}},{\"author\":\"learc83\",\"comment_text\":\"In ID4 it&#x27;s implied that our computers are based on technology we&#x27;ve been slowly reverse engineering from the Roswell crash. There&#x27;s also a deleted scene with a throwaway line about how he&#x27;s able to write the virus so quickly.\",\"story_id\":9843065,\"story_title\":\"Porting third-party programs to TempleOS\",\"story_url\":\"http://blog.jwhitham.org/2015/07/porting-third-party-programs-to-templeos.html?\",\"parent_id\":9843399,\"created_at_i\":1436239688,\"created_at\":\"2015-07-07T03:28:08Z\",\"updated_at\":\"2023-09-07T03:08:48Z\",\"_tags\":[\"comment\",\"author_learc83\",\"story_9843065\"],\"children\":[9843729],\"points\":null,\"objectID\":\"9843490\",\"_highlightResult\":{\"author\":{\"value\":\"learc83\",\"matchLevel\":\"none\",\"matchedWords\":[]},\"comment_text\":{\"value\":\"In ID4 it's implied that our computers are based on technology we've been slowly <em>reverse</em> <em>engineering</em> from the Roswell crash. There's also a deleted scene with a throwaway line about how he's able to write the virus so quickly.\",\"matchLevel\":\"full\",\"fullyHighlighted\":false,\"matchedWords\":[\"reverse\",\"engineering\"]},\"story_title\":{\"value\":\"Porting third-party programs to TempleOS\",\"matchLevel\":\"none\",\"matchedWords\":[]},\"story_url\":{\"value\":\"http://blog.jwhitham.org/2015/07/porting-third-party-programs-to-templeos.html?\",\"matchLevel\":\"none\",\"matchedWords\":[]}},\"_rankingInfo\":{\"nbTypos\":0,\"firstMatchedWord\":2000,\"proximityDistance\":1,\"userScore\":163,\"geoDistance\":0,\"geoPrecision\":1,\"nbExactWords\":2,\"words\":2,\"filters\":1}},{\"author\":\"lisag84\",\"comment_text\":\"Company: ISE (Independent Security Evaluators)<p>Location: Baltimore, MD or San Diego, CA<p>Who we are: An elite team of security professionals that use scientific approaches to improve our clients’ overall security posture, protect digital assets, harden existing technologies and secure infrastructures.<p>Who we want: Awesomely creative hackers, both mid-level and senior-level, that are looking to work with like-minded folks and doesn&#x27;t mind a fridge stocked full of goodies, healthy options as well!<p>Where you need to work: Candidates need to be able to commute to our Baltimore, MD or San Diego office. Willing to consider remote employees in the US, if they are super talented!<p>What you need to know to get hired: C and C++ and a strong background in at least two of the following: (1) Applied cryptography, cryptographic algorithm design and review, (2) Network security, protocols, and penetration testing, (3) Application security, secure software development, (4) Software vulnerability analysis, fuzzing, and code coverage analysis, (5) Static and dynamic software reverse engineering.<p>How do you apply: careers@securityevaluators.com\",\"story_id\":9812245,\"story_title\":\"Ask HN: Who is hiring? (July 2015)\",\"parent_id\":9812245,\"created_at_i\":1436193665,\"created_at\":\"2015-07-06T14:41:05Z\",\"updated_at\":\"2024-09-19T21:59:28Z\",\"_tags\":[\"comment\",\"author_lisag84\",\"story_9812245\"],\"objectID\":\"9839417\",\"_highlightResult\":{\"author\":{\"value\":\"lisag84\",\"matchLevel\":\"none\",\"matchedWords\":[]},\"comment_text\":{\"value\":\"Company: ISE (Independent Security Evaluators)<p>Location: Baltimore, MD or San Diego, CA<p>Who we are: An elite team of security professionals that use scientific approaches to improve our clients’ overall security posture, protect digital assets, harden existing technologies and secure infrastructures.<p>Who we want: Awesomely creative hackers, both mid-level and senior-level, that are looking to work with like-minded folks and doesn't mind a fridge stocked full of goodies, healthy options as well!<p>Where you need to work: Candidates need to be able to commute to our Baltimore, MD or San Diego office. Willing to consider remote employees in the US, if they are super talented!<p>What you need to know to get hired: C and C++ and a strong background in at least two of the following: (1) Applied cryptography, cryptographic algorithm design and review, (2) Network security, protocols, and penetration testing, (3) Application security, secure software development, (4) Software vulnerability analysis, fuzzing, and code coverage analysis, (5) Static and dynamic software <em>reverse</em> <em>engineering</em>.<p>How do you apply: careers@securityevaluators.com\",\"matchLevel\":\"full\",\"fullyHighlighted\":false,\"matchedWords\":[\"reverse\",\"engineering\"]},\"story_title\":{\"value\":\"Ask HN: Who is hiring? (July 2015)\",\"matchLevel\":\"none\",\"matchedWords\":[]}},\"_rankingInfo\":{\"nbTypos\":0,\"firstMatchedWord\":2000,\"proximityDistance\":1,\"userScore\":162,\"geoDistance\":0,\"geoPrecision\":1,\"nbExactWords\":2,\"words\":2,\"filters\":1}},{\"author\":\"kazinator\",\"comment_text\":\"Nobody today can offer a proprietary, binary-only program as a &quot;trial demo&quot;, and call it &quot;sharing&quot; with a straight face.<p>Not when you can get an entire operating system loaded with packages that are all freely redistributable, open source.<p>It&#x27;s still done, just not called &quot;shareware&quot;.<p>Ah yes, now I remember; there used to be an etiquette behind using the term &quot;shareware&quot;. Some people believed that the term &quot;shareware&quot; should only be applied to non-expiring, freely redistributable programs. Only thing is, those programs came only in binary form. Some forbade reverse engineering (like unlocking some increased functionality). It&#x27;s still really not sharing; it&#x27;s just dumping a free product on the market. Without bending that that etiquette too much, you could call Internet Explorer shareware.\",\"story_id\":9837353,\"story_title\":\"Ask HN: Is shareware dead?\",\"parent_id\":9837353,\"created_at_i\":1436192125,\"created_at\":\"2015-07-06T14:15:25Z\",\"updated_at\":\"2024-09-19T21:59:28Z\",\"_tags\":[\"comment\",\"author_kazinator\",\"story_9837353\"],\"objectID\":\"9839212\",\"_highlightResult\":{\"author\":{\"value\":\"kazinator\",\"matchLevel\":\"none\",\"matchedWords\":[]},\"comment_text\":{\"value\":\"Nobody today can offer a proprietary, binary-only program as a &quot;trial demo&quot;, and call it &quot;sharing&quot; with a straight face.<p>Not when you can get an entire operating system loaded with packages that are all freely redistributable, open source.<p>It's still done, just not called &quot;shareware&quot;.<p>Ah yes, now I remember; there used to be an etiquette behind using the term &quot;shareware&quot;. Some people believed that the term &quot;shareware&quot; should only be applied to non-expiring, freely redistributable programs. Only thing is, those programs came only in binary form. Some forbade <em>reverse</em> <em>engineering</em> (like unlocking some increased functionality). It's still really not sharing; it's just dumping a free product on the market. Without bending that that etiquette too much, you could call Internet Explorer shareware.\",\"matchLevel\":\"full\",\"fullyHighlighted\":false,\"matchedWords\":[\"reverse\",\"engineering\"]},\"story_title\":{\"value\":\"Ask HN: Is shareware dead?\",\"matchLevel\":\"none\",\"matchedWords\":[]}},\"_rankingInfo\":{\"nbTypos\":0,\"firstMatchedWord\":2000,\"proximityDistance\":1,\"userScore\":161,\"geoDistance\":0,\"geoPrecision\":1,\"nbExactWords\":2,\"words\":2,\"filters\":1}},{\"author\":\"ild\",\"comment_text\":\"&quot;Cracker&quot; has nothing to do with what article describes,or what Stallman, Raymond and others want it to mean. &quot;Cracker&quot; has very technical definition: subclass of <i>hackers</i>, who is involved only in removing of copy protection in commercial software and making keygens; &quot;crackers&quot; are the original creators of the demoscene, because cracks often included visual&#x2F;sound effects; also the industry of reverse engineering has roots in cracking.\",\"story_id\":9822911,\"story_title\":\"The Failed Attempt to Rebrand the Word 'Hacker'\",\"story_url\":\"http://motherboard.vice.com/read/the-failed-attempt-to-rebrand-the-word-hacker?trk_source=homepage-lede\",\"parent_id\":9822911,\"created_at_i\":1435890567,\"created_at\":\"2015-07-03T02:29:27Z\",\"updated_at\":\"2023-09-07T03:07:12Z\",\"_tags\":[\"comment\",\"author_ild\",\"story_9822911\"],\"points\":null,\"objectID\":\"9823064\",\"_highlightResult\":{\"author\":{\"value\":\"ild\",\"matchLevel\":\"none\",\"matchedWords\":[]},\"comment_text\":{\"value\":\"&quot;Cracker&quot; has nothing to do with what article describes,or what Stallman, Raymond and others want it to mean. &quot;Cracker&quot; has very technical definition: subclass of <i>hackers</i>, who is involved only in removing of copy protection in commercial software and making keygens; &quot;crackers&quot; are the original creators of the demoscene, because cracks often included visual/sound effects; also the industry of <em>reverse</em> <em>engineering</em> has roots in cracking.\",\"matchLevel\":\"full\",\"fullyHighlighted\":false,\"matchedWords\":[\"reverse\",\"engineering\"]},\"story_title\":{\"value\":\"The Failed Attempt to Rebrand the Word 'Hacker'\",\"matchLevel\":\"none\",\"matchedWords\":[]},\"story_url\":{\"value\":\"http://motherboard.vice.com/read/the-failed-attempt-to-rebrand-the-word-hacker?trk_source=homepage-lede\",\"matchLevel\":\"none\",\"matchedWords\":[]}},\"_rankingInfo\":{\"nbTypos\":0,\"firstMatchedWord\":2000,\"proximityDistance\":1,\"userScore\":160,\"geoDistance\":0,\"geoPrecision\":1,\"nbExactWords\":2,\"words\":2,\"filters\":1}},{\"author\":\"curbside\",\"comment_text\":\"Take a stab at our little challenge and ensure your CV gets reviewed by our team: curl <a href=\\\"http:&#x2F;&#x2F;challenge.shopcurbside.com\\\" rel=\\\"nofollow\\\">http:&#x2F;&#x2F;challenge.shopcurbside.com</a>\\nCurbside is enabling a new way to shop, built for the era of instant mobile commerce. The Curbside app makes it easy to find, buy and pickup products at nearby stores. Curbside searches realtime local inventory across retailers and uses location-based technologies to alert stores when a customer is arriving for a pickup. Curbside helps consumers quickly get what they need and helps retailers better serve their increasingly mobile centric customers. The Curbside Merchant Console enables alerts to staff as customers arrive to pick up orders and also manages online order workflow.<p>Curbside’s investors include Sutter Hill Ventures, Index Ventures, Jerry Yang’s AME Cloud Ventures, Qualcomm Ventures, Eric Schmidt’s Innovation Endeavors, O’Reilly AlphaTech Ventures, Gil Elbaz &amp; David Waxman’s TenOneTen and Chicago Ventures.<p>Tech Stack: Clojure, Python, Javascript, iOS, Android, Elasticsearch<p>Problem space: mobile commerce, big data, search, machine learning, reverse engineering, distributed systems, location services, user experience.<p><a href=\\\"http:&#x2F;&#x2F;www.shopcurbside.com&#x2F;jobs\\\" rel=\\\"nofollow\\\">http:&#x2F;&#x2F;www.shopcurbside.com&#x2F;jobs</a> • Palo Alto, Ca. • Relocation Available • Sorry at this time we cannot sponsor NEW H-1B’s, but we can transfer existing visa’s and sponsor new E3’s, TN’s, and O-1’s.\",\"story_id\":9812245,\"story_title\":\"Ask HN: Who is hiring? (July 2015)\",\"parent_id\":9812245,\"created_at_i\":1435788859,\"created_at\":\"2015-07-01T22:14:19Z\",\"updated_at\":\"2023-09-07T03:06:33Z\",\"_tags\":[\"comment\",\"author_curbside\",\"story_9812245\"],\"children\":[9815706],\"points\":null,\"objectID\":\"9815613\",\"_highlightResult\":{\"author\":{\"value\":\"curbside\",\"matchLevel\":\"none\",\"matchedWords\":[]},\"comment_text\":{\"value\":\"Take a stab at our little challenge and ensure your CV gets reviewed by our team: curl <a href=\\\"http://challenge.shopcurbside.com\\\" rel=\\\"nofollow\\\">http://challenge.shopcurbside.com</a>\\nCurbside is enabling a new way to shop, built for the era of instant mobile commerce. The Curbside app makes it easy to find, buy and pickup products at nearby stores. Curbside searches realtime local inventory across retailers and uses location-based technologies to alert stores when a customer is arriving for a pickup. Curbside helps consumers quickly get what they need and helps retailers better serve their increasingly mobile centric customers. The Curbside Merchant Console enables alerts to staff as customers arrive to pick up orders and also manages online order workflow.<p>Curbside’s investors include Sutter Hill Ventures, Index Ventures, Jerry Yang’s AME Cloud Ventures, Qualcomm Ventures, Eric Schmidt’s Innovation Endeavors, O’Reilly AlphaTech Ventures, Gil Elbaz &amp; David Waxman’s TenOneTen and Chicago Ventures.<p>Tech Stack: Clojure, Python, Javascript, iOS, Android, Elasticsearch<p>Problem space: mobile commerce, big data, search, machine learning, <em>reverse</em> <em>engineering</em>, distributed systems, location services, user experience.<p><a href=\\\"http://www.shopcurbside.com/jobs\\\" rel=\\\"nofollow\\\">http://www.shopcurbside.com/jobs</a> • Palo Alto, Ca. • Relocation Available • Sorry at this time we cannot sponsor NEW H-1B’s, but we can transfer existing visa’s and sponsor new E3’s, TN’s, and O-1’s.\",\"matchLevel\":\"full\",\"fullyHighlighted\":false,\"matchedWords\":[\"reverse\",\"engineering\"]},\"story_title\":{\"value\":\"Ask HN: Who is hiring? (July 2015)\",\"matchLevel\":\"none\",\"matchedWords\":[]}},\"_rankingInfo\":{\"nbTypos\":0,\"firstMatchedWord\":2000,\"proximityDistance\":1,\"userScore\":159,\"geoDistance\":0,\"geoPrecision\":1,\"nbExactWords\":2,\"words\":2,\"filters\":1}},{\"author\":\"jglauche\",\"comment_text\":\"SEEKING WORK: 3D Printing &amp; Small scale production expert\\nLocation: Germany (flexible for flying in for up to a few weeks to many countries)<p>I&#x27;m a 3D printing expert with 6 years experience in low-cost 3d printers and small scale production. I do 3D part&#x2F;machine&#x2F;robot designs and reverse engineering of physical things, example: \\n<a href=\\\"https:&#x2F;&#x2F;media.ccc.de&#x2F;browse&#x2F;conferences&#x2F;chaoscologne&#x2F;1c2&#x2F;1c2-6667-designing_a_3d_printed_tool_for_medical_use.html#video\\\" rel=\\\"nofollow\\\">https:&#x2F;&#x2F;media.ccc.de&#x2F;browse&#x2F;conferences&#x2F;chaoscologne&#x2F;1c2&#x2F;1c2...</a><p>I also repair, hack, calibrate and build 3D Printers as well as do consulting and teaching.<p>In addition to all of that, I&#x27;m laser safety certified (valid in Germany) and I&#x27;m having programming skills in Ruby.<p>GitHub: <a href=\\\"https:&#x2F;&#x2F;github.com&#x2F;Joaz\\\" rel=\\\"nofollow\\\">https:&#x2F;&#x2F;github.com&#x2F;Joaz</a><p>Contact: webmaster at joaz . de\",\"story_id\":9812246,\"story_title\":\"Ask HN: Freelancer? Seeking freelancer? (July 2015)\",\"parent_id\":9812246,\"created_at_i\":1435778200,\"created_at\":\"2015-07-01T19:16:40Z\",\"updated_at\":\"2023-09-07T03:06:28Z\",\"_tags\":[\"comment\",\"author_jglauche\",\"story_9812246\"],\"points\":null,\"objectID\":\"9814433\",\"_highlightResult\":{\"author\":{\"value\":\"jglauche\",\"matchLevel\":\"none\",\"matchedWords\":[]},\"comment_text\":{\"value\":\"SEEKING WORK: 3D Printing &amp; Small scale production expert\\nLocation: Germany (flexible for flying in for up to a few weeks to many countries)<p>I'm a 3D printing expert with 6 years experience in low-cost 3d printers and small scale production. I do 3D part/machine/robot designs and <em>reverse</em> <em>engineering</em> of physical things, example: \\n<a href=\\\"https://media.ccc.de/browse/conferences/chaoscologne/1c2/1c2-6667-designing_a_3d_printed_tool_for_medical_use.html#video\\\" rel=\\\"nofollow\\\">https://media.ccc.de/browse/conferences/chaoscologne/1c2/1c2...</a><p>I also repair, hack, calibrate and build 3D Printers as well as do consulting and teaching.<p>In addition to all of that, I'm laser safety certified (valid in Germany) and I'm having programming skills in Ruby.<p>GitHub: <a href=\\\"https://github.com/Joaz\\\" rel=\\\"nofollow\\\">https://github.com/Joaz</a><p>Contact: webmaster at joaz . de\",\"matchLevel\":\"full\",\"fullyHighlighted\":false,\"matchedWords\":[\"reverse\",\"engineering\"]},\"story_title\":{\"value\":\"Ask HN: Freelancer? Seeking freelancer? (July 2015)\",\"matchLevel\":\"none\",\"matchedWords\":[]}},\"_rankingInfo\":{\"nbTypos\":0,\"firstMatchedWord\":2000,\"proximityDistance\":1,\"userScore\":158,\"geoDistance\":0,\"geoPrecision\":1,\"nbExactWords\":2,\"words\":2,\"filters\":1}},{\"author\":\"joshjonescs\",\"comment_text\":\"Location: Orange County, CA (Irvine, Santa Ana, Newport, Costa Mesa) or lower Los Angeles, CA<p>Remote: Yes<p>Willing to relocate: Prefer not to relocate, but yes<p>Technologies: Java, JavaScript, HTML, CSS, Go, and MATLAB<p>Resume: <a href=\\\"https:&#x2F;&#x2F;drive.google.com&#x2F;file&#x2F;d&#x2F;0B4laQBRvYmBGQzlocGxCbVg5YXc&#x2F;view\\\" rel=\\\"nofollow\\\">https:&#x2F;&#x2F;drive.google.com&#x2F;file&#x2F;d&#x2F;0B4laQBRvYmBGQzlocGxCbVg5YXc...</a><p>Email: jtjones@uci.edu<p>I recently graduated Magna Cum Laude from UC Irvine with a B.S. in Computer Science. I&#x27;m primarily interested in reverse engineering and visualizing video game statistics. For instance, I reverse engineered League of Legends&#x27; spectator data format and visualized the real-time statistics at <a href=\\\"http:&#x2F;&#x2F;riftwalk.gg\\\" rel=\\\"nofollow\\\">http:&#x2F;&#x2F;riftwalk.gg</a> (now non-operational). However, I&#x27;m open to other opportunities as well.\",\"story_id\":9812247,\"story_title\":\"Ask HN: Who wants to be hired? (July 2015)\",\"parent_id\":9812247,\"created_at_i\":1435763261,\"created_at\":\"2015-07-01T15:07:41Z\",\"updated_at\":\"2023-09-07T03:06:19Z\",\"_tags\":[\"comment\",\"author_joshjonescs\",\"story_9812247\"],\"points\":null,\"objectID\":\"9812296\",\"_highlightResult\":{\"author\":{\"value\":\"joshjonescs\",\"matchLevel\":\"none\",\"matchedWords\":[]},\"comment_text\":{\"value\":\"Location: Orange County, CA (Irvine, Santa Ana, Newport, Costa Mesa) or lower Los Angeles, CA<p>Remote: Yes<p>Willing to relocate: Prefer not to relocate, but yes<p>Technologies: Java, JavaScript, HTML, CSS, Go, and MATLAB<p>Resume: <a href=\\\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/0B4laQBRvYmBGQzlocGxCbVg5YXc/view\\\" rel=\\\"nofollow\\\">https://drive.google.com/file/d/0B4laQBRvYmBGQzlocGxCbVg5YXc...</a><p>Email: jtjones@uci.edu<p>I recently graduated Magna Cum Laude from UC Irvine with a B.S. in Computer Science. I'm primarily interested in <em>reverse</em> <em>engineering</em> and visualizing video game statistics. For instance, I <em>reverse</em> engineered League of Legends' spectator data format and visualized the real-time statistics at <a href=\\\"http://riftwalk.gg\\\" rel=\\\"nofollow\\\">http://riftwalk.gg</a> (now non-operational). However, I'm open to other opportunities as well.\",\"matchLevel\":\"full\",\"fullyHighlighted\":false,\"matchedWords\":[\"reverse\",\"engineering\"]},\"story_title\":{\"value\":\"Ask HN: Who wants to be hired? (July 2015)\",\"matchLevel\":\"none\",\"matchedWords\":[]}},\"_rankingInfo\":{\"nbTypos\":0,\"firstMatchedWord\":2000,\"proximityDistance\":1,\"userScore\":157,\"geoDistance\":0,\"geoPrecision\":1,\"nbExactWords\":2,\"words\":2,\"filters\":1}},{\"author\":\"Mandatum\",\"comment_text\":\"Having looked at just the raw data from OBDII on a few vehicles; data format standardization there is none. Not between car makes, models, years or versions (ie 2014 Suzuki Swift vs 2014 Suzuki Swift S) - this would require either months (or years) of reverse-engineering, or unfettered access to auto maker&#x27;s internal documentation (for some I know it&#x27;s minimal). (Likely require partnership with the auto industry to avoid litigation.)<p>If you pulled that together and offered it in a usable format.. Wow.\",\"story_id\":9799007,\"story_title\":\"Ask HN: What problem in your industry is a potential startup?\",\"parent_id\":9799734,\"created_at_i\":1435618280,\"created_at\":\"2015-06-29T22:51:20Z\",\"updated_at\":\"2023-09-07T03:05:25Z\",\"_tags\":[\"comment\",\"author_Mandatum\",\"story_9799007\"],\"children\":[9802098],\"points\":null,\"objectID\":\"9801692\",\"_highlightResult\":{\"author\":{\"value\":\"Mandatum\",\"matchLevel\":\"none\",\"matchedWords\":[]},\"comment_text\":{\"value\":\"Having looked at just the raw data from OBDII on a few vehicles; data format standardization there is none. Not between car makes, models, years or versions (ie 2014 Suzuki Swift vs 2014 Suzuki Swift S) - this would require either months (or years) of <em>reverse</em>-<em>engineering</em>, or unfettered access to auto maker's internal documentation (for some I know it's minimal). (Likely require partnership with the auto industry to avoid litigation.)<p>If you pulled that together and offered it in a usable format.. Wow.\",\"matchLevel\":\"full\",\"fullyHighlighted\":false,\"matchedWords\":[\"reverse\",\"engineering\"]},\"story_title\":{\"value\":\"Ask HN: What problem in your industry is a potential startup?\",\"matchLevel\":\"none\",\"matchedWords\":[]}},\"_rankingInfo\":{\"nbTypos\":0,\"firstMatchedWord\":2000,\"proximityDistance\":1,\"userScore\":156,\"geoDistance\":0,\"geoPrecision\":1,\"nbExactWords\":2,\"words\":2,\"filters\":1}},{\"author\":\"amirmc\",\"comment_text\":\"Yup, I totally agree with your points. However, I also feel there&#x27;s some &#x27;fun&#x27; factor here too -- and we hoped to appeal to it. By having the entire code base available, it also reduces the need for reverse engineering that other programs might require.<p>We didn&#x27;t really expect the money to be the main motivator. Just a hook to draw attention.\",\"story_id\":9798507,\"story_title\":\"Reviewing the Bitcoin Piñata\",\"story_url\":\"https://mirage.io/blog/bitcoin-pinata-results\",\"parent_id\":9800984,\"created_at_i\":1435613830,\"created_at\":\"2015-06-29T21:37:10Z\",\"updated_at\":\"2023-09-07T03:05:25Z\",\"_tags\":[\"comment\",\"author_amirmc\",\"story_9798507\"],\"points\":null,\"objectID\":\"9801304\",\"_highlightResult\":{\"author\":{\"value\":\"amirmc\",\"matchLevel\":\"none\",\"matchedWords\":[]},\"comment_text\":{\"value\":\"Yup, I totally agree with your points. However, I also feel there's some 'fun' factor here too -- and we hoped to appeal to it. By having the entire code base available, it also reduces the need for <em>reverse</em> <em>engineering</em> that other programs might require.<p>We didn't really expect the money to be the main motivator. Just a hook to draw attention.\",\"matchLevel\":\"full\",\"fullyHighlighted\":false,\"matchedWords\":[\"reverse\",\"engineering\"]},\"story_title\":{\"value\":\"Reviewing the Bitcoin Piñata\",\"matchLevel\":\"none\",\"matchedWords\":[]},\"story_url\":{\"value\":\"https://mirage.io/blog/bitcoin-pinata-results\",\"matchLevel\":\"none\",\"matchedWords\":[]}},\"_rankingInfo\":{\"nbTypos\":0,\"firstMatchedWord\":2000,\"proximityDistance\":1,\"userScore\":155,\"geoDistance\":0,\"geoPrecision\":1,\"nbExactWords\":2,\"words\":2,\"filters\":1}},{\"author\":\"RodgerTheGreat\",\"comment_text\":\"Think of it like the security offered by a safe. With time and the correct tools, any obfuscated binary will eventually yield. However, the sheer volume of a complex obfuscated binary (especially one which defeats conventional reverse engineering techniques) can keep security researchers spinning in circles for days or weeks while malware remains at large, or frustrate would-be pirates to the point of giving up.\",\"story_id\":9800011,\"story_title\":\"Computing with traps\",\"story_url\":\"https://www.github.com/jbangert/trapcc\",\"parent_id\":9800420,\"created_at_i\":1435608329,\"created_at\":\"2015-06-29T20:05:29Z\",\"updated_at\":\"2023-09-07T03:05:20Z\",\"_tags\":[\"comment\",\"author_RodgerTheGreat\",\"story_9800011\"],\"points\":null,\"objectID\":\"9800578\",\"_highlightResult\":{\"author\":{\"value\":\"RodgerTheGreat\",\"matchLevel\":\"none\",\"matchedWords\":[]},\"comment_text\":{\"value\":\"Think of it like the security offered by a safe. With time and the correct tools, any obfuscated binary will eventually yield. However, the sheer volume of a complex obfuscated binary (especially one which defeats conventional <em>reverse</em> <em>engineering</em> techniques) can keep security researchers spinning in circles for days or weeks while malware remains at large, or frustrate would-be pirates to the point of giving up.\",\"matchLevel\":\"full\",\"fullyHighlighted\":false,\"matchedWords\":[\"reverse\",\"engineering\"]},\"story_title\":{\"value\":\"Computing with traps\",\"matchLevel\":\"none\",\"matchedWords\":[]},\"story_url\":{\"value\":\"https://www.github.com/jbangert/trapcc\",\"matchLevel\":\"none\",\"matchedWords\":[]}},\"_rankingInfo\":{\"nbTypos\":0,\"firstMatchedWord\":2000,\"proximityDistance\":1,\"userScore\":154,\"geoDistance\":0,\"geoPrecision\":1,\"nbExactWords\":2,\"words\":2,\"filters\":1}},{\"author\":\"makomk\",\"comment_text\":\"As far as I know it&#x27;s still true. Even if you&#x27;re using those open source graphics drivers you still need the binary GPU blob in order to actually boot the Pi. The Pi&#x27;s initial bootloader has to run on the VPU and those open source drivers don&#x27;t - indeed, they&#x27;re from a related chip that doesn&#x27;t even have a VPU. Last time I looked there was no documentation for the VPU, no open-source version of the bootloader code, no compiler that targets the VPU, and the only assembler was based on unofficial reverse engineering.\",\"story_id\":9797985,\"story_title\":\"Own-Mailbox, the first 100% confidential mailbox\",\"story_url\":\"http://www.own-mailbox.com/\",\"parent_id\":9799515,\"created_at_i\":1435602568,\"created_at\":\"2015-06-29T18:29:28Z\",\"updated_at\":\"2023-09-07T03:05:15Z\",\"_tags\":[\"comment\",\"author_makomk\",\"story_9797985\"],\"points\":null,\"objectID\":\"9799938\",\"_highlightResult\":{\"author\":{\"value\":\"makomk\",\"matchLevel\":\"none\",\"matchedWords\":[]},\"comment_text\":{\"value\":\"As far as I know it's still true. Even if you're using those open source graphics drivers you still need the binary GPU blob in order to actually boot the Pi. The Pi's initial bootloader has to run on the VPU and those open source drivers don't - indeed, they're from a related chip that doesn't even have a VPU. Last time I looked there was no documentation for the VPU, no open-source version of the bootloader code, no compiler that targets the VPU, and the only assembler was based on unofficial <em>reverse</em> <em>engineering</em>.\",\"matchLevel\":\"full\",\"fullyHighlighted\":false,\"matchedWords\":[\"reverse\",\"engineering\"]},\"story_title\":{\"value\":\"Own-Mailbox, the first 100% confidential mailbox\",\"matchLevel\":\"none\",\"matchedWords\":[]},\"story_url\":{\"value\":\"http://www.own-mailbox.com/\",\"matchLevel\":\"none\",\"matchedWords\":[]}},\"_rankingInfo\":{\"nbTypos\":0,\"firstMatchedWord\":2000,\"proximityDistance\":1,\"userScore\":153,\"geoDistance\":0,\"geoPrecision\":1,\"nbExactWords\":2,\"words\":2,\"filters\":1}},{\"author\":\"agumonkey\",\"comment_text\":\"Few things animals did that had me sitting:<p># Instant group work, two monkeys having to analyse a situation to pull ropes (all rigged by researchers of course) with the right choreography to free some food. One came, saw that pulling either end alone wouldn&#x27;t cut it, call his buddy, they look at each other and the rig for a second, then split each to its own end, synchronize then pull, profit. The &#x27;no brainer&#x27; attiude killed me.<p># Crows &#x27;reverse engineering&#x27; and social hacking: <a href=\\\"https:&#x2F;&#x2F;www.google.com&#x2F;?gws_rd=ssl#q=ted+talk+crows\\\" rel=\\\"nofollow\\\">https:&#x2F;&#x2F;www.google.com&#x2F;?gws_rd=ssl#q=ted+talk+crows</a><p># Pity bargaining, a puppy lab trying to guilt trip us into letting her go outside after being reprimanded<p># An octopuss mother heating its eggs until near death, saving her last forces to swim as far away as possible so her dead body wouldn&#x27;t attract predators. The sacrifice got me emotional.<p># The &#x27;lion hugging its savior&#x27; video on youtube. That lion looked overwhelmed... More human than a Disney antropomorphic lion character. Just thinking about it warms my heart.<p>None of this is abstract enough for us to stop thinking we&#x27;re not above. I&#x27;ll agree that I&#x27;ve never seen an animal contemplating the world the way humans do. But maybe there&#x27;s no point in doing it and we&#x27;re just full of ourselves :)\",\"story_id\":9796140,\"story_title\":\"Are killer whales persons?\",\"story_url\":\"http://www.salon.com/2015/06/27/are_killer_whales_persons_the_more_we_learn_about_orcas_the_more_our_assumption_of_innate_superiority_looks_like_a_presumption/\",\"parent_id\":9796627,\"created_at_i\":1435560033,\"created_at\":\"2015-06-29T06:40:33Z\",\"updated_at\":\"2023-09-07T03:04:59Z\",\"_tags\":[\"comment\",\"author_agumonkey\",\"story_9796140\"],\"points\":null,\"objectID\":\"9796696\",\"_highlightResult\":{\"author\":{\"value\":\"agumonkey\",\"matchLevel\":\"none\",\"matchedWords\":[]},\"comment_text\":{\"value\":\"Few things animals did that had me sitting:<p># Instant group work, two monkeys having to analyse a situation to pull ropes (all rigged by researchers of course) with the right choreography to free some food. One came, saw that pulling either end alone wouldn't cut it, call his buddy, they look at each other and the rig for a second, then split each to its own end, synchronize then pull, profit. The 'no brainer' attiude killed me.<p># Crows '<em>reverse</em> <em>engineering</em>' and social hacking: <a href=\\\"https://www.google.com/?gws_rd=ssl#q=ted+talk+crows\\\" rel=\\\"nofollow\\\">https://www.google.com/?gws_rd=ssl#q=ted+talk+crows</a><p># Pity bargaining, a puppy lab trying to guilt trip us into letting her go outside after being reprimanded<p># An octopuss mother heating its eggs until near death, saving her last forces to swim as far away as possible so her dead body wouldn't attract predators. The sacrifice got me emotional.<p># The 'lion hugging its savior' video on youtube. That lion looked overwhelmed... More human than a Disney antropomorphic lion character. Just thinking about it warms my heart.<p>None of this is abstract enough for us to stop thinking we're not above. I'll agree that I've never seen an animal contemplating the world the way humans do. But maybe there's no point in doing it and we're just full of ourselves :)\",\"matchLevel\":\"full\",\"fullyHighlighted\":false,\"matchedWords\":[\"reverse\",\"engineering\"]},\"story_title\":{\"value\":\"Are killer whales persons?\",\"matchLevel\":\"none\",\"matchedWords\":[]},\"story_url\":{\"value\":\"http://www.salon.com/2015/06/27/are_killer_whales_persons_the_more_we_learn_about_orcas_the_more_our_assumption_of_innate_superiority_looks_like_a_presumption/\",\"matchLevel\":\"none\",\"matchedWords\":[]}},\"_rankingInfo\":{\"nbTypos\":0,\"firstMatchedWord\":2000,\"proximityDistance\":1,\"userScore\":152,\"geoDistance\":0,\"geoPrecision\":1,\"nbExactWords\":2,\"words\":2,\"filters\":1}},{\"author\":\"paulkon\",\"comment_text\":\"This was awesome! Any recommended introductory books for assembly programming and reverse engineering to appreciate this hack even more?\",\"story_id\":9792649,\"story_title\":\"Hacking Super Mario World to Warp to the Credits [video]\",\"story_url\":\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=vAHXK2wut_I\",\"parent_id\":9792649,\"created_at_i\":1435479803,\"created_at\":\"2015-06-28T08:23:23Z\",\"updated_at\":\"2023-09-07T03:04:40Z\",\"_tags\":[\"comment\",\"author_paulkon\",\"story_9792649\"],\"children\":[9792958,9793010],\"points\":null,\"objectID\":\"9792858\",\"_highlightResult\":{\"author\":{\"value\":\"paulkon\",\"matchLevel\":\"none\",\"matchedWords\":[]},\"comment_text\":{\"value\":\"This was awesome! Any recommended introductory books for assembly programming and <em>reverse</em> <em>engineering</em> to appreciate this hack even more?\",\"matchLevel\":\"full\",\"fullyHighlighted\":false,\"matchedWords\":[\"reverse\",\"engineering\"]},\"story_title\":{\"value\":\"Hacking Super Mario World to Warp to the Credits [video]\",\"matchLevel\":\"none\",\"matchedWords\":[]},\"story_url\":{\"value\":\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=vAHXK2wut_I\",\"matchLevel\":\"none\",\"matchedWords\":[]}},\"_rankingInfo\":{\"nbTypos\":0,\"firstMatchedWord\":2000,\"proximityDistance\":1,\"userScore\":151,\"geoDistance\":0,\"geoPrecision\":1,\"nbExactWords\":2,\"words\":2,\"filters\":1}},{\"author\":\"nickysielicki\",\"comment_text\":\"From the title and the context of this post being on HN, I expected to see someone reverse engineering Super Mario 64 or Super Mario Bros and using that in the context of UE4.<p>But this is really just a typical game mod. Someone made some models for Mario and coins and put them in various UE4 tech demo scenes.<p>That&#x27;s not to say it isn&#x27;t cool. it just isn&#x27;t extremely interesting from a technical perspective, besides the amazingness of UE4 in general.\",\"story_id\":9791997,\"story_title\":\"Mario running in Unreal Engine 4 [video]\",\"story_url\":\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=VUKcSiAPJoQ\",\"parent_id\":9791997,\"created_at_i\":1435457925,\"created_at\":\"2015-06-28T02:18:45Z\",\"updated_at\":\"2023-09-07T03:04:40Z\",\"_tags\":[\"comment\",\"author_nickysielicki\",\"story_9791997\"],\"children\":[9792303],\"points\":null,\"objectID\":\"9792207\",\"_highlightResult\":{\"author\":{\"value\":\"nickysielicki\",\"matchLevel\":\"none\",\"matchedWords\":[]},\"comment_text\":{\"value\":\"From the title and the context of this post being on HN, I expected to see someone <em>reverse</em> <em>engineering</em> Super Mario 64 or Super Mario Bros and using that in the context of UE4.<p>But this is really just a typical game mod. Someone made some models for Mario and coins and put them in various UE4 tech demo scenes.<p>That's not to say it isn't cool. it just isn't extremely interesting from a technical perspective, besides the amazingness of UE4 in general.\",\"matchLevel\":\"full\",\"fullyHighlighted\":false,\"matchedWords\":[\"reverse\",\"engineering\"]},\"story_title\":{\"value\":\"Mario running in Unreal Engine 4 [video]\",\"matchLevel\":\"none\",\"matchedWords\":[]},\"story_url\":{\"value\":\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=VUKcSiAPJoQ\",\"matchLevel\":\"none\",\"matchedWords\":[]}},\"_rankingInfo\":{\"nbTypos\":0,\"firstMatchedWord\":2000,\"proximityDistance\":1,\"userScore\":150,\"geoDistance\":0,\"geoPrecision\":1,\"nbExactWords\":2,\"words\":2,\"filters\":1}}],\"nbHits\":16680,\"page\":1,\"nbPages\":34,\"hitsPerPage\":30,\"exhaustiveNbHits\":false,\"exhaustiveTypo\":false,\"exhaustive\":{\"nbHits\":false,\"typo\":false},\"query\":\"reverse engineering\",\"params\":\"query=reverse+engineering&analyticsTags=%5B%22web%22%5D&page=1&hitsPerPage=30&minWordSizefor1Typo=4&minWordSizefor2Typos=8&advancedSyntax=true&ignorePlurals=false&clickAnalytics=true&minProximity=1&numericFilters=%5B%5D&tagFilters=%5B%5B%22comment%22%5D%2C%5B%5D%5D&typoTolerance=true&queryType=prefixNone&restrictSearchableAttributes=%5B%22title%22%2C%22comment_text%22%2C%22url%22%2C%22story_text%22%2C%22author%22%5D&getRankingInfo=true\",\"queryID\":\"4b91941c565b199acb9aea321f5eb6de\",\"serverUsed\":\"t5-eu-1.algolia.net\",\"indexUsed\":\"Item_dev\",\"parsedQuery\":\"reverse engineering\",\"timeoutCounts\":false,\"timeoutHits\":false,\"processingTimeMS\":48,\"processingTimingsMS\":{\"_request\":{\"roundTrip\":21},\