     "content": {
      "size": 106475,
      "mimeType": "application/json",
      "compression": 0,
      "text": "{\"author\":\"cjd8\",\"children\":[{\"author\":\"mdspan\",\"children\":[{\"author\":\"degamad\",\"children\":[],\"created_at\":\"2026-10-19T08:01:59.000Z\",\"created_at_i\":1792396919,\"id\":50149621,\"options\":[],\"parent_id\":50149603,\"points\":null,\"story_id\":50149392,\"text\":\"Reading the description, looks like a stricter chroot for purely-network applications, maybe?\",\"title\":null,\"type\":\"comment\",\"url\":null},{\"author\":\"rustyhancock\",\"children\":[],\"created_at\":\"2026-10-19T08:06:08.000Z\",\"created_at_i\":1792397168,\"id\":50149643,\"options\":[],\"parent_id\":50149603,\"points\":null,\"story_id\":50149392,\"text\":\"It looks like it&#x27;s useful for sandboxing by preventing any possible filesystem movement.<p><a href=\\\"https:&#x2F;&#x2F;docs.kernel.org&#x2F;next&#x2F;filesystems&#x2F;failfs.html\\\" rel=\\\"nofollow\\\">https:&#x2F;&#x2F;docs.kernel.org&#x2F;next&#x2F;filesystems&#x2F;failfs.html</a>\",\"title\":null,\"type\":\"comment\",\"url\":null},{\"author\":\"rwmj\",\"children\":[{\"author\":\"shevy-java\",\"children\":[{\"author\":\"rwmj\",\"children\":[],\"created_at\":\"2026-10-19T08:26:31.000Z\",\"created_at_i\":1792398391,\"id\":50149790,\"options\":[],\"parent_id\":50149757,\"points\":null,\"story_id\":50149392,\"text\":\"We wanted capabilities[1].  Instead we get this over-complicated series of system calls with no design consistency that you have to use very carefully, else they can be bypassed (see the list of caveats on the failfs page).<p>[1] <a href=\\\"https:&#x2F;&#x2F;en.wikipedia.org&#x2F;wiki&#x2F;Capability-based_security\\\" rel=\\\"nofollow\\\">https:&#x2F;&#x2F;en.wikipedia.org&#x2F;wiki&#x2F;Capability-based_security</a>\",\"title\":null,\"type\":\"comment\",\"url\":null}],\"created_at\":\"2026-10-19T08:22:00.000Z\",\"created_at_i\":1792398120,\"id\":50149757,\"options\":[],\"parent_id\":50149675,\"points\":null,\"story_id\":50149392,\"text\":\"So basically more fine tuned jail controls.<p>I&#x27;d love if chrooting would become easier. In particular a \\nrequirement to have bash or a specific shell is annoying;\\nimo a simple REPL should suffice already there. I wrote one\\nthat works years ago, need to look into what is all needed\\nfor a chroot (I always used bash for this but I&#x27;d also want\\nto change to my own shell eventually).\",\"title\":null,\"type\":\"comment\",\"url\":null},{\"author\":\"nesarkvechnep\",\"children\":[],\"created_at\":\"2026-10-19T12:18:14.000Z\",\"created_at_i\":1792412294,\"id\":50151491,\"options\":[],\"parent_id\":50149675,\"points\":null,\"story_id\":50149392,\"text\":\"They\\u2019ll do everything but adopt Capsicum. Shame.\",\"title\":null,\"type\":\"comment\",\"url\":null}],\"created_at\":\"2026-10-19T08:10:10.000Z\",\"created_at_i\":1792397410,\"id\":50149675,\"options\":[],\"parent_id\":50149603,\"points\":null,\"story_id\":50149392,\"text\":\"For sandboxing: <a href=\\\"https:&#x2F;&#x2F;hackaday.com&#x2F;2026&#x2F;08&#x2F;07&#x2F;this-filesystem-is-born-to-fail&#x2F;\\\" rel=\\\"nofollow\\\">https:&#x2F;&#x2F;hackaday.com&#x2F;2026&#x2F;08&#x2F;07&#x2F;this-filesystem-is-born-to-f...</a><p>A process which is chrooted into failfs cannot open new files.  It is limited to the set of filesystem file descriptors it already has open (presumably set up before the chroot or inherited into the process from some container runtime).\",\"title\":null,\"type\":\"comment\",\"url\":null},{\"author\":\"saint-evan\",\"children\":[{\"author\":\"wpyoga\",\"children\":[{\"author\":\"saint-evan\",\"children\":[{\"author\":\"ffsm8\",\"children\":[],\"created_at\":\"2026-10-19T10:13:18.000Z\",\"created_at_i\":1792404798,\"id\":50150530,\"options\":[],\"parent_id\":50149917,\"points\":null,\"story_id\":50149392,\"text\":\"You explicitly stated as such, I just want to emphasize it again: it only breaks <i>dynamically linked libraries</i>, which - from a stability point of view - is an antipattern anyway.<p>Almost always youre better off producing a bundle which has everything (excluding the runtime) included... Or just use better languages which categorically don&#x27;t even have this scenario, eg golang, Java, rust, etc pp\",\"title\":null,\"type\":\"comment\",\"url\":null}],\"created_at\":\"2026-10-19T08:41:32.000Z\",\"created_at_i\":1792399292,\"id\":50149917,\"options\":[],\"parent_id\":50149840,\"points\":null,\"story_id\":50149392,\"text\":\"That&#x27;s a fun idea but take python (very familiar)... It&#x27;s very deeply dynamic and even if yu run imports at the top of your code yu still have many large libs with lazy code execution baked deep inside their internal functions so that assumes yu actually know what code paths the application will hit later. More importantly this goes in the opposite direction of Python&#x27;s lazy imports thingy.. basically you&#x27;ve got a bomb and yu wouldn&#x27;t wanna do that. Also, imagine the startup time for large apps. I&#x27;d just put a statically-linked binary in front and proxy back to the dynamically-linked process if I can&#x27;t rewrite the main app in something like Go or Rust.\",\"title\":null,\"type\":\"comment\",\"url\":null}],\"created_at\":\"2026-10-19T08:33:20.000Z\",\"created_at_i\":1792398800,\"id\":50149840,\"options\":[],\"parent_id\":50149755,\"points\":null,\"story_id\":50149392,\"text\":\"Is it possible to load the dynamic libraries before fork()ing and somehow prevent the path lookup and library loading? This will open up new possibilities for hardened security models.\",\"title\":null,\"type\":\"comment\",\"url\":null},{\"author\":\"pandemic_region\",\"children\":[{\"author\":\"saint-evan\",\"children\":[{\"author\":\"pandemic_region\",\"children\":[{\"author\":\"asimovDev\",\"children\":[],\"created_at\":\"2026-10-19T10:04:49.000Z\",\"created_at_i\":1792404289,\"id\":50150471,\"options\":[],\"parent_id\":50150117,\"points\":null,\"story_id\":50149392,\"text\":\"I immediately parsed it as some CLI i don&#x27;t know about as well\",\"title\":null,\"type\":\"comment\",\"url\":null}],\"created_at\":\"2026-10-19T09:08:06.000Z\",\"created_at_i\":1792400886,\"id\":50150117,\"options\":[],\"parent_id\":50149945,\"points\":null,\"story_id\":50149392,\"text\":\"Thanks, at first I thought it was the name of another tool or library ;)\",\"title\":null,\"type\":\"comment\",\"url\":null}],\"created_at\":\"2026-10-19T08:45:19.000Z\",\"created_at_i\":1792399519,\"id\":50149945,\"options\":[],\"parent_id\":50149937,\"points\":null,\"story_id\":50149392,\"text\":\"no biggie... I just like how &#x27;yu&#x27; looks. It&#x27;s an aesthetic.\",\"title\":null,\"type\":\"comment\",\"url\":null}],\"created_at\":\"2026-10-19T08:44:00.000Z\",\"created_at_i\":1792399440,\"id\":50149937,\"options\":[],\"parent_id\":50149755,\"points\":null,\"story_id\":50149392,\"text\":\"Sorry man I&#x27;ve been out of the loop with the hip crowd for a good while, but is &quot;yu&quot; now a thing like &quot;thru&quot; once was?\",\"title\":null,\"type\":\"comment\",\"url\":null},{\"author\":\"jack_pp\",\"children\":[{\"author\":\"Nexxxeh\",\"children\":[],\"created_at\":\"2026-10-19T10:26:52.000Z\",\"created_at_i\":1792405612,\"id\":50150625,\"options\":[],\"parent_id\":50149983,\"points\":null,\"story_id\":50149392,\"text\":\"Yeah, it feels &quot;not ND friendly&quot; too. Totally breaks my reading flow.<p>I am the person addressed when someone says &quot;you&quot;, not &quot;yu&quot;. Why is that person changing how I&#x27;m addressed?<p>It&#x27;s also confusing because it feels initially like it&#x27;s some new Linux command or user, like &quot;su&quot;.\",\"title\":null,\"type\":\"comment\",\"url\":null},{\"author\":\"tgv\",\"children\":[],\"created_at\":\"2026-10-19T10:37:29.000Z\",\"created_at_i\":1792406249,\"id\":50150699,\"options\":[],\"parent_id\":50149983,\"points\":null,\"story_id\":50149392,\"text\":\"Lil&#x27; TAFKAP, the modern version of Saint Exupery.\",\"title\":null,\"type\":\"comment\",\"url\":null},{\"author\":\"matsemann\",\"children\":[],\"created_at\":\"2026-10-19T13:17:29.000Z\",\"created_at_i\":1792415849,\"id\":50152080,\"options\":[],\"parent_id\":50149983,\"points\":null,\"story_id\":50149392,\"text\":\"ah, first sentence I thought yu was a typo. Second sentence I saw it I thought it might be a linux component of sorts. So it&#x27;s not just a stylistic choice, it&#x27;s actually harming my comprehension of what they&#x27;re writing.\",\"title\":null,\"type\":\"comment\",\"url\":null}],\"created_at\":\"2026-10-19T08:49:51.000Z\",\"created_at_i\":1792399791,\"id\":50149983,\"options\":[],\"parent_id\":50149755,\"points\":null,\"story_id\":50149392,\"text\":\"idk if it&#x27;s just me but deliberately using &quot;yu&quot; instead of &quot;you&quot; is annoying and makes me not really take whatever you say seriously\",\"title\":null,\"type\":\"comment\",\"url\":null},{\"author\":\"dhx\",\"children\":[],\"created_at\":\"2026-10-19T10:45:30.000Z\",\"created_at_i\":1792406730,\"id\":50150753,\"options\":[],\"parent_id\":50149755,\"points\":null,\"story_id\":50149392,\"text\":\"Is it different from using seccomp with SECCOMP_RET_ERRNO and return code of EOPNOTSUPP for all filesystem syscalls?\",\"title\":null,\"type\":\"comment\",\"url\":null},{\"author\":\"yonatan8070\",\"children\":[],\"created_at\":\"2026-10-19T17:00:46.000Z\",\"created_at_i\":1792429246,\"id\":50154844,\"options\":[],\"parent_id\":50149755,\"points\":null,\"story_id\":50149392,\"text\":\"How is that different from putting an app in a chroot jail or container?\",\"title\":null,\"type\":\"comment\",\"url\":null}],\"created_at\":\"2026-10-19T08:21:49.000Z\",\"created_at_i\":1792398109,\"id\":50149755,\"options\":[],\"parent_id\":50149603,\"points\":null,\"story_id\":50149392,\"text\":\"Failfs is a pretty cool pseudo-filesystem where every path lookup fails. So if yu place a statically-linked process into failfs with ONLY pre-allocated file-descriptors at process birth, close down unneeded sockets and enforce a default-deny firewall discipline, compromised applications are effectively isolated and unable to access local files or spawn shells during an exploit. An attacker can NOT move adjacently from the exploited process to other parts of the system because to do so they&#x27;ll need to navigate from root and that would fail since yu set root as failfs and you&#x27;ve got yourself a pure, isolated data-processing pipe that can only interact via the exact file descriptors it was given!!<p>Unfortunately, yu can&#x27;t use dynamically-linked apps anyway since failfs would break absolute path resolution loading system libraries.<p>EDIT::\\nBut I guess yu could put a statically linked app on the socket, wrap it in failfs and gave it proxy requests to a dynamically-linked app process if your app must use something like python.\",\"title\":null,\"type\":\"comment\",\"url\":null},{\"author\":\"zero_k\",\"children\":[{\"author\":\"rwmj\",\"children\":[{\"author\":\"tosti\",\"children\":[],\"created_at\":\"2026-10-19T13:05:17.000Z\",\"created_at_i\":1792415117,\"id\":50151964,\"options\":[],\"parent_id\":50150303,\"points\":null,\"story_id\":50149392,\"text\":\"Such a filesystem could be done with FUSE. Devices can be done by Linux itself: <a href=\\\"https:&#x2F;&#x2F;docs.kernel.org&#x2F;admin-guide&#x2F;device-mapper&#x2F;dm-flakey.html\\\" rel=\\\"nofollow\\\">https:&#x2F;&#x2F;docs.kernel.org&#x2F;admin-guide&#x2F;device-mapper&#x2F;dm-flakey....</a>\",\"title\":null,\"type\":\"comment\",\"url\":null}],\"created_at\":\"2026-10-19T09:39:01.000Z\",\"created_at_i\":1792402741,\"id\":50150303,\"options\":[],\"parent_id\":50150022,\"points\":null,\"story_id\":50149392,\"text\":\"Devices rather than filesystems, but .. <a href=\\\"https:&#x2F;&#x2F;libguestfs.org&#x2F;nbdkit-error-filter.1.html\\\" rel=\\\"nofollow\\\">https:&#x2F;&#x2F;libguestfs.org&#x2F;nbdkit-error-filter.1.html</a> &amp; <a href=\\\"https:&#x2F;&#x2F;libguestfs.org&#x2F;nbdkit-evil-filter.1.html\\\" rel=\\\"nofollow\\\">https:&#x2F;&#x2F;libguestfs.org&#x2F;nbdkit-evil-filter.1.html</a>\",\"title\":null,\"type\":\"comment\",\"url\":null}],\"created_at\":\"2026-10-19T08:56:25.000Z\",\"created_at_i\":1792400185,\"id\":50150022,\"options\":[],\"parent_id\":50149603,\"points\":null,\"story_id\":50149392,\"text\":\"I&#x27;d love to have one that randomly fails, so we could debug e.g. databases and other apps that are supposed to be resilient. I&#x27;d call it &quot;havocfs&quot;. Doesn&#x27;t corrupt data, just randomly fails reads&#x2F;writes&#x2F;etc. Some software is supposed to handle that gracefully. Would be a great test tool...\",\"title\":null,\"type\":\"comment\",\"url\":null}],\"created_at\":\"2026-10-19T07:58:35.000Z\",\"created_at_i\":1792396715,\"id\":50149603,\"options\":[],\"parent_id\":50149392,\"points\":null,\"story_id\":50149392,\"text\":\"First time hearing about failfs. Does it have a niche use case or is it just for testing?\",\"title\":null,\"type\":\"comment\",\"url\":null},{\"author\":\"sippingabonedry\",\"children\":[{\"author\":\"tecleandor\",\"children\":[],\"created_at\":\"2026-10-19T08:08:08.000Z\",\"created_at_i\":1792397288,\"id\":50149660,\"options\":[],\"parent_id\":50149639,\"points\":null,\"story_id\":50149392,\"text\":\"~2021: <a href=\\\"https:&#x2F;&#x2F;news.ycombinator.com&#x2F;item?id=28355754\\\">https:&#x2F;&#x2F;news.ycombinator.com&#x2F;item?id=28355754</a>\",\"title\":null,\"type\":\"comment\",\"url\":null},{\"author\":\"u8080\",\"children\":[{\"author\":\"vbezhenar\",\"children\":[{\"author\":\"u8080\",\"children\":[{\"author\":\"speed_spread\",\"children\":[],\"created_at\":\"2026-10-19T13:48:21.000Z\",\"created_at_i\":1792417701,\"id\":50152474,\"options\":[],\"parent_id\":50151909,\"points\":null,\"story_id\":50149392,\"text\":\"If the kernel had a standard ABI, an out-of-tree kernel module would be the way. As it is, DKMS is a pain and so in-tree modules are the easiest where license allows it.\",\"title\":null,\"type\":\"comment\",\"url\":null}],\"created_at\":\"2026-10-19T12:59:38.000Z\",\"created_at_i\":1792414778,\"id\":50151909,\"options\":[],\"parent_id\":50151264,\"points\":null,\"story_id\":50149392,\"text\":\"But it could be DKMS module or something other run as separate project? I got software arch point, but it seems very odd having such things in kernel project, no?\",\"title\":null,\"type\":\"comment\",\"url\":null}],\"created_at\":\"2026-10-19T11:54:03.000Z\",\"created_at_i\":1792410843,\"id\":50151264,\"options\":[],\"parent_id\":50151111,\"points\":null,\"story_id\":50149392,\"text\":\"For performance.\",\"title\":null,\"type\":\"comment\",\"url\":null},{\"author\":\"kasabali\",\"children\":[],\"created_at\":\"2026-10-19T12:24:12.000Z\",\"created_at_i\":1792412652,\"id\":50151540,\"options\":[],\"parent_id\":50151111,\"points\":null,\"story_id\":50149392,\"text\":\"Because Samba is GPLv3\",\"title\":null,\"type\":\"comment\",\"url\":null}],\"created_at\":\"2026-10-19T11:36:37.000Z\",\"created_at_i\":1792409797,\"id\":50151111,\"options\":[],\"parent_id\":50149639,\"points\":null,\"story_id\":50149392,\"text\":\"Btw, never understood why do we need it in kernel as a part of kernel project.\",\"title\":null,\"type\":\"comment\",\"url\":null}],\"created_at\":\"2026-10-19T08:05:01.000Z\",\"created_at_i\":1792397101,\"id\":50149639,\"options\":[],\"parent_id\":50149392,\"points\":null,\"story_id\":50149392,\"text\":\"Linux has an in-kernel SMB server like Solaris? When the hell did this happen?\",\"title\":null,\"type\":\"comment\",\"url\":null},{\"author\":\"sirnicolaz\",\"children\":[{\"author\":\"amelius\",\"children\":[{\"author\":\"Hendrikto\",\"children\":[{\"author\":\"secondcoming\",\"children\":[{\"author\":\"cromka\",\"children\":[{\"author\":\"Sammi\",\"children\":[],\"created_at\":\"2026-10-19T10:45:02.000Z\",\"created_at_i\":1792406702,\"id\":50150747,\"options\":[],\"parent_id\":50150218,\"points\":null,\"story_id\":50149392,\"text\":\"Linux is big business.\",\"title\":null,\"type\":\"comment\",\"url\":null}],\"created_at\":\"2026-10-19T09:22:27.000Z\",\"created_at_i\":1792401747,\"id\":50150218,\"options\":[],\"parent_id\":50150026,\"points\":null,\"story_id\":50149392,\"text\":\"Most, if not all, are employed by different companies or Kernel.org.\",\"title\":null,\"type\":\"comment\",\"url\":null},{\"author\":\"lexicality\",\"children\":[],\"created_at\":\"2026-10-19T09:25:14.000Z\",\"created_at_i\":1792401914,\"id\":50150229,\"options\":[],\"parent_id\":50150026,\"points\":null,\"story_id\":50149392,\"text\":\"It&#x27;s a big ask to expect people to do the thing they volunteered to do and can step back from doing any time they want?\",\"title\":null,\"type\":\"comment\",\"url\":null},{\"author\":\"patadura\",\"children\":[],\"created_at\":\"2026-10-19T09:41:55.000Z\",\"created_at_i\":1792402915,\"id\":50150325,\"options\":[],\"parent_id\":50150026,\"points\":null,\"story_id\":50149392,\"text\":\"Do people still think that Linux is being developed by unpaid hackers in a garage?\",\"title\":null,\"type\":\"comment\",\"url\":null}],\"created_at\":\"2026-10-19T08:56:48.000Z\",\"created_at_i\":1792400208,\"id\":50150026,\"options\":[],\"parent_id\":50149893,\"points\":null,\"story_id\":50149392,\"text\":\"That&#x27;s a big ask for those maintainers who are volunteers\",\"title\":null,\"type\":\"comment\",\"url\":null}],\"created_at\":\"2026-10-19T08:39:22.000Z\",\"created_at_i\":1792399162,\"id\":50149893,\"options\":[],\"parent_id\":50149806,\"points\":null,\"story_id\":50149392,\"text\":\"That\\u2018s what subsystem maintainers are for.\",\"title\":null,\"type\":\"comment\",\"url\":null},{\"author\":\"cjd8\",\"children\":[],\"created_at\":\"2026-10-19T08:43:34.000Z\",\"created_at_i\":1792399414,\"id\":50149932,\"options\":[],\"parent_id\":50149806,\"points\":null,\"story_id\":50149392,\"text\":\"Only if he finds something odd in the pull request summary.\",\"title\":null,\"type\":\"comment\",\"url\":null},{\"author\":\"bakugo\",\"children\":[],\"created_at\":\"2026-10-19T15:02:25.000Z\",\"created_at_i\":1792422145,\"id\":50153350,\"options\":[],\"parent_id\":50149806,\"points\":null,\"story_id\":50149392,\"text\":\"Linus thinks AI is no different from a compiler, so it&#x27;s likely that none of these commits are being reviewed. After all, how often do you doubt your compiler&#x27;s output?\",\"title\":null,\"type\":\"comment\",\"url\":null}],\"created_at\":\"2026-10-19T08:29:26.000Z\",\"created_at_i\":1792398566,\"id\":50149806,\"options\":[],\"parent_id\":50149654,\"points\":null,\"story_id\":50149392,\"text\":\"Is Linus still checking every commit?\",\"title\":null,\"type\":\"comment\",\"url\":null},{\"author\":\"sedan_baklazhan\",\"children\":[{\"author\":\"machomaster\",\"children\":[{\"author\":\"izacus\",\"children\":[{\"author\":\"literallyroy\",\"children\":[],\"created_at\":\"2026-10-19T12:36:40.000Z\",\"created_at_i\":1792413400,\"id\":50151651,\"options\":[],\"parent_id\":50150936,\"points\":null,\"story_id\":50149392,\"text\":\"I think in Linux world commits usually mean something which isn\\u2019t always the case at random company X. A commit being a meaningful change where number of lines doesn\\u2019t matter.\",\"title\":null,\"type\":\"comment\",\"url\":null}],\"created_at\":\"2026-10-19T11:12:07.000Z\",\"created_at_i\":1792408327,\"id\":50150936,\"options\":[],\"parent_id\":50150395,\"points\":null,\"story_id\":50149392,\"text\":\"&gt; 1. They weren&#x27;t counting LOC though, but commits. It&#x27;s a totally different metric.<p>Is this satire? :D\",\"title\":null,\"type\":\"comment\",\"url\":null}],\"created_at\":\"2026-10-19T09:53:01.000Z\",\"created_at_i\":1792403581,\"id\":50150395,\"options\":[],\"parent_id\":50150323,\"points\":null,\"story_id\":50149392,\"text\":\"1. They weren&#x27;t counting LOC though, but commits. It&#x27;s a totally different metric.<p>2. This type of stat was always being shared with each new release, giving a general indication of the size of changes. They did not started posting it all of a sudden due to AI.\",\"title\":null,\"type\":\"comment\",\"url\":null},{\"author\":\"jjice\",\"children\":[],\"created_at\":\"2026-10-19T11:57:53.000Z\",\"created_at_i\":1792411073,\"id\":50151297,\"options\":[],\"parent_id\":50150323,\"points\":null,\"story_id\":50149392,\"text\":\"It felt to me like it&#x27;s more of a neat fact rather than a measure of quality.\",\"title\":null,\"type\":\"comment\",\"url\":null}],\"created_at\":\"2026-10-19T09:41:28.000Z\",\"created_at_i\":1792402888,\"id\":50150323,\"options\":[],\"parent_id\":50149654,\"points\":null,\"story_id\":50149392,\"text\":\"BTW, here we are: LOC metric everyone made fun of has suddenly became commit count metric.<p>Features? Bugfixes? What? Look how many commits we&#x27;ve got!\",\"title\":null,\"type\":\"comment\",\"url\":null}],\"created_at\":\"2026-10-19T08:06:55.000Z\",\"created_at_i\":1792397215,\"id\":50149654,\"options\":[],\"parent_id\":50149392,\"points\":null,\"story_id\":50149392,\"text\":\"&gt; This release is a record-breaker on all fronts; it brought in 18,765 non-merge commits from 3,021 developers, 807 of whom were first-time kernel developers.<p>hopefully without kernel-slops :P good job everyone btw!\",\"title\":null,\"type\":\"comment\",\"url\":null},{\"author\":\"theden\",\"children\":[{\"author\":\"raverbashing\",\"children\":[{\"author\":\"robin_reala\",\"children\":[],\"created_at\":\"2026-10-19T08:23:44.000Z\",\"created_at_i\":1792398224,\"id\":50149767,\"options\":[],\"parent_id\":50149738,\"points\":null,\"story_id\":50149392,\"text\":\"Yep. My household backs up to a Synology NAS via Time Machine: <a href=\\\"https:&#x2F;&#x2F;kb.synology.com&#x2F;en-global&#x2F;DSM&#x2F;tutorial&#x2F;How_to_back_up_files_from_Mac_to_Synology_NAS_with_Time_Machine\\\" rel=\\\"nofollow\\\">https:&#x2F;&#x2F;kb.synology.com&#x2F;en-global&#x2F;DSM&#x2F;tutorial&#x2F;How_to_back_u...</a>\",\"title\":null,\"type\":\"comment\",\"url\":null},{\"author\":\"tonyedgecombe\",\"children\":[{\"author\":\"TheFuzzball\",\"children\":[],\"created_at\":\"2026-10-19T10:31:23.000Z\",\"created_at_i\":1792405883,\"id\":50150659,\"options\":[],\"parent_id\":50149776,\"points\":null,\"story_id\":50149392,\"text\":\"Apple have dropped support for AFP, so SMB is the only game in town.\",\"title\":null,\"type\":\"comment\",\"url\":null},{\"author\":\"alt227\",\"children\":[{\"author\":\"tonyedgecombe\",\"children\":[{\"author\":\"alt227\",\"children\":[{\"author\":\"throw0101a\",\"children\":[{\"author\":\"alt227\",\"children\":[{\"author\":\"hecifato\",\"children\":[{\"author\":\"cormorant\",\"children\":[],\"created_at\":\"2026-10-19T16:15:58.000Z\",\"created_at_i\":1792426558,\"id\":50154233,\"options\":[],\"parent_id\":50153500,\"points\":null,\"story_id\":50149392,\"text\":\"They recognize NAS, still, though for instructions they refer you to the manufacturer: <a href=\\\"https:&#x2F;&#x2F;support.apple.com&#x2F;en-us&#x2F;102423#nas\\\" rel=\\\"nofollow\\\">https:&#x2F;&#x2F;support.apple.com&#x2F;en-us&#x2F;102423#nas</a>\",\"title\":null,\"type\":\"comment\",\"url\":null}],\"created_at\":\"2026-10-19T15:15:58.000Z\",\"created_at_i\":1792422958,\"id\":50153500,\"options\":[],\"parent_id\":50152650,\"points\":null,\"story_id\":50149392,\"text\":\"You\\u2019d think a network drive would be included. Apple used to also provide instructions for connecting a network drive but they stopped with macOS 15 or 26. Now a drive that is physically connected to a Mac is the only method of using Time Machine that Apple officially recognizes.<p>edit: to be clear you can still use a network drive, but how well that works is the big question. It will probably depend on the user and the setup. The Macs in my house backup to a share on a Pi 4. They can still restore files from it just fine.\",\"title\":null,\"type\":\"comment\",\"url\":null},{\"author\":\"cormorant\",\"children\":[],\"created_at\":\"2026-10-19T16:23:32.000Z\",\"created_at_i\":1792427012,\"id\":50154330,\"options\":[],\"parent_id\":50152650,\"points\":null,\"story_id\":50149392,\"text\":\"Thunderbolt external SSD, would be the only Apple-ish thing. (Formerly of course, FireWire.)<p>To be absolutely complete, I could mention that at least one SAS HBA PCIe card exists on the market with support for macOS 27, even.  Sometimes bundled with other products...\",\"title\":null,\"type\":\"comment\",\"url\":null}],\"created_at\":\"2026-10-19T14:02:32.000Z\",\"created_at_i\":1792418552,\"id\":50152650,\"options\":[],\"parent_id\":50151368,\"points\":null,\"story_id\":50149392,\"text\":\"What would an example of &quot;other external storage device&quot; be in this instance?\",\"title\":null,\"type\":\"comment\",\"url\":null}],\"created_at\":\"2026-10-19T12:05:03.000Z\",\"created_at_i\":1792411503,\"id\":50151368,\"options\":[],\"parent_id\":50150990,\"points\":null,\"story_id\":50149392,\"text\":\"&gt; <i>If you have a USB drive or other external storage device, you can use Time Machine to automatically back up your files, including apps, music, photos, email, and documents.</i><p>* <a href=\\\"https:&#x2F;&#x2F;support.apple.com&#x2F;en-ca&#x2F;104984\\\" rel=\\\"nofollow\\\">https:&#x2F;&#x2F;support.apple.com&#x2F;en-ca&#x2F;104984</a><p>The &quot;3-2-1&quot; strategy is even possible if you rotate drives between locations:<p>&gt; <i>For extra security and convenience, you can back up to multiple disks. If you work in two locations, you may want to keep backup disks in both places.</i><p>* <a href=\\\"https:&#x2F;&#x2F;support.apple.com&#x2F;en-ca&#x2F;guide&#x2F;mac-help&#x2F;mh40739&#x2F;mac\\\" rel=\\\"nofollow\\\">https:&#x2F;&#x2F;support.apple.com&#x2F;en-ca&#x2F;guide&#x2F;mac-help&#x2F;mh40739&#x2F;mac</a>\",\"title\":null,\"type\":\"comment\",\"url\":null}],\"created_at\":\"2026-10-19T11:19:27.000Z\",\"created_at_i\":1792408767,\"id\":50150990,\"options\":[],\"parent_id\":50150883,\"points\":null,\"story_id\":50149392,\"text\":\"Is this the recommended way to use time machine then, as opposed to over a network?\",\"title\":null,\"type\":\"comment\",\"url\":null}],\"created_at\":\"2026-10-19T11:04:06.000Z\",\"created_at_i\":1792407846,\"id\":50150883,\"options\":[],\"parent_id\":50150695,\"points\":null,\"story_id\":50149392,\"text\":\"A locally attached drive.\",\"title\":null,\"type\":\"comment\",\"url\":null}],\"created_at\":\"2026-10-19T10:36:45.000Z\",\"created_at_i\":1792406205,\"id\":50150695,\"options\":[],\"parent_id\":50149776,\"points\":null,\"story_id\":50149392,\"text\":\"What are the alternatives to SMB for time machine?\",\"title\":null,\"type\":\"comment\",\"url\":null}],\"created_at\":\"2026-10-19T08:24:51.000Z\",\"created_at_i\":1792398291,\"id\":50149776,\"options\":[],\"parent_id\":50149738,\"points\":null,\"story_id\":50149392,\"text\":\"Works might be a stretch. Every time I hear someone complaining about Time Machine it turns out they are trying to do it over SMB.\",\"title\":null,\"type\":\"comment\",\"url\":null},{\"author\":\"egorfine\",\"children\":[],\"created_at\":\"2026-10-19T08:39:18.000Z\",\"created_at_i\":1792399158,\"id\":50149891,\"options\":[],\"parent_id\":50149738,\"points\":null,\"story_id\":50149392,\"text\":\"For like 15 years.\",\"title\":null,\"type\":\"comment\",\"url\":null},{\"author\":\"reftel\",\"children\":[],\"created_at\":\"2026-10-19T10:54:51.000Z\",\"created_at_i\":1792407291,\"id\":50150816,\"options\":[],\"parent_id\":50149738,\"points\":null,\"story_id\":50149392,\"text\":\"Since macOS 26, it\\u2019s SMB only. Time Machine over AFP is no longer supported.\",\"title\":null,\"type\":\"comment\",\"url\":null},{\"author\":\"windowsrookie\",\"children\":[],\"created_at\":\"2026-10-19T11:41:08.000Z\",\"created_at_i\":1792410068,\"id\":50151147,\"options\":[],\"parent_id\":50149738,\"points\":null,\"story_id\":50149392,\"text\":\"I\\u2019ve been using Time Machine with my synology nas for ~5 years. Works pretty well. I\\u2019d say once every 2 months it looses connection but seems to re-connect itself after a few minutes.\",\"title\":null,\"type\":\"comment\",\"url\":null}],\"created_at\":\"2026-10-19T08:18:16.000Z\",\"created_at_i\":1792397896,\"id\":50149738,\"options\":[],\"parent_id\":50149670,\"points\":null,\"story_id\":50149392,\"text\":\"Wait so Time Machine works over SMB? Huh\",\"title\":null,\"type\":\"comment\",\"url\":null},{\"author\":\"teekert\",\"children\":[{\"author\":\"amelius\",\"children\":[{\"author\":\"teekert\",\"children\":[],\"created_at\":\"2026-10-19T08:37:57.000Z\",\"created_at_i\":1792399077,\"id\":50149879,\"options\":[],\"parent_id\":50149843,\"points\":null,\"story_id\":50149392,\"text\":\"Apple will also use AI to make it at least as painful and as unexpected as before. It&#x27;s their business model (I have come to believe).<p>Edit: But good point of course, but I believe it will remain cat and mouse, no idea how Apple internally sees it, whether they really don&#x27;t care, or actively break stuff (break your ** face tonight).\",\"title\":null,\"type\":\"comment\",\"url\":null},{\"author\":\"saagarjha\",\"children\":[{\"author\":\"amelius\",\"children\":[{\"author\":\"saagarjha\",\"children\":[],\"created_at\":\"2026-10-19T10:52:09.000Z\",\"created_at_i\":1792407129,\"id\":50150798,\"options\":[],\"parent_id\":50150437,\"points\":null,\"story_id\":50149392,\"text\":\"Suggesting that there are these things called computers that are now a lot simpler and reliable would be met with derision because it is a valueless comment\",\"title\":null,\"type\":\"comment\",\"url\":null},{\"author\":\"mort96\",\"children\":[{\"author\":\"amelius\",\"children\":[{\"author\":\"mort96\",\"children\":[],\"created_at\":\"2026-10-19T14:08:33.000Z\",\"created_at_i\":1792418913,\"id\":50152721,\"options\":[],\"parent_id\":50151942,\"points\":null,\"story_id\":50149392,\"text\":\"No, their argument was not &quot;we should do reverse engineering exclusively by hand&quot;. Their argument was that automating reverse engineering to some degree doesn&#x27;t automatically fix the issue.\",\"title\":null,\"type\":\"comment\",\"url\":null}],\"created_at\":\"2026-10-19T13:02:14.000Z\",\"created_at_i\":1792414934,\"id\":50151942,\"options\":[],\"parent_id\":50151766,\"points\":null,\"story_id\":50149392,\"text\":\"No it&#x27;s &quot;let&#x27;s do it the oldfashioned way&quot;.\",\"title\":null,\"type\":\"comment\",\"url\":null}],\"created_at\":\"2026-10-19T12:47:15.000Z\",\"created_at_i\":1792414035,\"id\":50151766,\"options\":[],\"parent_id\":50150437,\"points\":null,\"story_id\":50149392,\"text\":\"Your comment, ending in &quot;let&#x27;s go back to the abacus&quot;, only makes sense if &quot;let&#x27;s stop doing reverse engineering&quot; is a message which can be read out from your parent comment. Is it?\",\"title\":null,\"type\":\"comment\",\"url\":null}],\"created_at\":\"2026-10-19T10:00:00.000Z\",\"created_at_i\":1792404000,\"id\":50150437,\"options\":[],\"parent_id\":50150412,\"points\":null,\"story_id\":50149392,\"text\":\"Computers are not a magic wand that will solve all your problems. Let&#x27;s go back to the abacus!\",\"title\":null,\"type\":\"comment\",\"url\":null}],\"created_at\":\"2026-10-19T09:56:40.000Z\",\"created_at_i\":1792403800,\"id\":50150412,\"options\":[],\"parent_id\":50149843,\"points\":null,\"story_id\":50149392,\"text\":\"Reverse engineering is not a magic wand you can wave on everything and expect something good to come out of it. You need to actually know what you are trying to get out of it.\",\"title\":null,\"type\":\"comment\",\"url\":null},{\"author\":\"makeitdouble\",\"children\":[],\"created_at\":\"2026-10-19T10:25:53.000Z\",\"created_at_i\":1792405553,\"id\":50150616,\"options\":[],\"parent_id\":50149843,\"points\":null,\"story_id\":50149392,\"text\":\"The issue is rarely to have some working setup  at a given time, IMHO the real fight is to keep it working thought the updates and ecosystem changes.<p>That was the same issue with jailbreaking, it gave a lot of freedom, while also being a PITA to deal with at every security fix and system change. Many jailbreakers came to parent&#x27;s conclusion and just &quot;give up&quot;.\",\"title\":null,\"type\":\"comment\",\"url\":null},{\"author\":\"the_hoffa\",\"children\":[{\"author\":\"joshspankit\",\"children\":[],\"created_at\":\"2026-10-19T11:36:55.000Z\",\"created_at_i\":1792409815,\"id\":50151112,\"options\":[],\"parent_id\":50150636,\"points\":null,\"story_id\":50149392,\"text\":\"This already happens and has been happening for a while, especially in the Apple ecosystem. I can\\u2019t even count the number of apps that did something novel, got popular, had that novelty restricted by some update, then got \\u201creplaced\\u201d by an Apple app&#x2F;feature as though they never existed.\",\"title\":null,\"type\":\"comment\",\"url\":null}],\"created_at\":\"2026-10-19T10:27:55.000Z\",\"created_at_i\":1792405675,\"id\":50150636,\"options\":[],\"parent_id\":50149843,\"points\":null,\"story_id\":50149392,\"text\":\"I suspect what we might actually see in that regard is something similar to Minecraft (the Java based one): people were regularly modding it because it was Java based and easy to decomp and know where to inject a mod.<p>So the Minecraft crew would regularly release updates and obfuscate the code again - most updates were to fix issues&#x2F;add features, but some were purely for obfuscation. So if you wanted your mod to work it meant not playing any other version.<p>I&#x27;m guessing we&#x27;ll start seeing more regular updates from software makers; will the updates be fixing bugs or adding features - sure - but I&#x27;m guessing that quite a few updates will be simply to obfuscate the code and change addresses around so that any mods that worked before, won&#x27;t in the new version. Sure an LLM could just decomp it again, but I figure most people won&#x27;t want to spend the tokens&#x2F;time it takes for the LLM to actually do the work on that new decomp effort. And with the fact that most people have &quot;auto-update&quot; defaulted to on, I&#x27;m foreseeing that apps will get auto-updated likely daily in the near future to combat this.<p>Years ago, someone once told me &quot;I hope you live in interesting times&quot; ... I don&#x27;t think this is what they had in mind.\",\"title\":null,\"type\":\"comment\",\"url\":null}],\"created_at\":\"2026-10-19T08:33:50.000Z\",\"created_at_i\":1792398830,\"id\":50149843,\"options\":[],\"parent_id\":50149749,\"points\":null,\"story_id\":50149392,\"text\":\"I think AI might change that. Reverse engineering has just become a lot simpler and more reliable.\",\"title\":null,\"type\":\"comment\",\"url\":null},{\"author\":\"msm_\",\"children\":[{\"author\":\"teekert\",\"children\":[{\"author\":\"brnt\",\"children\":[{\"author\":\"Alexander-Barth\",\"children\":[],\"created_at\":\"2026-10-19T11:52:56.000Z\",\"created_at_i\":1792410776,\"id\":50151253,\"options\":[],\"parent_id\":50150923,\"points\":null,\"story_id\":50149392,\"text\":\"I can confirm. That is basically what I did for my father-in-law. The laptop is quite ancient by now (more than 10 years old) but still good enough for running current version of Ubuntu.\",\"title\":null,\"type\":\"comment\",\"url\":null},{\"author\":\"jorvi\",\"children\":[{\"author\":\"teekert\",\"children\":[{\"author\":\"torstenvl\",\"children\":[],\"created_at\":\"2026-10-19T12:43:33.000Z\",\"created_at_i\":1792413813,\"id\":50151721,\"options\":[],\"parent_id\":50151615,\"points\":null,\"story_id\":50149392,\"text\":\"That&#x27;s the point.\",\"title\":null,\"type\":\"comment\",\"url\":null},{\"author\":\"pjmlp\",\"children\":[],\"created_at\":\"2026-10-19T12:56:45.000Z\",\"created_at_i\":1792414605,\"id\":50151876,\"options\":[],\"parent_id\":50151615,\"points\":null,\"story_id\":50149392,\"text\":\"Yes, they are always changing, I know Linux since 1996, use it as part of my work, last time I used a system like Ubuntu was last year, where it failed to work with a GIGABYTE BRIX Mini-PC UEFI firmware when starting from internal SSD, which I eventually gave up after several weeks trying various workarounds.\",\"title\":null,\"type\":\"comment\",\"url\":null}],\"created_at\":\"2026-10-19T12:32:42.000Z\",\"created_at_i\":1792413162,\"id\":50151615,\"options\":[],\"parent_id\":50151482,\"points\":null,\"story_id\":50149392,\"text\":\"When did you last evaluate a system like Ubuntu? I&#x27;d have agreed with you 10, perhaps 5 years ago. But things have changed.\",\"title\":null,\"type\":\"comment\",\"url\":null},{\"author\":\"brnt\",\"children\":[],\"created_at\":\"2026-10-19T12:54:46.000Z\",\"created_at_i\":1792414486,\"id\":50151854,\"options\":[],\"parent_id\":50151482,\"points\":null,\"story_id\":50149392,\"text\":\"&gt; Linux is the opposite of fire-and-forget. Eventually a papercut always turns up that will require the terminal.<p>When I say it&#x27;s been fire-and-forget, I mean that for at least two decades.<p>If papercuts turn up, you are running the wrong distro or secretly a power user that naturally causes problems for themselves every now and then. This won&#x27;t happen with normal users on a long term stable distro.\",\"title\":null,\"type\":\"comment\",\"url\":null},{\"author\":\"orwin\",\"children\":[{\"author\":\"bornfreddy\",\"children\":[],\"created_at\":\"2026-10-19T16:05:45.000Z\",\"created_at_i\":1792425945,\"id\":50154090,\"options\":[],\"parent_id\":50151996,\"points\":null,\"story_id\":50149392,\"text\":\"This. A competent admin and a clueless user is a perfect match for Linux. Either clueless admin or &quot;competent&quot; user however... not so much.\",\"title\":null,\"type\":\"comment\",\"url\":null}],\"created_at\":\"2026-10-19T13:09:08.000Z\",\"created_at_i\":1792415348,\"id\":50151996,\"options\":[],\"parent_id\":50151482,\"points\":null,\"story_id\":50149392,\"text\":\"Linux is not fire and forget for people who use technology without really understanding it, but for people who don&#x27;t use technology and just need to check their mails and connect to government website to pay their taxes, it is the best.<p>Once it&#x27;s configured for these kind of people, it&#x27;s really fire and forget, and it was already that in 2016.\",\"title\":null,\"type\":\"comment\",\"url\":null},{\"author\":\"fullstop\",\"children\":[],\"created_at\":\"2026-10-19T13:47:19.000Z\",\"created_at_i\":1792417639,\"id\":50152455,\"options\":[],\"parent_id\":50151482,\"points\":null,\"story_id\":50149392,\"text\":\"My daughter has Kinoite set up for her boyfriend&#x27;s grandparents.  It&#x27;s immutable, so there&#x27;s not much damage they can do without admin access, and she made it look much like Windows.  They were already doing everything with Firefox as it was, so it wasn&#x27;t a huge transition for them.<p>She did go back to fix things because the grandma managed to delete the printer, and she said that the downloads folder was full of exe files with sketchy names.<p>I&#x27;m with you on the macOS stability in the past.  The thing that gets me is that Windows somehow gets a pass in this regard, but there have been some pretty big changes over the years.\",\"title\":null,\"type\":\"comment\",\"url\":null}],\"created_at\":\"2026-10-19T12:17:28.000Z\",\"created_at_i\":1792412248,\"id\":50151482,\"options\":[],\"parent_id\":50150923,\"points\":null,\"story_id\":50149392,\"text\":\"Linux is the opposite of fire-and-forget. Eventually a papercut always turns up that will require the terminal.<p>macOS also has (well, <i>had</i>) extreme UI stability. You could transport someone from 2003 used to Mac OS X 10.0 two decades into the future and they would be able to use macOS Ventura (and all previous versions) purely by muscle memory. Good luck doing that with Windows and Linux with their constant UI churn.<p>Interestingly enough, by iOS-ifying macOS, they&#x27;ve made it much easier to shift someone using an iPhone from Windows to macOS, because they already recognize many of the UI and UX paradigms.\",\"title\":null,\"type\":\"comment\",\"url\":null},{\"author\":\"pjmlp\",\"children\":[{\"author\":\"brnt\",\"children\":[],\"created_at\":\"2026-10-19T12:56:30.000Z\",\"created_at_i\":1792414590,\"id\":50151871,\"options\":[],\"parent_id\":50151841,\"points\":null,\"story_id\":50149392,\"text\":\"Of course you need to help them to alternatives, if they are not that tech-illiterate that they have very specific wishes for software.<p>It&#x27;s been a while since I experienced people sharing software one discs by the way, it&#x27;s been a while since I saw discs at all ;)\",\"title\":null,\"type\":\"comment\",\"url\":null},{\"author\":\"teekert\",\"children\":[],\"created_at\":\"2026-10-19T13:42:33.000Z\",\"created_at_i\":1792417353,\"id\":50152402,\"options\":[],\"parent_id\":50151841,\"points\":null,\"story_id\":50149392,\"text\":\"I do the same with acquaintances whom I sometimes sell secondhand gear, I don&#x27;t want to do any support so they get Windows. For my father it&#x27;s different, I can do support, plus he really appreciates and is willing to make sacrifices for, the privacy angle.\",\"title\":null,\"type\":\"comment\",\"url\":null}],\"created_at\":\"2026-10-19T12:53:29.000Z\",\"created_at_i\":1792414409,\"id\":50151841,\"options\":[],\"parent_id\":50150923,\"points\":null,\"story_id\":50149392,\"text\":\"I beg to differ, I tried that approach a few years back with my folks, only to discover on my next visit a few months later that they went down to a local PC store and got Windows re-installed.<p>They never managed to get it working for their liking, it wasn&#x27;t able to run the stuff their friends used to talk about or share via CD&#x2F;DVD&#x2F;USB sticks, and they did not wanted to bother me with &quot;dumb&quot; questions.<p>So I rather that they have Windows, go down to the local PC store when something isn&#x27;t working as it should.\",\"title\":null,\"type\":\"comment\",\"url\":null},{\"author\":\"outworlder\",\"children\":[],\"created_at\":\"2026-10-19T16:27:16.000Z\",\"created_at_i\":1792427236,\"id\":50154372,\"options\":[],\"parent_id\":50150923,\"points\":null,\"story_id\":50149392,\"text\":\"&gt; Linux is a great &quot;fire-and-forget&quot; OS for situations tech-illiterate loved ones.<p>Only if the device is truly &#x27;forgotten&#x27;. As in, it is an appliance that&#x27;s going to do that task forever and ever. Be it web browsing or what have you.<p>If requirements change, it becomes a problem. If they have to do work on them and interact with companies, you may need either Windows or Linux. Thankfully online Office365 removed a big use-case. But there are others. It is very likely that whatever package you need will be available on Windows or OSX.\",\"title\":null,\"type\":\"comment\",\"url\":null}],\"created_at\":\"2026-10-19T11:09:44.000Z\",\"created_at_i\":1792408184,\"id\":50150923,\"options\":[],\"parent_id\":50150431,\"points\":null,\"story_id\":50149392,\"text\":\"Macbooks are great devices, but Linux is a great &quot;fire-and-forget&quot; OS for situations tech-illiterate loved ones. I&#x27;ve done it many times, with much succes and satisfaction (on both ends) without barely any aftercare. All you really need to do is teach them to install those updates and install a major upgrade every few years (or not, Ubuntu will run fine for a decade).\",\"title\":null,\"type\":\"comment\",\"url\":null},{\"author\":\"windowsrookie\",\"children\":[{\"author\":\"ptman\",\"children\":[],\"created_at\":\"2026-10-19T12:34:59.000Z\",\"created_at_i\":1792413299,\"id\":50151634,\"options\":[],\"parent_id\":50151129,\"points\":null,\"story_id\":50149392,\"text\":\"Viruses were a problem and you&#x27;re suggesting an old unpatched system.\",\"title\":null,\"type\":\"comment\",\"url\":null}],\"created_at\":\"2026-10-19T11:38:14.000Z\",\"created_at_i\":1792409894,\"id\":50151129,\"options\":[],\"parent_id\":50150431,\"points\":null,\"story_id\":50149392,\"text\":\"Have you considered open core legacy? I am running macOS Sonoma nearly perfectly on a 2010 iMac.\",\"title\":null,\"type\":\"comment\",\"url\":null},{\"author\":\"brightball\",\"children\":[{\"author\":\"outworlder\",\"children\":[],\"created_at\":\"2026-10-19T16:25:09.000Z\",\"created_at_i\":1792427109,\"id\":50154343,\"options\":[],\"parent_id\":50153564,\"points\":null,\"story_id\":50149392,\"text\":\"Yes, did the same more than 10 years ago.<p>My mother wanted a device to just access internet banking. Gave her a netbook (remember those?). Only successful Linux use-case for non techies I&#x27;ve succeeded at.\",\"title\":null,\"type\":\"comment\",\"url\":null}],\"created_at\":\"2026-10-19T15:21:56.000Z\",\"created_at_i\":1792423316,\"id\":50153564,\"options\":[],\"parent_id\":50150431,\"points\":null,\"story_id\":50149392,\"text\":\"This is why I direct most family to Apple devices. Otherwise it\\u2019s constant tech support.\",\"title\":null,\"type\":\"comment\",\"url\":null}],\"created_at\":\"2026-10-19T09:59:11.000Z\",\"created_at_i\":1792403951,\"id\":50150431,\"options\":[],\"parent_id\":50150367,\"points\":null,\"story_id\":50149392,\"text\":\"We made the call to go Apple years and years ago when he was always fighting viruses and virus scanners on Windows. His Macbook was still (hardware-wise) perfectly functional, but does not receive security updates anymore, so he went iPad. Indeed there are more problems than expected. I expected even less IT support from me was needed. Instead he now complains about the lack of control (which I am certainly ok with btw, the complaining I mean.)<p>The current plan is to put Ubuntu on the Macbook, not sure what to do with the iPad but make it less &quot;primary computer&quot; will be one of the policies to implement indeed. Maybe he will be happy with an external disk and f-spot, and his music nicely sorted into files. Then some generic backup solution to a Synology NAS or something...\",\"title\":null,\"type\":\"comment\",\"url\":null}],\"created_at\":\"2026-10-19T09:48:36.000Z\",\"created_at_i\":1792403316,\"id\":50150367,\"options\":[],\"parent_id\":50149749,\"points\":null,\"story_id\":50149392,\"text\":\"&gt;My father (...) tries to use his iPad as a PC (...) He&#x27;s mostly on the iPad + keyboard because it&#x27;s a nicely managed device update-wise and &quot;what I can install&quot;-wise, virus-wise. (...) But I recent told him: Just give up<p>Since he apparently wants a PC, isn&#x27;t the logical conclusion the opposite? &quot;Just give up. Buy a Windows&#x2F;Linux laptop, you are too dependent on Apple, there is always something with the Apple solutions.&quot;\",\"title\":null,\"type\":\"comment\",\"url\":null},{\"author\":\"yuck39\",\"children\":[],\"created_at\":\"2026-10-19T13:58:41.000Z\",\"created_at_i\":1792418321,\"id\":50152594,\"options\":[],\"parent_id\":50149749,\"points\":null,\"story_id\":50149392,\"text\":\"&gt; Yes, Proton Drive eats 400 GB of your iPad storage now, I don&#x27;t know how to trim that regularly.<p>I love Proton services and have been a subscriber for years. I do not understand what is going on with this. I have proton drive on my iPhone, I manually open it monthly to let my photos sync. I pay for the storage on proton drive to _save_ space locally, but whenever I sync to proton drive the storage space used locally by the app is almost equal to a local photo library! Yes I can delete the app, re-install it monthly to sync my library once, but it quickly balloons to dozens of gigabytes when I start to sync my local photos.<p>Can anyone at proton explain what is going on here?\",\"title\":null,\"type\":\"comment\",\"url\":null}],\"created_at\":\"2026-10-19T08:20:00.000Z\",\"created_at_i\":1792398000,\"id\":50149749,\"options\":[],\"parent_id\":50149670,\"points\":null,\"story_id\":50149392,\"text\":\"Over my life I have found that with any Apple technology it may work great with anything outside Apple&#x27;s control... Until it doesn&#x27;t, usually out of nowhere.<p>My father is mostly on Apple and tries to use his iPad as a PC, ie with files and &quot;the filesystem&quot;, he tries to get out of using extra iCloud for pictures (using Proton, or he asks me about Immich, or he wants to use an external HDD, or use Synology Photos and backup). He&#x27;s mostly on the iPad + keyboard because it&#x27;s a nicely managed device update-wise and &quot;what I can install&quot;-wise, virus-wise.<p>But I recent told him: Just give up. Buy (rent!) the storage, you are too depended on me, there is always something with the non-Apple solutions. Yes, your iTunes library is a mess on the filesystem, just don&#x27;t look at it through anything else than &quot;Music&quot;. Yes, Proton Drive eats 400 GB of your iPad storage now, I don&#x27;t know how to trim that regularly. I&#x27;m sorry, it&#x27;s either Linux and I can help set some things up, or full Apple. Choose. His current mixed, &quot;trying to get best-of-both-worlds&quot;, approach is just pure unexpected pain, every time again.<p>Edit: I also have an iPad, it does work for me, but where my father is trying to use his iPad as a central, primary device (the hub), for me it is a client (spoke) of my own infra. Apps get access to information and I never use Apple services for anything. So indeed, Immich, Proton, Home Assistant, government and banking apps, etc. My iPad is throw-away: If it dies, it dies... I buy a new one, log into all (self-hosed) services and I&#x27;m 100% back up and running. Doing the same for my father is also pain, because his Home Assistant will have issues, or his Immich instance, and it&#x27;s real work to fix, and he won&#x27;t tell me for months if it does not work and work piles up. So, I tell him to surrender to Apple... It die a little inside.\",\"title\":null,\"type\":\"comment\",\"url\":null},{\"author\":\"Hamuko\",\"children\":[{\"author\":\"alt227\",\"children\":[{\"author\":\"bombcar\",\"children\":[],\"created_at\":\"2026-10-19T13:16:55.000Z\",\"created_at_i\":1792415815,\"id\":50152070,\"options\":[],\"parent_id\":50150717,\"points\":null,\"story_id\":50149392,\"text\":\"Sometimes I wish Apple had gone-head-to-head with Samba and forked v4 and kept it GPL-2 - they might have had enough power to force the issue.\",\"title\":null,\"type\":\"comment\",\"url\":null},{\"author\":\"fps-hero\",\"children\":[],\"created_at\":\"2026-10-19T16:00:35.000Z\",\"created_at_i\":1792425635,\"id\":50154030,\"options\":[],\"parent_id\":50150717,\"points\":null,\"story_id\":50149392,\"text\":\"It\\u2019s the insane having these discussions in 2026 given samba issues have been a thing as long I\\u2019ve been using OSX&#x2F;macOS. Mixed windows&#x2F;mac network shares have always been a PITA.\",\"title\":null,\"type\":\"comment\",\"url\":null}],\"created_at\":\"2026-10-19T10:39:18.000Z\",\"created_at_i\":1792406358,\"id\":50150717,\"options\":[],\"parent_id\":50149795,\"points\":null,\"story_id\":50149392,\"text\":\"Apple refuse to pay Microsoft the license for their SMB stack and have dropped open source Samba due to license conflicts with GPL, and so have developed their own implementation (SMBX). This is the reason it is so buggy, and there are loads of articles and posts all over the internet about it. Heres one example from the top of google.<p><a href=\\\"https:&#x2F;&#x2F;www.reddit.com&#x2F;r&#x2F;MacOS&#x2F;comments&#x2F;18sjnwb&#x2F;state_of_macos_smb&#x2F;\\\" rel=\\\"nofollow\\\">https:&#x2F;&#x2F;www.reddit.com&#x2F;r&#x2F;MacOS&#x2F;comments&#x2F;18sjnwb&#x2F;state_of_mac...</a>\",\"title\":null,\"type\":\"comment\",\"url\":null},{\"author\":\"lostmsu\",\"children\":[],\"created_at\":\"2026-10-19T10:45:00.000Z\",\"created_at_i\":1792406700,\"id\":50150745,\"options\":[],\"parent_id\":50149795,\"points\":null,\"story_id\":50149392,\"text\":\"I feel the same about Linux clients. I wish there was some focus\\nthere.\",\"title\":null,\"type\":\"comment\",\"url\":null}],\"created_at\":\"2026-10-19T08:27:39.000Z\",\"created_at_i\":1792398459,\"id\":50149795,\"options\":[],\"parent_id\":50149670,\"points\":null,\"story_id\":50149392,\"text\":\"Gotta say, using SMB with Apple clients is a massive pain in the ass. I imagine a large part of that is just Microsoft cruft, leaving you to deal with limitations designed around Windows. And you don&#x27;t really have another option after Apple deprecated AFP.\",\"title\":null,\"type\":\"comment\",\"url\":null},{\"author\":\"crossroadsguy\",\"children\":[],\"created_at\":\"2026-10-19T08:46:49.000Z\",\"created_at_i\":1792399609,\"id\":50149955,\"options\":[],\"parent_id\":50149670,\"points\":null,\"story_id\":50149392,\"text\":\"This really makes me sad. They devoted time and effort to support something as locked and as broken as Apple Time Machine. That <i>thing</i> is a bad choice even within a 100% Apple ecosystem.\",\"title\":null,\"type\":\"comment\",\"url\":null},{\"author\":\"spwa4\",\"children\":[{\"author\":\"AshamedCaptain\",\"children\":[{\"author\":\"tosti\",\"children\":[{\"author\":\"tremon\",\"children\":[],\"created_at\":\"2026-10-19T15:23:08.000Z\",\"created_at_i\":1792423388,\"id\":50153572,\"options\":[],\"parent_id\":50151904,\"points\":null,\"story_id\":50149392,\"text\":\"I don&#x27;t think tux was ever included in the mainline kernel. It was closer to a proof-of-concept than a serious http server alternative.\",\"title\":null,\"type\":\"comment\",\"url\":null}],\"created_at\":\"2026-10-19T12:58:50.000Z\",\"created_at_i\":1792414730,\"id\":50151904,\"options\":[],\"parent_id\":50150501,\"points\":null,\"story_id\":50149392,\"text\":\"Is that the reason? In the past, a kernel webserver called tux (IIRC) was removed because it was just rediculous and the performance gap was considered a solved problem, not worth keeping an in-kernel server around.\",\"title\":null,\"type\":\"comment\",\"url\":null},{\"author\":\"hackernudes\",\"children\":[],\"created_at\":\"2026-10-19T16:10:14.000Z\",\"created_at_i\":1792426214,\"id\":50154162,\"options\":[],\"parent_id\":50150501,\"points\":null,\"story_id\":50149392,\"text\":\"It also supports RDMA (sometimes called &quot;SMB Direct&quot;).\",\"title\":null,\"type\":\"comment\",\"url\":null}],\"created_at\":\"2026-10-19T10:09:10.000Z\",\"created_at_i\":1792404550,\"id\":50150501,\"options\":[],\"parent_id\":50149999,\"points\":null,\"story_id\":50149392,\"text\":\"And basically only used by those allergic to Samba&#x27;s GPLv3.\",\"title\":null,\"type\":\"comment\",\"url\":null}],\"created_at\":\"2026-10-19T08:52:42.000Z\",\"created_at_i\":1792399962,\"id\":50149999,\"options\":[],\"parent_id\":50149670,\"points\":null,\"story_id\":50149392,\"text\":\"Wait ... the linux kernel has an SMB server?\",\"title\":null,\"type\":\"comment\",\"url\":null},{\"author\":\"buybackoff\",\"children\":[{\"author\":\"e12e\",\"children\":[{\"author\":\"philjohn\",\"children\":[],\"created_at\":\"2026-10-19T10:19:43.000Z\",\"created_at_i\":1792405183,\"id\":50150581,\"options\":[],\"parent_id\":50150381,\"points\":null,\"story_id\":50149392,\"text\":\"Correct. TrueNAS uses Samba, which has supported this for a while. On TrueNAS it also creates a ZFS snapshot after a successful TimeMachine backup which is really nice for the times MacOS decides to corrupt a TimeMachine volume for no apparent reason.\",\"title\":null,\"type\":\"comment\",\"url\":null}],\"created_at\":\"2026-10-19T09:50:53.000Z\",\"created_at_i\":1792403453,\"id\":50150381,\"options\":[],\"parent_id\":50150248,\"points\":null,\"story_id\":50149392,\"text\":\"I think this is in-kernel smb?\",\"title\":null,\"type\":\"comment\",\"url\":null},{\"author\":\"nign\",\"children\":[],\"created_at\":\"2026-10-19T11:31:59.000Z\",\"created_at_i\":1792409519,\"id\":50151086,\"options\":[],\"parent_id\":50150248,\"points\":null,\"story_id\":50149392,\"text\":\"This never really worked for me. It would start a backup, very slowly send over a tiny amount of preliminary data, and then fail after several hours. I&#x27;m hoping the in-kernel support will get this working for me, but who knows...\",\"title\":null,\"type\":\"comment\",\"url\":null}],\"created_at\":\"2026-10-19T09:27:55.000Z\",\"created_at_i\":1792402075,\"id\":50150248,\"options\":[],\"parent_id\":50149670,\"points\":null,\"story_id\":50149392,\"text\":\"Time Machine support has been working on TrueNAS Scale, just via one checkbox on a SMB share config, for years. I thought it was just something in a share config that was always there. Now I wonder how did they do it.\",\"title\":null,\"type\":\"comment\",\"url\":null},{\"author\":\"Razengan\",\"children\":[{\"author\":\"nine_k\",\"children\":[],\"created_at\":\"2026-10-19T12:53:25.000Z\",\"created_at_i\":1792414405,\"id\":50151840,\"options\":[],\"parent_id\":50151772,\"points\":null,\"story_id\":50149392,\"text\":\"It does. It&#x27;s the difference between &quot;be my guest&quot; and &quot;catch me if you can&quot;. Currently we have better luck catching, but there&#x27;s no promise that it should stay this way.\",\"title\":null,\"type\":\"comment\",\"url\":null}],\"created_at\":\"2026-10-19T12:47:42.000Z\",\"created_at_i\":1792414062,\"id\":50151772,\"options\":[],\"parent_id\":50149670,\"points\":null,\"story_id\":50149392,\"text\":\"&gt; <i>Still sucks that it has to be reverse-engineered</i><p>With AI and stuff like rea.tools does that really matter anymore?<p>Let&#x27;s just accept that this is the new norm: Giant companies who had over 20 years to open their shit up, chose to be ass about it and refused, and now we can just take it. Screw them.\",\"title\":null,\"type\":\"comment\",\"url\":null},{\"author\":\"ahartmetz\",\"children\":[{\"author\":\"14u2c\",\"children\":[],\"created_at\":\"2026-10-19T15:24:16.000Z\",\"created_at_i\":1792423456,\"id\":50153589,\"options\":[],\"parent_id\":50152803,\"points\":null,\"story_id\":50149392,\"text\":\"&gt; A user-space daemon can saturate almost any network link using a single CPU core.<p>Certainly not a guarantee at today&#x27;s link speeds. We&#x27;re increasingly leaning into RDMA and other forms of offloading for a reason.\",\"title\":null,\"type\":\"comment\",\"url\":null},{\"author\":\"loeg\",\"children\":[{\"author\":\"sophacles\",\"children\":[],\"created_at\":\"2026-10-19T16:48:44.000Z\",\"created_at_i\":1792428524,\"id\":50154676,\"options\":[],\"parent_id\":50153945,\"points\":null,\"story_id\":50149392,\"text\":\"In context of time machine backups...<p>The link will be whatever the  fastest network port you can get on an apple machine is. Its not 200Gbps - the apple end will be the limiting factor. A single core can probably fill that just fine.<p>And being pedantic - 200Gbps links are not a significant percentage of installed network ports in the computers in the world... so &quot;almost any&quot; is pretty accurate.\",\"title\":null,\"type\":\"comment\",\"url\":null}],\"created_at\":\"2026-10-19T15:54:08.000Z\",\"created_at_i\":1792425248,\"id\":50153945,\"options\":[],\"parent_id\":50152803,\"points\":null,\"story_id\":50149392,\"text\":\"&gt; A user-space daemon can saturate almost any network link using <i>a single CPU core.</i><p>Not 200Gbps of 16kB block writes, that&#x27;s for sure.<p>Agree with you on the rest about kernel vs userspace, just taking issue with the bit about a single CPU core.\",\"title\":null,\"type\":\"comment\",\"url\":null}],\"created_at\":\"2026-10-19T14:15:38.000Z\",\"created_at_i\":1792419338,\"id\":50152803,\"options\":[],\"parent_id\":50149670,\"points\":null,\"story_id\":50149392,\"text\":\"I&#x27;m not sure if ksmbd still makes all that much sense. A user-space daemon can saturate almost any network link using a single CPU core. And the attack surface... if you control ksmbd, you immediately own the machine.\",\"title\":null,\"type\":\"comment\",\"url\":null},{\"author\":\"caycep\",\"children\":[],\"created_at\":\"2026-10-19T15:36:54.000Z\",\"created_at_i\":1792424214,\"id\":50153731,\"options\":[],\"parent_id\":50149670,\"points\":null,\"story_id\":50149392,\"text\":\"Hmmm...this is separate from using SAMBA?<p>I tried setting up SAMBA by itself (on Debian) for Time Machine, but for it to work it seems to want Avahi running for mDNS; the problem is there is some weird bug or maybe user-related blooper where it collides with itself from time to time, leading to local DNS weirdness\",\"title\":null,\"type\":\"comment\",\"url\":null}],\"created_at\":\"2026-10-19T08:09:10.000Z\",\"created_at_i\":1792397350,\"id\":50149670,\"options\":[],\"parent_id\":50149392,\"points\":null,\"story_id\":50149392,\"text\":\"SMB improvements (<a href=\\\"https:&#x2F;&#x2F;git.kernel.org&#x2F;pub&#x2F;scm&#x2F;linux&#x2F;kernel&#x2F;git&#x2F;torvalds&#x2F;linux.git&#x2F;commit&#x2F;?id=61a09cfc121472013f99ae75066676739a5db626\\\" rel=\\\"nofollow\\\">https:&#x2F;&#x2F;git.kernel.org&#x2F;pub&#x2F;scm&#x2F;linux&#x2F;kernel&#x2F;git&#x2F;torvalds&#x2F;lin...</a>), I&#x27;ve moved to borg, but depending on how this goes may give time machine another try:<p><pre><code>   - Add opt-in Apple Time Machine support\\n\\n     Implement the AAPL negotiation and related Finder, stream,\\n     COPYCHUNK, sparse-file, CHANGE_NOTIFY, and RPC compatibility\\n     required for Time Machine shares, allowing macOS backupd to use\\n     ksmbd for backups\\n</code></pre>\\n<a href=\\\"https:&#x2F;&#x2F;www.phoronix.com&#x2F;news&#x2F;Linux-7.3-KSMBD\\\" rel=\\\"nofollow\\\">https:&#x2F;&#x2F;www.phoronix.com&#x2F;news&#x2F;Linux-7.3-KSMBD</a><p>Still sucks that it has to be reverse-engineered\",\"title\":null,\"type\":\"comment\",\"url\":null},{\"author\":\"djfergus\",\"children\":[{\"author\":\"cromka\",\"children\":[{\"author\":\"palata\",\"children\":[{\"author\":\"vbezhenar\",\"children\":[{\"author\":\"pmontra\",\"children\":[{\"author\":\"cromka\",\"children\":[{\"author\":\"pmontra\",\"children\":[],\"created_at\":\"2026-10-19T15:57:18.000Z\",\"created_at_i\":1792425438,\"id\":50153983,\"options\":[],\"parent_id\":50153707,\"points\":null,\"story_id\":50149392,\"text\":\"We agree on that, it is inevitable.<p>Furthermore we are lucky that Apple at least tolerates Asahi and does not throw at them more technical hurdles, legal issues (DMCA?) or whatever. Based on past history (in the 90s, so a lot of management changes) Apple would shut them down if they were a threat to their bottom line. I remember the shortly allowed Mac clones so, a possible scenario: a significant fraction of sold Macs is immediately reinstalled with Linux and provide zero dollars to cloud services and followup sales of other hardware. Apple is not going to like that.\",\"title\":null,\"type\":\"comment\",\"url\":null}],\"created_at\":\"2026-10-19T15:34:47.000Z\",\"created_at_i\":1792424087,\"id\":50153707,\"options\":[],\"parent_id\":50152218,\"points\":null,\"story_id\":50149392,\"text\":\"No one said otherwise. We\\u2019re discussing the speed of onboarding here. Intel is fast, Apple support via Asahi can do what they can do, but it must  be and is slow without any documentation whatsoever.\",\"title\":null,\"type\":\"comment\",\"url\":null}],\"created_at\":\"2026-10-19T13:28:28.000Z\",\"created_at_i\":1792416508,\"id\":50152218,\"options\":[],\"parent_id\":50151245,\"points\":null,\"story_id\":50149392,\"text\":\"Intel and AMD sell to customers that build computers running Linux, especially servers. Apple sells to itself, to build computers that run their own OS.\",\"title\":null,\"type\":\"comment\",\"url\":null},{\"author\":\"palata\",\"children\":[{\"author\":\"sejje\",\"children\":[{\"author\":\"palata\",\"children\":[],\"created_at\":\"2026-10-19T15:02:39.000Z\",\"created_at_i\":1792422159,\"id\":50153352,\"options\":[],\"parent_id\":50152672,\"points\":null,\"story_id\":50149392,\"text\":\"&gt; and -- yeah, that&#x27;s true.<p>So we agree :-). Of course I would like it if Apple was making my life better. I just find it naive to consider that companies are anything other than profit-maximizing machines. If they happen to do something you like, it&#x27;s because it is profitable for them. They couldn&#x27;t care less about you.<p>&gt; But people are gonna bitch.<p>Whatever you do, people are gonna bitch. Again it would be a bit naive to think that if you do &quot;your best&quot;, or if you do &quot;what&#x27;s right&quot;, or if you &quot;genuinely try&quot;, then everybody will appreciate. There will always be enough people bitching, of that I am certain.\",\"title\":null,\"type\":\"comment\",\"url\":null}],\"created_at\":\"2026-10-19T14:03:44.000Z\",\"created_at_i\":1792418624,\"id\":50152672,\"options\":[],\"parent_id\":50152511,\"points\":null,\"story_id\":50149392,\"text\":\"&gt; I don&#x27;t see a reason to complain about Apple not making bikes. I mean... they don&#x27;t have to, do they?<p>This analogy is really tortured. Apple doesn&#x27;t make bikes.<p>But they do make software, and hardware. People want to use them however they choose.<p>This is more like a bike company choosing a proprietary air valve that only lets you inflate your bike tires with their own company-branded air pump, even though there&#x27;s been other air pumps for many decades before them, and their design isn&#x27;t particularly better than the others. And you say &quot;well they don&#x27;t have to design an air valve adapter to use other air pumps&quot; and -- yeah, that&#x27;s true. But people are gonna bitch.\",\"title\":null,\"type\":\"comment\",\"url\":null},{\"author\":\"delecti\",\"children\":[],\"created_at\":\"2026-10-19T16:07:20.000Z\",\"created_at_i\":1792426040,\"id\":50154127,\"options\":[],\"parent_id\":50152511,\"points\":null,\"story_id\":50149392,\"text\":\"It&#x27;s perfectly reasonable to complain about things you aren&#x27;t entitled to. My Surface brand laptop has similarly ad-hoc Linux driver support. I don&#x27;t expect  Microsoft to support it better, but I&#x27;m still annoyed they don&#x27;t.\",\"title\":null,\"type\":\"comment\",\"url\":null}],\"created_at\":\"2026-10-19T13:51:50.000Z\",\"created_at_i\":1792417910,\"id\":50152511,\"options\":[],\"parent_id\":50151245,\"points\":null,\"story_id\":50149392,\"text\":\"Sure. But is that a reason to complain? If Apple made bikes, they may be nice bikes. But I don&#x27;t see a reason to complain about Apple not making bikes. I mean... they don&#x27;t <i>have to</i>, do they?<p>Would be cool if Apple decided to support Linux, of course. But why would they? If it was profitable for them, they would do it. Instead they think it&#x27;s more profitable to enshittify their products. That&#x27;s life.<p>Now people who reverse engineer that to help us run Linux on Apple hardware... I would guess they don&#x27;t make a fortune doing that, and I find it impressive work.\",\"title\":null,\"type\":\"comment\",\"url\":null}],\"created_at\":\"2026-10-19T11:51:30.000Z\",\"created_at_i\":1792410690,\"id\":50151245,\"options\":[],\"parent_id\":50150754,\"points\":null,\"story_id\":50149392,\"text\":\"And if Apple did it themselves, like Intel or AMD do, it would be released at the same time as macOS.\",\"title\":null,\"type\":\"comment\",\"url\":null}],\"created_at\":\"2026-10-19T10:45:41.000Z\",\"created_at_i\":1792406741,\"id\":50150754,\"options\":[],\"parent_id\":50150216,\"points\":null,\"story_id\":50149392,\"text\":\"If I was to do it myself, it would take centuries. I guess it&#x27;s a matter of perspective :-).\",\"title\":null,\"type\":\"comment\",\"url\":null}],\"created_at\":\"2026-10-19T09:21:43.000Z\",\"created_at_i\":1792401703,\"id\":50150216,\"options\":[],\"parent_id\":50149959,\"points\":null,\"story_id\":50149392,\"text\":\"I wouldn&#x27;t call it fast, these are several years old and no GPU, Thunderbolt, etc. support still. It&#x27;s steady, but not fast.\",\"title\":null,\"type\":\"comment\",\"url\":null},{\"author\":\"e12e\",\"children\":[{\"author\":\"pmontra\",\"children\":[],\"created_at\":\"2026-10-19T13:30:45.000Z\",\"created_at_i\":1792416645,\"id\":50152252,\"options\":[],\"parent_id\":50150429,\"points\":null,\"story_id\":50149392,\"text\":\"Not one of the downvoters. How would the kernel fry the Wi-Fi chip?\",\"title\":null,\"type\":\"comment\",\"url\":null},{\"author\":\"cromka\",\"children\":[],\"created_at\":\"2026-10-19T15:41:13.000Z\",\"created_at_i\":1792424473,\"id\":50153785,\"options\":[],\"parent_id\":50150429,\"points\":null,\"story_id\":50149392,\"text\":\"The radio limits are very heavily guarded by the firmware solely because its regulatory compliance: different jurisdictions have different limits and you can\\u2019t just let userspace or kernel change it at will. Heck, most manufacturers don\\u2019t let you change WiFi card anymore or allow only a whitelisted WWAN card for same reason.<p>So, I would be super interested how you inferred it was Linux\\u2019 fault but I sssume b&#x2F;s at this point.\",\"title\":null,\"type\":\"comment\",\"url\":null}],\"created_at\":\"2026-10-19T09:59:00.000Z\",\"created_at_i\":1792403940,\"id\":50150429,\"options\":[],\"parent_id\":50149959,\"points\":null,\"story_id\":50149392,\"text\":\"On the other hand - I just had to send my Asus vivobook s15 to repair, because Linux (most likely) fried the wifi chip.<p>Pretty disappointed in Qualcomm&#x27;s promise of upstream support for Snapdragon X Elite turning out to be: lol, not actually so you can run Linux on devices, only so you can maybe boot without most devices working...<p>Hope things will keep improving despite lack of help from vendors.\",\"title\":null,\"type\":\"comment\",\"url\":null},{\"author\":\"nubinetwork\",\"children\":[],\"created_at\":\"2026-10-19T10:41:20.000Z\",\"created_at_i\":1792406480,\"id\":50150731,\"options\":[],\"parent_id\":50149959,\"points\":null,\"story_id\":50149392,\"text\":\"Just because you have a device tree file, doesn&#x27;t mean its correct, or even accurate.\",\"title\":null,\"type\":\"comment\",\"url\":null},{\"author\":\"emacdona\",\"children\":[{\"author\":\"wang_li\",\"children\":[{\"author\":\"troyvit\",\"children\":[],\"created_at\":\"2026-10-19T15:33:08.000Z\",\"created_at_i\":1792423988,\"id\":50153685,\"options\":[],\"parent_id\":50153290,\"points\":null,\"story_id\":50149392,\"text\":\"I&#x27;m sure a lot has changed since <i>cough</i> 2004, but that was the state with the PowerPC G4 and G5. Machine learning Linux applications used the IBM Power architecture because of their vectorization features, and that hardware was ridiculously expensive. When Apple started using Motorola and IBM&#x27;s cheaper  PowerPC chips with Altivec this small market was pumped, but none of their groovy scientific applications worked on OSX.<p>This created a large enough market for Linux distros installed on Apple hardware to make it a sustainable business model the makers of Yellow Dog Linux to buy the hardware through a reseller channel, ship it to the building, install Linux on it, then ship the device to customers. I worked there and it was fun.<p>Lots has changed since then of course, one of which being that Apple controls the chip from top to bottom, and their near-indifference in 2004 now seems to have transformed into something more adversarial.\",\"title\":null,\"type\":\"comment\",\"url\":null}],\"created_at\":\"2026-10-19T14:57:41.000Z\",\"created_at_i\":1792421861,\"id\":50153290,\"options\":[],\"parent_id\":50150898,\"points\":null,\"story_id\":50149392,\"text\":\"I&#x27;m curious on why you think that, say, an M2 running linux will ever be a better choice for AI workloads than an M5 Pro running MacOS? What is it that you think is in the hardware that you&#x27;d be able to unlock with linux but isn&#x27;t exposed by MacOS?\",\"title\":null,\"type\":\"comment\",\"url\":null}],\"created_at\":\"2026-10-19T11:05:31.000Z\",\"created_at_i\":1792407931,\"id\":50150898,\"options\":[],\"parent_id\":50149959,\"points\":null,\"story_id\":50149392,\"text\":\"I have this dream that previous gen M chips will be worth more than current gen M chips b&#x2F;c people want to run local inference on Apple silicon... but they want to use Linux and not OS X.\",\"title\":null,\"type\":\"comment\",\"url\":null},{\"author\":\"axelfontaine\",\"children\":[{\"author\":\"cromka\",\"children\":[{\"author\":\"maipen\",\"children\":[{\"author\":\"Normal_gaussian\",\"children\":[],\"created_at\":\"2026-10-19T16:41:56.000Z\",\"created_at_i\":1792428116,\"id\":50154586,\"options\":[],\"parent_id\":50154010,\"points\":null,\"story_id\":50149392,\"text\":\"As someone who hasn&#x27;t looked at the repositories in question;<p>&gt; why don&#x27;t you submit some PRs to help them improve?<p>Is often a non-starter. If you believe a codebase is poor quality, any changes to improve it have a strong risk of making it worse and&#x2F;or wasting your own time. In the case that you aren&#x27;t a key player in said codebase already, it is even more likely your attempts to make changes will fail. Its not impossible to improve these repositories however doing so is quite a different skill-set to identifying their failings.\",\"title\":null,\"type\":\"comment\",\"url\":null},{\"author\":\"bigyabai\",\"children\":[],\"created_at\":\"2026-10-19T16:48:23.000Z\",\"created_at_i\":1792428503,\"id\":50154670,\"options\":[],\"parent_id\":50154010,\"points\":null,\"story_id\":50149392,\"text\":\"&gt; I coudln&#x27;t care less about what&#x27;s going on under the hood.<p>This is why there will likely be fragmented efforts for bringing up the hardware. Asahi already struggled merging all of their patches into the kernel, there&#x27;s a 99%+ chance that Omarchy&#x27;s patches will never be fully upstreamed if the reverse engineered devicetree is uninterpretable vibeslop.<p>So, even though Asahi&#x27;s the slow-and-steady side, it makes a lot more sense to channel your effort there.\",\"title\":null,\"type\":\"comment\",\"url\":null},{\"author\":\"cromka\",\"children\":[],\"created_at\":\"2026-10-19T17:03:06.000Z\",\"created_at_i\":1792429386,\"id\":50154868,\"options\":[],\"parent_id\":50154010,\"points\":null,\"story_id\":50149392,\"text\":\"&gt; If you took the time to really analyze their codebase and judge, why don&#x27;t you submit some PRs to help them improve?<p>You think it&#x27;s the same amount of time for both?\",\"title\":null,\"type\":\"comment\",\"url\":null}],\"created_at\":\"2026-10-19T15:59:11.000Z\",\"created_at_i\":1792425551,\"id\":50154010,\"options\":[],\"parent_id\":50153655,\"points\":null,\"story_id\":50149392,\"text\":\"If you took the time to really analyze their codebase and judge, why don&#x27;t you submit some PRs to help them improve?<p>It makes no sense to put them down on HN. \\nI love using Omarchy, and like many others, I coudln&#x27;t care less about what&#x27;s going on under the hood. It works great for me, is OSS and I can tweak it anytime I want.\\nCritical Bugs get caught quick too.\",\"title\":null,\"type\":\"comment\",\"url\":null}],\"created_at\":\"2026-10-19T15:30:50.000Z\",\"created_at_i\":1792423850,\"id\":50153655,\"options\":[],\"parent_id\":50151143,\"points\":null,\"story_id\":50149392,\"text\":\"I personally looked into their code an patches. This is an absolute mess of workarounds, permissioning negligence and other types of antipatterns.<p>They are truly and utterly a definition of a slop. And, expectedly, their work is unstable and not efficient, just look into Reddit discussion complaining about power draining.<p>I am afraid no one, or very few, has any deep understanding of low level architecture of these platforms or even Linux kernel and hardware in general, looking at their contributors\\u2019 previous front end NodeJS projects.<p>The only good thing is you can take their otherwise time-consuming work, have an agent infer and write a spec and use that to implement proper improvements.\",\"title\":null,\"type\":\"comment\",\"url\":null}],\"created_at\":\"2026-10-19T11:40:22.000Z\",\"created_at_i\":1792410022,\"id\":50151143,\"options\":[],\"parent_id\":50149959,\"points\":null,\"story_id\":50149392,\"text\":\"The Omarchy M team is doing great work with this. They are currently working on the M6 bring-up already!<p><a href=\\\"https:&#x2F;&#x2F;x.com&#x2F;JoshuaSWarren&#x2F;status&#x2F;2111923039378211100?s=20\\\" rel=\\\"nofollow\\\">https:&#x2F;&#x2F;x.com&#x2F;JoshuaSWarren&#x2F;status&#x2F;2111923039378211100?s=20</a>\",\"title\":null,\"type\":\"comment\",\"url\":null}],\"created_at\":\"2026-10-19T08:47:39.000Z\",\"created_at_i\":1792399659,\"id\":50149959,\"options\":[],\"parent_id\":50149392,\"points\":null,\"story_id\":50149392,\"text\":\"&gt;ARM<p>&gt; Device Tree Updates<p>&gt;  Apple platform gains support for M3 Pro, Max and Ultra SoC<p>Incredible how fast support for Apple silicon is progressing.\",\"title\":null,\"type\":\"comment\",\"url\":null},{\"author\":\"sgt\",\"children\":[{\"author\":\"NamlchakKhandro\",\"children\":[{\"author\":\"vconnor\",\"children\":[],\"created_at\":\"2026-10-19T10:21:05.000Z\",\"created_at_i\":1792405265,\"id\":50150590,\"options\":[],\"parent_id\":50150087,\"points\":null,\"story_id\":50149392,\"text\":\"Linux now is LLM friendly, Hacker News will be all over this.\",\"title\":null,\"type\":\"comment\",\"url\":null}],\"created_at\":\"2026-10-19T09:04:45.000Z\",\"created_at_i\":1792400685,\"id\":50150087,\"options\":[],\"parent_id\":50150074,\"points\":null,\"story_id\":50149392,\"text\":\"not an apple or anthropic announcement, so hackernews will be tumbleweeds on this.\",\"title\":null,\"type\":\"comment\",\"url\":null},{\"author\":\"JohnKemeny\",\"children\":[{\"author\":\"Bluestein\",\"children\":[],\"created_at\":\"2026-10-19T09:39:37.000Z\",\"created_at_i\":1792402777,\"id\":50150310,\"options\":[],\"parent_id\":50150203,\"points\":null,\"story_id\":50149392,\"text\":\"I&#x27;ll take an NSIS installer meself :)\",\"title\":null,\"type\":\"comment\",\"url\":null},{\"author\":\"e12e\",\"children\":[],\"created_at\":\"2026-10-19T10:03:19.000Z\",\"created_at_i\":1792404199,\"id\":50150462,\"options\":[],\"parent_id\":50150203,\"points\":null,\"story_id\":50149392,\"text\":\"There&#x27;s WUBI:<p><a href=\\\"https:&#x2F;&#x2F;github.com&#x2F;hakuna-m&#x2F;wubiuefi\\\" rel=\\\"nofollow\\\">https:&#x2F;&#x2F;github.com&#x2F;hakuna-m&#x2F;wubiuefi</a>\",\"title\":null,\"type\":\"comment\",\"url\":null},{\"author\":\"greenavocado\",\"children\":[],\"created_at\":\"2026-10-19T13:35:30.000Z\",\"created_at_i\":1792416930,\"id\":50152306,\"options\":[],\"parent_id\":50150203,\"points\":null,\"story_id\":50149392,\"text\":\"You missed this internet classic <a href=\\\"https:&#x2F;&#x2F;github.com&#x2F;twitter&#x2F;the-algorithm&#x2F;issues&#x2F;1999\\\" rel=\\\"nofollow\\\">https:&#x2F;&#x2F;github.com&#x2F;twitter&#x2F;the-algorithm&#x2F;issues&#x2F;1999</a>\",\"title\":null,\"type\":\"comment\",\"url\":null}],\"created_at\":\"2026-10-19T09:18:27.000Z\",\"created_at_i\":1792401507,\"id\":50150203,\"options\":[],\"parent_id\":50150074,\"points\":null,\"story_id\":50149392,\"text\":\"Can you point me to the `.msi` file?\",\"title\":null,\"type\":\"comment\",\"url\":null},{\"author\":\"not-kinsale-joe\",\"children\":[],\"created_at\":\"2026-10-19T10:57:48.000Z\",\"created_at_i\":1792407468,\"id\":50150838,\"options\":[],\"parent_id\":50150074,\"points\":null,\"story_id\":50149392,\"text\":\"Why would people expect that?\",\"title\":null,\"type\":\"comment\",\"url\":null}],\"created_at\":\"2026-10-19T09:02:49.000Z\",\"created_at_i\":1792400569,\"id\":50150074,\"options\":[],\"parent_id\":50149392,\"points\":null,\"story_id\":50149392,\"text\":\"I wonder how many people nowadays see something like this, clicking on it and expecting a &quot;Download and Install&quot; button.\",\"title\":null,\"type\":\"comment\",\"url\":null},{\"author\":\"INTPenis\",\"children\":[{\"author\":\"MisterMunchkin\",\"children\":[{\"author\":\"pbhjpbhj\",\"children\":[{\"author\":\"londons_explore\",\"children\":[],\"created_at\":\"2026-10-19T16:02:54.000Z\",\"created_at_i\":1792425774,\"id\":50154057,\"options\":[],\"parent_id\":50151206,\"points\":null,\"story_id\":50149392,\"text\":\"People said the same about requiring a compiler to compile a compiler.<p>It worked out.\",\"title\":null,\"type\":\"comment\",\"url\":null}],\"created_at\":\"2026-10-19T11:45:56.000Z\",\"created_at_i\":1792410356,\"id\":50151206,\"options\":[],\"parent_id\":50150234,\"points\":null,\"story_id\":50149392,\"text\":\"As long as &quot;the iPad generation&quot; can write and maintain ML tools? Because otherwise all software gets locked behind the tools which as they grow more complex will require more access to more resources to produce.<p>Doesn&#x27;t this just end up with the tech billionaires being the only ones who can make software. Will they decide to allow their models to maintain&#x2F;create FOSS? Seems unlikely.\",\"title\":null,\"type\":\"comment\",\"url\":null}],\"created_at\":\"2026-10-19T09:25:50.000Z\",\"created_at_i\":1792401950,\"id\":50150234,\"options\":[],\"parent_id\":50150088,\"points\":null,\"story_id\":50149392,\"text\":\"On the one hand it is concerning, on the other hand\\u2026 I was getting quite worried the iPad generation was going to be incapable of developing operating systems. If AI is now capable of maintaining it, that will save us from collapse.\",\"title\":null,\"type\":\"comment\",\"url\":null},{\"author\":\"leonidasrup\",\"children\":[{\"author\":\"machomaster\",\"children\":[{\"author\":\"pbhjpbhj\",\"children\":[{\"author\":\"wang_li\",\"children\":[],\"created_at\":\"2026-10-19T14:45:05.000Z\",\"created_at_i\":1792421105,\"id\":50153116,\"options\":[],\"parent_id\":50151142,\"points\":null,\"story_id\":50149392,\"text\":\"Except writing patches to an existing project creates a derivative work, and the original copyright still applies to the project, even though your individual changes are owned by you.<p>(This is why fan made videos using copyrighted characters can be taken down.)\",\"title\":null,\"type\":\"comment\",\"url\":null},{\"author\":\"xboxnolifes\",\"children\":[],\"created_at\":\"2026-10-19T16:00:01.000Z\",\"created_at_i\":1792425601,\"id\":50154022,\"options\":[],\"parent_id\":50151142,\"points\":null,\"story_id\":50149392,\"text\":\"Its a pointless tag. You can say a tag is required if you use AI, but the linux maintainers know some people are still just going to lie. And they know having this rule will lead to the occasional witch huntwhen someone tries to sus out code that seems AI written but does not have the tag.<p>They dont think the juice is worth the squeeze. LLMs are just another tool.\",\"title\":null,\"type\":\"comment\",\"url\":null}],\"created_at\":\"2026-10-19T11:40:10.000Z\",\"created_at_i\":1792410010,\"id\":50151142,\"options\":[],\"parent_id\":50150456,\"points\":null,\"story_id\":50149392,\"text\":\"Surprised by that. I expected more openness.<p>How long until we get &quot;all FOSS code is made by AI and so it&#x27;s not copyrighted&quot; arguments when using code in unlicensed ways?<p>It also hides what would be a great signal on which ML models to use to get the best quality code. Like, wouldn&#x27;t the community like to know which models made the code with the least bugs?\",\"title\":null,\"type\":\"comment\",\"url\":null},{\"author\":\"AlfeG\",\"children\":[],\"created_at\":\"2026-10-19T12:29:42.000Z\",\"created_at_i\":1792412982,\"id\":50151583,\"options\":[],\"parent_id\":50150456,\"points\":null,\"story_id\":50149392,\"text\":\"Nowadays it&#x27;s more correct to require commit be signed by AI - with Assisted-by: Human row...\",\"title\":null,\"type\":\"comment\",\"url\":null},{\"author\":\"corbet\",\"children\":[],\"created_at\":\"2026-10-19T14:49:08.000Z\",\"created_at_i\":1792421348,\"id\":50153175,\"options\":[],\"parent_id\":50150456,\"points\":null,\"story_id\":50149392,\"text\":\"The documentation update has been applied, you&#x27;ll see it in linux-next; it should move to the mainline in the next few days.\",\"title\":null,\"type\":\"comment\",\"url\":null}],\"created_at\":\"2026-10-19T10:02:37.000Z\",\"created_at_i\":1792404157,\"id\":50150456,\"options\":[],\"parent_id\":50150391,\"points\":null,\"story_id\":50149392,\"text\":\"The docs have not been properly updated to reflect the changes. Linux developers voted to stop requiring &quot;Assisted-by&quot; attribution.\",\"title\":null,\"type\":\"comment\",\"url\":null},{\"author\":\"n4r9\",\"children\":[{\"author\":\"jonathanlydall\",\"children\":[{\"author\":\"palata\",\"children\":[],\"created_at\":\"2026-10-19T10:41:20.000Z\",\"created_at_i\":1792406480,\"id\":50150730,\"options\":[],\"parent_id\":50150657,\"points\":null,\"story_id\":50149392,\"text\":\"I agree, I was writing something along those lines but with more words (which is not a good thing) :-).\",\"title\":null,\"type\":\"comment\",\"url\":null},{\"author\":\"sejje\",\"children\":[],\"created_at\":\"2026-10-19T14:00:35.000Z\",\"created_at_i\":1792418435,\"id\":50152616,\"options\":[],\"parent_id\":50150657,\"points\":null,\"story_id\":50149392,\"text\":\"&gt; should be applied to any non-vibe-coded software<p>Should be applied to anything someone is releasing without big disclosures.<p>At least until a few months from now when it will be clear that human code is the weak point.\",\"title\":null,\"type\":\"comment\",\"url\":null},{\"author\":\"bombcar\",\"children\":[],\"created_at\":\"2026-10-19T16:14:46.000Z\",\"created_at_i\":1792426486,\"id\":50154215,\"options\":[],\"parent_id\":50150657,\"points\":null,\"story_id\":50149392,\"text\":\"The problem with this policy is that it works well when dealing with individuals, but you can still be swamped by &quot;first-time sloppers&quot; - sure, each one cannot ever submit again because it&#x27;s low-quality slop, but the damage is done.<p>(And in reality EVERYONE&#x27;s AI policy is a variation of this no matter how strict they get, because very few people will suddenly rip all of tridge&#x27;s code out of Samba if he admits he used AI once, and nobody can withstand slopspam for long.)\",\"title\":null,\"type\":\"comment\",\"url\":null}],\"created_at\":\"2026-10-19T10:31:01.000Z\",\"created_at_i\":1792405861,\"id\":50150657,\"options\":[],\"parent_id\":50150511,\"points\":null,\"story_id\":50149392,\"text\":\"I&#x27;ve never been involved with Linux kernel work, but in terms of the &quot;You, as a person, not the AI, are responsible for the quality&quot; policy, this to me is a just an &quot;obvious&quot; policy that should be applied to any non-vibe-coded software.<p>It&#x27;s just another way of saying, you can&#x27;t have AI submit things on its own and you can&#x27;t &quot;blame&quot; AI for anything you submit, you&#x27;re expected to understand and be able to explain anything you&#x27;re putting forward, if there is an issue with your submission, doesn&#x27;t matter at all if you go back to AI to address it, so long as all issues are properly addressed.<p>The code is either of sufficient quality, or it&#x27;s not, doesn&#x27;t matter whether you or AI wrote it, it&#x27;s your name on it, you&#x27;re responsible for it and its your reputation on the line.\",\"title\":null,\"type\":\"comment\",\"url\":null},{\"author\":\"palata\",\"children\":[{\"author\":\"RunSet\",\"children\":[{\"author\":\"palata\",\"children\":[],\"created_at\":\"2026-10-19T15:14:52.000Z\",\"created_at_i\":1792422892,\"id\":50153485,\"options\":[],\"parent_id\":50152692,\"points\":null,\"story_id\":50149392,\"text\":\"I meant as a maintainer. As a maintainer, I receive patches from contributors, I decide if I merge them or not (and under which conditions), and then I take ownership of the merged patch (as in, if it breaks I am responsible for getting it fixed).<p>Which means that &quot;as a maintainer&quot;, I don&#x27;t write anything. I merge code that falls from the sky. Whether it was an AI cloud or a manual-coding cloud, I don&#x27;t know: from where I stand, it falls from the sky.<p>&gt; there is a significant difference between &quot;I wrote this.&quot; and &quot;I found this.&quot;<p>Of course. When I write code (&quot;I wrote this&quot;), then it does not fall from the sky: I wrote it. I know, it&#x27;s me :-). But in the open source world, being a maintainer means that you deal with code that falls from the sky. Unless you decide to close your project to contributions and only accept people you trust.\",\"title\":null,\"type\":\"comment\",\"url\":null}],\"created_at\":\"2026-10-19T14:05:43.000Z\",\"created_at_i\":1792418743,\"id\":50152692,\"options\":[],\"parent_id\":50150721,\"points\":null,\"story_id\":50149392,\"text\":\"&gt; In terms of maintenance, I don&#x27;t see a difference between getting a contribution from a stranger or from a stranger using an AI.<p>Perhaps not in terms of maintenance. In terms of provenance there is a significant difference between &quot;I wrote this.&quot; and &quot;I found this.&quot;\",\"title\":null,\"type\":\"comment\",\"url\":null},{\"author\":\"Verdex\",\"children\":[{\"author\":\"palata\",\"children\":[],\"created_at\":\"2026-10-19T15:11:06.000Z\",\"created_at_i\":1792422666,\"id\":50153440,\"options\":[],\"parent_id\":50153142,\"points\":null,\"story_id\":50149392,\"text\":\"I don&#x27;t disagree, and AI helps scaling many things: good work, bad work, malware.<p>However, I remember that a couple of university researchers (PhD candidates maybe?) thought it would be a good idea to introduce security issues in Linux, and managed to do it. Without AI and without a lot of resources.<p>A consequence of that was that Linux immediately reverted all the patches coming from that university, saying &quot;we don&#x27;t know if we can trust you guys&quot; (but really I guess as a way to tell universities &quot;if you fuck with us we ban you, now keep your people in line&quot;).<p>And that&#x27;s what I was thinking about when I said that if too many contributors misbehave, as a maintainer I will just stop accepting contributions from people I don&#x27;t know. And maybe that will happen. Maybe it will be harder and harder to just be accepted into the club of the people who can send a contribution and have it reviewed at all.\",\"title\":null,\"type\":\"comment\",\"url\":null}],\"created_at\":\"2026-10-19T14:46:52.000Z\",\"created_at_i\":1792421212,\"id\":50153142,\"options\":[],\"parent_id\":50150721,\"points\":null,\"story_id\":50149392,\"text\":\"This was my take on it as well.  Which in many ways makes a lot of sense and is pragmatic.<p>The thing which I think is going to be a very sore lesson for everyone at some point is that if anyone uses AI it becomes a burden for everyone.  Even in projects that reject all AI usage.<p>What I can do is create 50 personas that all contribute patches.  40 of them get called out and are distrusted, but 10 make it through.  I can give more focus on those 10 to ensure their trust level increases. Then slowly have them all come together to shepard some subsystem where I&#x27;m going to be the only real person involved and can sign off on my own work in a that looks like I have oversight from the outside.<p>In the past this was possible but took a very high cost.  You need a bunch of competent software engineers who you can trust not to blab their secret mission.  Today anyone can give it a try.\",\"title\":null,\"type\":\"comment\",\"url\":null}],\"created_at\":\"2026-10-19T10:39:47.000Z\",\"created_at_i\":1792406387,\"id\":50150721,\"options\":[],\"parent_id\":50150511,\"points\":null,\"story_id\":50149392,\"text\":\"I think what it means is &quot;we will judge <i>you</i> based on the code <i>you</i> submit, we don&#x27;t care if it comes from an AI&quot;. Also probably &quot;we will judge <i>you</i> if you obviously don&#x27;t understand the code <i>you</i> submit&quot;.<p>At least that&#x27;s how I do it. When someone offers a patch to an open source project, I don&#x27;t really care if that person has a PhD, or if that person does this in their free time, or if that patch is actually the result of a whole team working together. I judge the patch based on the value it brings to me as a maintainer.<p>Obviously the code has to be good, but also the interaction with whoever offers the patch has to be smooth: if I have a question, if I need them to confirm&#x2F;change&#x2F;test&#x2F;elaborate on something, I need that to work.<p>If I feel like I would be faster doing the work myself AND this author is not putting effort into it (e.g. they just copy-paste an AI), I will not review that work. Possibly I will distrust that author. If too many contributions end up wasting my time, I will start limiting the contributions to people I know&#x2F;trust, etc.<p>In terms of maintenance, I don&#x27;t see a difference between getting a contribution from a stranger or from a stranger using an AI.<p>In terms of philosophy, that&#x27;s obviously different: One may not want to accept contributions from people using AI because they think that AI is bad for society, for instance. And I would totally understand that. But if the stance (and it seems to be Linus&#x27; stance) is that a patch is only judged based on its technical merit, then I don&#x27;t see why AI-in-the-loop would be an issue.\",\"title\":null,\"type\":\"comment\",\"url\":null},{\"author\":\"gwd\",\"children\":[],\"created_at\":\"2026-10-19T10:54:10.000Z\",\"created_at_i\":1792407250,\"id\":50150810,\"options\":[],\"parent_id\":50150511,\"points\":null,\"story_id\":50149392,\"text\":\"Basically, you can&#x27;t just post slop and then say, &quot;Oh, sorry, the AI did that.&quot;  If the comments are too wordy, or the patch is unnecessary or poorly written or contains errors or doesn&#x27;t follow the style guide, you submitted it, so that&#x27;s on you.  If you get in the habit of posting bad patches, people may start ignoring you.\",\"title\":null,\"type\":\"comment\",\"url\":null},{\"author\":\"giancarlostoro\",\"children\":[],\"created_at\":\"2026-10-19T14:07:47.000Z\",\"created_at_i\":1792418867,\"id\":50152715,\"options\":[],\"parent_id\":50150511,\"points\":null,\"story_id\":50149392,\"text\":\"Don&#x27;t forget having Linus Torvalds ridicule you in front of the entire world. That alone is reason to be vigilant that your code submitted isn&#x27;t hot garbage.\",\"title\":null,\"type\":\"comment\",\"url\":null},{\"author\":\"bityard\",\"children\":[],\"created_at\":\"2026-10-19T16:13:15.000Z\",\"created_at_i\":1792426395,\"id\":50154200,\"options\":[],\"parent_id\":50150511,\"points\":null,\"story_id\":50149392,\"text\":\"Being responsible for the quality of the submission means that when other kernel developers say, &quot;hey this part sucks, please fix it,&quot; you are obligated to fix it or defend your position, if you want the patch to be accepted into the kernel.<p>That last part is worth highlighting: you always have the option to not follow-up and abandon your proposed change, in which case it won&#x27;t make it into the kernel. (Unless someone else picks it up and volunteers to get it into shape for that is acceptable for merging into the kernel, but that is vanishingly rare, unless the change is trivial.)<p>You aren&#x27;t expected to hang around and maintain your patch once merged, that&#x27;s what subsystem maintainers are for. But you would be making their lives a lot easier and earn a lot of respect if you did.\",\"title\":null,\"type\":\"comment\",\"url\":null}],\"created_at\":\"2026-10-19T10:10:08.000Z\",\"created_at_i\":1792404608,\"id\":50150511,\"options\":[],\"parent_id\":50150391,\"points\":null,\"story_id\":50149392,\"text\":\"&gt; You, as a person, not the AI, are responsible for the quality<p>As someone who doesn&#x27;t know much about linux kernel work: what does being responsible for the submission look like in practice? Are you expected to find fixes if your change causes unanticipated issues?\",\"title\":null,\"type\":\"comment\",\"url\":null},{\"author\":\"LtWorf\",\"children\":[{\"author\":\"eru\",\"children\":[{\"author\":\"palata\",\"children\":[{\"author\":\"LtWorf\",\"children\":[{\"author\":\"palata\",\"children\":[{\"author\":\"LtWorf\",\"children\":[],\"created_at\":\"2026-10-19T16:37:06.000Z\",\"created_at_i\":1792427826,\"id\":50154516,\"options\":[],\"parent_id\":50152534,\"points\":null,\"story_id\":50149392,\"text\":\"I think there&#x27;s very few if any unpaid contributors to the linux kernel these days.\",\"title\":null,\"type\":\"comment\",\"url\":null}],\"created_at\":\"2026-10-19T13:53:12.000Z\",\"created_at_i\":1792417992,\"id\":50152534,\"options\":[],\"parent_id\":50151742,\"points\":null,\"story_id\":50149392,\"text\":\"Well if my employer was willing to pay me to contribute to open source projects like Linux, <i>under the condition that I used AI</i>, I would gladly do it :-).\",\"title\":null,\"type\":\"comment\",\"url\":null}],\"created_at\":\"2026-10-19T12:44:57.000Z\",\"created_at_i\":1792413897,\"id\":50151742,\"options\":[],\"parent_id\":50150742,\"points\":null,\"story_id\":50149392,\"text\":\"&gt; Nothing says you have to use AI.<p>Except your employer, usually.\",\"title\":null,\"type\":\"comment\",\"url\":null}],\"created_at\":\"2026-10-19T10:44:34.000Z\",\"created_at_i\":1792406674,\"id\":50150742,\"options\":[],\"parent_id\":50150669,\"points\":null,\"story_id\":50149392,\"text\":\"Or they don&#x27;t care whether the code was produced with vim, VScode with an autocomplete tool or AI; they will judge the code based on the quality of the code and the quality of the interaction with whoever submits it?<p>Nothing says you <i>have</i> to use AI. Just like nothing says you <i>have</i> to use a computer. You could have someone print the code for you, write your patch with a pencil on paper, get a pigeon to send your patch to someone who can then submit it, and it would be judge in the same manner. Granted, it&#x27;s probably more efficient to use a computer.\",\"title\":null,\"type\":\"comment\",\"url\":null}],\"created_at\":\"2026-10-19T10:33:30.000Z\",\"created_at_i\":1792406010,\"id\":50150669,\"options\":[],\"parent_id\":50150617,\"points\":null,\"story_id\":50149392,\"text\":\"Basically, they expect everyone to always use AI all the time anyway.\",\"title\":null,\"type\":\"comment\",\"url\":null}],\"created_at\":\"2026-10-19T10:26:07.000Z\",\"created_at_i\":1792405567,\"id\":50150617,\"options\":[],\"parent_id\":50150391,\"points\":null,\"story_id\":50149392,\"text\":\"In their last meeting they&#x27;ve agreed to give up on the disclosing part.\",\"title\":null,\"type\":\"comment\",\"url\":null},{\"author\":\"shakna\",\"children\":[],\"created_at\":\"2026-10-19T11:23:18.000Z\",\"created_at_i\":1792408998,\"id\":50151025,\"options\":[],\"parent_id\":50150391,\"points\":null,\"story_id\":50149392,\"text\":\"Voted to be removed attribution in the last meeting [0], but the patch thread is a bit controversial and ongoing at the moment. [1]<p>[0] <a href=\\\"https:&#x2F;&#x2F;lwn.net&#x2F;Articles&#x2F;1099594&#x2F;\\\" rel=\\\"nofollow\\\">https:&#x2F;&#x2F;lwn.net&#x2F;Articles&#x2F;1099594&#x2F;</a> (user required).<p>[1] <a href=\\\"https:&#x2F;&#x2F;lore.kernel.org&#x2F;lkml&#x2F;20261009083137.3-1-sashal@kernel.org&#x2F;\\\" rel=\\\"nofollow\\\">https:&#x2F;&#x2F;lore.kernel.org&#x2F;lkml&#x2F;20261009083137.3-1-sashal@kerne...</a>\",\"title\":null,\"type\":\"comment\",\"url\":null},{\"author\":\"onel\",\"children\":[{\"author\":\"wang_li\",\"children\":[],\"created_at\":\"2026-10-19T14:49:39.000Z\",\"created_at_i\":1792421379,\"id\":50153182,\"options\":[],\"parent_id\":50151382,\"points\":null,\"story_id\":50149392,\"text\":\"100% of AI use should have to be disclosed in all cases, not just software. A person using an AI is foregoing responsibility from the get go by using an AI to do things that they can&#x27;t personally do. What would it even mean for them to be responsible if they submit a patch to a project written by an AI as their own work? Such a person submitting a patch they don&#x27;t understand that is rejected is in exactly the same place as if they had never submitted a patch. They have nothing to lose.\",\"title\":null,\"type\":\"comment\",\"url\":null},{\"author\":\"onel\",\"children\":[],\"created_at\":\"2026-10-19T15:34:02.000Z\",\"created_at_i\":1792424042,\"id\":50153701,\"options\":[],\"parent_id\":50151382,\"points\":null,\"story_id\":50149392,\"text\":\"I hear you, but I don&#x27;t think is a good idea to say that someone who used AI for a patch, did not understand the changes.\\nWe can&#x27;t conflate the two.<p>If you spent 20 seconds to generate a patch and just hurled over the fence to be reviewed is no worse than spending 3 hours to manually write a fix that fixes 1 thing and break 2 others.\",\"title\":null,\"type\":\"comment\",\"url\":null}],\"created_at\":\"2026-10-19T12:06:45.000Z\",\"created_at_i\":1792411605,\"id\":50151382,\"options\":[],\"parent_id\":50150391,\"points\":null,\"story_id\":50149392,\"text\":\"That sounds reasonable. \\nI would even go one step further and drop requirements to disclose AI use. \\nI think the person submitting the work should be fully responsible for what they submit and is more than enough. \\nIf you have a karma system for contributors, you get a pretty good idea of who does a good job.p\",\"title\":null,\"type\":\"comment\",\"url\":null}],\"created_at\":\"2026-10-19T09:52:24.000Z\",\"created_at_i\":1792403544,\"id\":50150391,\"options\":[],\"parent_id\":50150088,\"points\":null,\"story_id\":50149392,\"text\":\"You can see the curreny Linux AI policy here:<p><a href=\\\"https:&#x2F;&#x2F;docs.kernel.org&#x2F;process&#x2F;coding-assistants.html\\\" rel=\\\"nofollow\\\">https:&#x2F;&#x2F;docs.kernel.org&#x2F;process&#x2F;coding-assistants.html</a><p>Broadly it says: you can use AI as much as you like. You have to disclose use of AI. You, as a person, not the AI, are responsible for the quality of Linux kernel submissions.\",\"title\":null,\"type\":\"comment\",\"url\":null}],\"created_at\":\"2026-10-19T09:04:53.000Z\",\"created_at_i\":1792400693,\"id\":50150088,\"options\":[],\"parent_id\":50149392,\"points\":null,\"story_id\":50149392,\"text\":\"&gt; This release is a record-breaker on all fronts; it brought in 18,765 non-merge commits from 3,021 developers, 807 of whom were first-time kernel developers.<p>I&#x27;m reading that as many new AI assisted code patches. Not everyone does an obviously bad job at submitting AI assisted code.<p>Not a criticism, just an observation.\",\"title\":null,\"type\":\"comment\",\"url\":null},{\"author\":\"KolmogorovComp\",\"children\":[],\"created_at\":\"2026-10-19T09:38:48.000Z\",\"created_at_i\":1792402728,\"id\":50150301,\"options\":[],\"parent_id\":50149392,\"points\":null,\"story_id\":50149392,\"text\":\"It\\u2019d be interesting to see if rust pickup is improving or not.\",\"title\":null,\"type\":\"comment\",\"url\":null},{\"author\":\"Harukadia\",\"children\":[{\"author\":\"seb1204\",\"children\":[],\"created_at\":\"2026-10-19T11:50:14.000Z\",\"created_at_i\":1792410614,\"id\":50151237,\"options\":[],\"parent_id\":50150713,\"points\":null,\"story_id\":50149392,\"text\":\"A record breaker on all fronts according to post\",\"title\":null,\"type\":\"comment\",\"url\":null}],\"created_at\":\"2026-10-19T10:38:52.000Z\",\"created_at_i\":1792406332,\"id\":50150713,\"options\":[],\"parent_id\":50149392,\"points\":null,\"story_id\":50149392,\"text\":\"How big change is this?\",\"title\":null,\"type\":\"comment\",\"url\":null},{\"author\":\"skybrian\",\"children\":[{\"author\":\"abainbridge\",\"children\":[{\"author\":\"gpm\",\"children\":[],\"created_at\":\"2026-10-19T16:20:04.000Z\",\"created_at_i\":1792426804,\"id\":50154286,\"options\":[],\"parent_id\":50153633,\"points\":null,\"story_id\":50149392,\"text\":\"There&#x27;s a graph for bugs and bug-fixes (not CVEs, but in linux close) near the end of this article that looks approximately like what you are interested in: <a href=\\\"https:&#x2F;&#x2F;lwn.net&#x2F;Articles&#x2F;1088776&#x2F;\\\" rel=\\\"nofollow\\\">https:&#x2F;&#x2F;lwn.net&#x2F;Articles&#x2F;1088776&#x2F;</a>\",\"title\":null,\"type\":\"comment\",\"url\":null}],\"created_at\":\"2026-10-19T15:28:32.000Z\",\"created_at_i\":1792423712,\"id\":50153633,\"options\":[],\"parent_id\":50151340,\"points\":null,\"story_id\":50149392,\"text\":\"I want to see the same graph but for number of CVEs introduced. ie, when we find a CVE, find out which kernel version introduced it. This would only be useful for kernel versions that were a few years old, so we&#x27;d have had time to find most of the CVEs. But it&#x27;d still be interesting.\",\"title\":null,\"type\":\"comment\",\"url\":null}],\"created_at\":\"2026-10-19T12:01:51.000Z\",\"created_at_i\":1792411311,\"id\":50151340,\"options\":[],\"parent_id\":50149392,\"points\":null,\"story_id\":50149392,\"text\":\"Number of CVEs fixed at an all-time high:<p><a href=\\\"https:&#x2F;&#x2F;pages.skybrian.com&#x2F;2026&#x2F;kernel-cve-fixes&#x2F;\\\" rel=\\\"nofollow\\\">https:&#x2F;&#x2F;pages.skybrian.com&#x2F;2026&#x2F;kernel-cve-fixes&#x2F;</a>\",\"title\":null,\"type\":\"comment\",\"url\":null},{\"author\":\"wryun\",\"children\":[{\"author\":\"Avamander\",\"children\":[],\"created_at\":\"2026-10-19T12:59:38.000Z\",\"created_at_i\":1792414778,\"id\":50151910,\"options\":[],\"parent_id\":50151374,\"points\":null,\"story_id\":50149392,\"text\":\"Now we just need hostapd and wpa_supplicant support? (Minus the things missing from the driver.)\",\"title\":null,\"type\":\"comment\",\"url\":null},{\"author\":\"londons_explore\",\"children\":[{\"author\":\"bronson\",\"children\":[],\"created_at\":\"2026-10-19T16:56:17.000Z\",\"created_at_i\":1792428977,\"id\":50154779,\"options\":[],\"parent_id\":50154120,\"points\":null,\"story_id\":50149392,\"text\":\"The code is right there. You can go look and see if your guess is right.<p>You can tell that I&#x27;m skeptical. Kernel code tends to be pretty tight.\",\"title\":null,\"type\":\"comment\",\"url\":null}],\"created_at\":\"2026-10-19T16:06:57.000Z\",\"created_at_i\":1792426017,\"id\":50154120,\"options\":[],\"parent_id\":50151374,\"points\":null,\"story_id\":50149392,\"text\":\"Kinda sad that there are so many thousands of lines of code here, yet at its core this is much the same as any other WiFi device.<p>Surely some code reuse would have been possible?   Especially when the same team who write the driver are also making the firmware\",\"title\":null,\"type\":\"comment\",\"url\":null},{\"author\":\"drannex\",\"children\":[],\"created_at\":\"2026-10-19T16:31:50.000Z\",\"created_at_i\":1792427510,\"id\":50154443,\"options\":[],\"parent_id\":50151374,\"points\":null,\"story_id\":50149392,\"text\":\"Oh this is incredible.. HaLow has been one of my favorite new wireless tech implementations, and I feel almost like a preacher with how much I have been pushing the gospel to the people around me for the last ~2 years. being introduced into the kernel should really help integration and usage. I am just hoping chips start being produced by more than one (?) company eventually for more market capture. Any thoughts on that front?\",\"title\":null,\"type\":\"comment\",\"url\":null}],\"created_at\":\"2026-10-19T12:05:52.000Z\",\"created_at_i\":1792411552,\"id\":50151374,\"options\":[],\"parent_id\":50149392,\"points\":null,\"story_id\":50149392,\"text\":\"I&#x27;m excited because of <a href=\\\"https:&#x2F;&#x2F;git.kernel.org&#x2F;pub&#x2F;scm&#x2F;linux&#x2F;kernel&#x2F;git&#x2F;torvalds&#x2F;linux.git&#x2F;commit&#x2F;?id=b1906cea00b021acea22460225401d5b27bc7c36\\\" rel=\\\"nofollow\\\">https:&#x2F;&#x2F;git.kernel.org&#x2F;pub&#x2F;scm&#x2F;linux&#x2F;kernel&#x2F;git&#x2F;torvalds&#x2F;lin...</a>, which is the first kernel driver to use the new Wi-Fi HaLow (s1g&#x2F;900MHz Wi-Fi) support.<p>(Disclosure: I work for Morse)\",\"title\":null,\"type\":\"comment\",\"url\":null},{\"author\":\"tosti\",\"children\":[],\"created_at\":\"2026-10-19T13:08:57.000Z\",\"created_at_i\":1792415337,\"id\":50151995,\"options\":[],\"parent_id\":50149392,\"points\":null,\"story_id\":50149392,\"text\":\"Is anyone already running this seeing &#x2F;dev&#x2F;input&#x2F;events appear as usual? Last rc didn&#x27;t work for me, events was gone :( same config 7.2.10 was fine\",\"title\":null,\"type\":\"comment\",\"url\":null},{\"author\":\"VCFundedGenYer\",\"children\":[{\"author\":\"surajrmal\",\"children\":[],\"created_at\":\"2026-10-19T13:57:22.000Z\",\"created_at_i\":1792418242,\"id\":50152579,\"options\":[],\"parent_id\":50152368,\"points\":null,\"story_id\":50149392,\"text\":\"Which will just put pressure to improve the testing situation, not necessarily change stance on AI written patches. In fact AI is quite good at catching issues and root causing them back to recent changes so they could automate that journey a bit better as well to fill in any gaps not caught at review time.\",\"title\":null,\"type\":\"comment\",\"url\":null},{\"author\":\"rvz\",\"children\":[],\"created_at\":\"2026-10-19T14:35:12.000Z\",\"created_at_i\":1792420512,\"id\":50153019,\"options\":[],\"parent_id\":50152368,\"points\":null,\"story_id\":50149392,\"text\":\"Remember, you are &quot;not allowed&quot; to criticize Linus about how he uses LLMs on patches he himself doesn&#x27;t understand in the Linux Kernel, despite screaming at others in doing the exact same thing (with him and Claude introduces a bug in the process).<p>This is especially with C code which has hundreds of foot-guns and you can just look at how Linus does not care about code quality in something as critical like an OS kernel and then carelessly introduces a race condition.<p>If that was anyone else and Linus caught it, they would be completely smoked by his wrath in the mailing list.\",\"title\":null,\"type\":\"comment\",\"url\":null}],\"created_at\":\"2026-10-19T13:40:04.000Z\",\"created_at_i\":1792417204,\"id\":50152368,\"options\":[],\"parent_id\":50149392,\"points\":null,\"story_id\":50149392,\"text\":\"Beware - this was Torvalds&#x27; fever dream of suddenly discovering vibe coding and unleashing it on the kernel like a five year old with candy.<p>Expect catastrophic problems. There is likely a lot of slop in the code now + testers already found race conditions.<p>Torvalds&#x27; behavior in the past few weeks has been alarming.\",\"title\":null,\"type\":\"comment\",\"url\":null},{\"author\":\"lp92\",\"children\":[{\"author\":\"roryirvine\",\"children\":[],\"created_at\":\"2026-10-19T14:01:47.000Z\",\"created_at_i\":1792418507,\"id\":50152636,\"options\":[],\"parent_id\":50152430,\"points\":null,\"story_id\":50149392,\"text\":\"Helps prevent chroot escapes - see discussion at <a href=\\\"https:&#x2F;&#x2F;lwn.net&#x2F;Articles&#x2F;1089244&#x2F;\\\" rel=\\\"nofollow\\\">https:&#x2F;&#x2F;lwn.net&#x2F;Articles&#x2F;1089244&#x2F;</a>\",\"title\":null,\"type\":\"comment\",\"url\":null}],\"created_at\":\"2026-10-19T13:45:20.000Z\",\"created_at_i\":1792417520,\"id\":50152430,\"options\":[],\"parent_id\":50149392,\"points\":null,\"story_id\":50149392,\"text\":\"Can someone explain to me what failfs is relevent for?\",\"title\":null,\"type\":\"comment\",\"url\":null},{\"author\":\"alisharify7\",\"children\":[],\"created_at\":\"2026-10-19T14:02:13.000Z\",\"created_at_i\":1792418533,\"id\":50152642,\"options\":[],\"parent_id\":50149392,\"points\":null,\"story_id\":50149392,\"text\":\"nice\",\"title\":null,\"type\":\"comment\",\"url\":null},{\"author\":\"aether781536\",\"children\":[],\"created_at\":\"2026-10-19T14:46:05.000Z\",\"created_at_i\":1792421165,\"id\":50153129,\"options\":[],\"parent_id\":50149392,\"points\":null,\"story_id\":50149392,\"text\":\"Congrats to the kernel team! The pace of releases over the last year has been impressive. Looking forward to testing this on my machines this weekend.\",\"title\":null,\"type\":\"comment\",\"url\":null},{\"author\":\"pdmccormick\",\"children\":[{\"author\":\"yonatan8070\",\"children\":[],\"created_at\":\"2026-10-19T16:59:09.000Z\",\"created_at_i\":1792429149,\"id\":50154821,\"options\":[],\"parent_id\":50154004,\"points\":null,\"story_id\":50149392,\"text\":\"The kernel doesn&#x27;t follow conventional semver practices, meaning that the major number incrementing has no effect beyong the minor number got too large. So there&#x27;s nothing inherently different in a 7.X release from a 6.X release.<p>Either way, an LTS appears to be released ~every 6th version, 6.18 is the newest LTS, so after that there&#x27;s 6.19, 7.0, 7.1, 7.2, 7.3, 7.4 maybe LTS?<p>kernel.org has a nice FAQ about this topic.<p><a href=\\\"https:&#x2F;&#x2F;www.kernel.org&#x2F;category&#x2F;releases.html\\\" rel=\\\"nofollow\\\">https:&#x2F;&#x2F;www.kernel.org&#x2F;category&#x2F;releases.html</a>\",\"title\":null,\"type\":\"comment\",\"url\":null}],\"created_at\":\"2026-10-19T15:58:43.000Z\",\"created_at_i\":1792425523,\"id\":50154004,\"options\":[],\"parent_id\":50149392,\"points\":null,\"story_id\":50149392,\"text\":\"Does anyone know if there has been any guidance or discussion on when a 7.x release might become a longterm supported release?\",\"title\":null,\"type\":\"comment\",\"url\":null},{\"author\":\"devplus31\",\"children\":[],\"created_at\":\"2026-10-19T16:15:54.000Z\",\"created_at_i\":1792426554,\"id\":50154232,\"options\":[],\"parent_id\":50149392,\"points\":null,\"story_id\":50149392,\"text\":\"more rust?\",\"title\":null,\"type\":\"comment\",\"url\":null}],\"created_at\":\"2026-10-19T07:23:57.000Z\",\"created_at_i\":1792394637,\"id\":50149392,\"options\":[],\"parent_id\":null,\"points\":528,\"story_id\":50149392,\"text\":null,\"title\":\"Linux 7.3\",\"type\":\"story\",\"url\":\"https://lwn.net/Articles/1101143/\"}\n"
     },
